#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Preface]
<preface>
A student came to me some years ago with a hard disk of RNA-seq data and a deadline. She had been taught molecular biology thoroughly and had generated the samples herself. What she had not been taught was what to do with sixty gigabytes of compressed text. She had spent a fortnight trying to open a FASTQ file in a spreadsheet.

That gap --- between producing biological data and being able to do anything with it --- is the reason this book exists. It is a gap that has widened every year since high-throughput sequencing became routine. Sequencing a bacterial genome is now a service you can buy; interpreting it is not. The limiting reagent in a great deal of modern biology is no longer the experiment but the analysis.

The usual remedies have not worked well. Bioinformatics is often taught either as computer science with biological examples, which loses the biologists, or as a tour of web tools, which produces users who can click through an interface but cannot tell when its output is wrong. Neither approach produces someone who can take an unfamiliar dataset and make defensible sense of it.

This book takes a third approach, which is simply to do the work. Every concept is introduced because it is needed for a decision in an actual analysis. Every method is demonstrated on real data that comes with the book. Every piece of output you see was generated by the code printed above it, on the data described beside it. When a tool cannot be run inside the book, the command is shown as it was actually run, and its real output is quoted.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[What this book assumes]
<what-this-book-assumes>
It assumes you have seen the central dogma before and can be reminded rather than taught. It assumes no programming experience whatsoever. It assumes you have a computer with a terminal and the patience to type into it.

It does not assume you are a computer scientist, and it does not try to make you one. There is no complexity analysis here, and the algorithms are derived only to the depth that changes how you use them. There is, however, a sustained insistence on one point that is genuinely computational: that an analysis you cannot repeat is not a result. Scripts, environments, version control and recorded provenance appear from Chapter 1 and never go away.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[What this book is not]
<what-this-book-is-not>
It is not a reference manual. Tools change; the documentation for any one of them will be more current than this book by the time you read it. What is durable is the reasoning --- why you align before you count, why a p-value from a genome-wide test needs correcting, why an annotation inherited from a homologue is a hypothesis. That reasoning is what I have tried to write down.

Nor is it a clinical text. Chapter 13 covers variant interpretation and pharmacogenomics because they are now part of the field, but patient-facing decisions belong to accredited laboratories and qualified clinicians, and the chapter says so.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[A note on where this was written]
<a-note-on-where-this-was-written>
Much of my own work has been done in settings where compute is shared, bandwidth is intermittent and software licences are out of reach. That shapes the book. Every tool used here is free and open source. Every dataset is small enough to download over a poor connection. Nothing requires a cluster, though Chapter 5 explains how to use one when you have access. Genomics will only be as global as its methods are accessible, and I have tried to write accordingly.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Acknowledgement of the reader]
<acknowledgement-of-the-reader>
Bioinformatics rewards stubbornness more than talent. The first time an aligner fails with an error you have never seen, it will be tempting to conclude that you are not the sort of person who does this. You will be wrong. Everyone who works in this field spends a substantial fraction of their time confused, and the difference between those who continue and those who do not is mostly a willingness to read the error message carefully and try again.

#emph[Umar Ahmad] #emph[Bauchi, Nigeria]

#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Why read this book]
<why-read-this-book>
There are many bioinformatics books. Here is what is different about this one, so you can decide quickly whether it is the one you need.

#strong[Everything in it was run.] The figures were generated by the code printed beside them, on data that ships with the book. The alignment scores, the read counts, the p-values, the RMSD values, the tool output --- all of it came out of an actual execution. This sounds like a low bar. It is not: a great deal of teaching material contains code that has never been executed and output that was written by hand, and readers lose days discovering this the hard way.

#strong[It teaches judgement, not just procedure.] Knowing how to run DESeq2 is a few lines. Knowing that your experimental design confounds batch with condition, that your principal component one is library size, or that a significant GO term is a hypothesis rather than a result --- that is the part that takes years to acquire by osmosis, and this book tries to compress it. Several chapters deliberately demonstrate a wrong analysis before the right one, because the failure mode is the lesson.

#strong[It covers the whole working pipeline, not a slice.] The command line, Python, R and Bioconductor, databases, alignment, genome annotation, sequencing data, protein structure, networks, statistics, machine learning, clinical and pathogen genomics, and the ethics of human genomic data. These are presented as one connected skill rather than as separate specialisms, because in practice a single project touches most of them.

#strong[It is reproducible by construction.] The book is a Quarto project. You can clone it, rebuild it, change an analysis and see what happens. The data provenance is recorded with checksums. The software environment is pinned. The habits the book asks you to adopt are the habits it was itself written with.

#strong[It is free to read and free to run.] Every tool used is open source, every dataset is public and small, and the online edition is openly licensed.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[This book is for you if]
<this-book-is-for-you-if>
- you generate biological data and want to analyse it yourself rather than wait for a collaborator;
- you are a student who needs working skills for a project or thesis;
- you can program but do not know the biology, the file formats or the conventions;
- you teach bioinformatics and want material with exercises and solutions that can be run in a classroom;
- you are a clinician or public-health scientist meeting genomic data in your work.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[It is probably not for you if]
<it-is-probably-not-for-you-if>
- you want a reference manual for a specific tool --- read its documentation;
- you want algorithm theory at the level of a computer science course;
- you want a survey of the literature rather than a set of skills;
- you are looking for point-and-click instructions for web servers.

#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Structure of the book]
<structure-of-the-book>
The book has sixteen chapters in eight parts. The parts are ordered so that each one supplies something the next one needs, but most chapters can be read on their own if you already have the prerequisite.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Part I --- Foundations]
<part-i-foundations>
#strong[Chapter 1] defines the field as it is actually practised, works through the arithmetic that makes computation unavoidable, traces how the methods grew with the data, and sets up your computer. By the end of it you will have run your first analysis.

#strong[Chapter 2] covers the molecular biology that shows up in files: reading frames, reverse complements, splicing, coordinate systems, and the vocabulary of variation. Every concept is tied to something that will otherwise bite you later.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Part II --- Biological data and databases]
<part-ii-biological-data-and-databases>
#strong[Chapter 3] is about the archives: what distinguishes a primary archive from a curated knowledgebase, what accession numbers and versions actually mean, how to read a record, and how to retrieve data programmatically without abusing a public service.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Part III --- Sequence analysis]
<part-iii-sequence-analysis>
#strong[Chapter 4] derives pairwise alignment from first principles, implements it, then shows what scoring matrices do, how BLAST makes search tractable, what an E-value means, how multiple alignment works and how a phylogeny follows from one.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Part IV --- The bioinformatics toolchain]
<part-iv-the-bioinformatics-toolchain>
#strong[Chapter 5] teaches the command line as a working environment: streams and pipes, the text-processing tools, scripting, and job submission on a cluster.

#strong[Chapter 6] teaches Python through biological data, ending with a reusable module and a command-line tool of your own.

#strong[Chapter 7] teaches R and Bioconductor: the tidyverse, ggplot2 as a grammar, and the Bioconductor classes --- `GRanges`, `SummarizedExperiment` --- that the analysis chapters rely on.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Part V --- Genomes, reads and structures]
<part-v-genomes-reads-and-structures>
#strong[Chapter 8] goes from assembled sequence to annotated genome, including a real comparison between an #emph[ab initio] gene prediction and the reference annotation.

#strong[Chapter 9] is the longest chapter: sequencing data end to end, from FASTQ and quality encodings through alignment, SAM/BAM anatomy and variant calling, to a complete differential expression analysis.

#strong[Chapter 10] computes real quantities from protein coordinates, and takes predicted structures seriously enough to explain what their confidence measures do and do not mean.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Part VI --- Systems biology and data science]
<part-vi-systems-biology-and-data-science>
#strong[Chapter 11] treats biological networks quantitatively and implements enrichment analysis from first principles so that you know what the tools are doing.

#strong[Chapter 12] covers the statistics and machine learning that omics data demands, with particular attention to the ways high-dimensional data produces confident nonsense.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Part VII --- Applications and responsible practice]
<part-vii-applications-and-responsible-practice>
#strong[Chapter 13] applies all of it to cancer genomics, rare disease diagnostics, pharmacogenomics and pathogen surveillance.

#strong[Chapter 14] addresses privacy, consent, governance, equity and data sovereignty --- the obligations that attach to human genomic data.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Part VIII --- Consolidation]
<part-viii-consolidation>
#strong[Chapter 15] is a capstone project carried out in full, including the parts that usually go unrecorded: the dead ends, the environment capture, the write-up.

#strong[Chapter 16] synthesises the whole book, provides a graded question bank with worked answers, and sets out learning pathways for different starting points.

