#part[Foundations]
= What bioinformatics is, and how to set up to do it
<what-bioinformatics-is-and-how-to-set-up-to-do-it>
#block[
#strong[After working through this chapter you will be able to]

- distinguish bioinformatics from computational biology by what each discipline produces
- estimate the size of a sequencing dataset from coverage, read length and genome size, and explain why spreadsheet software cannot hold it
- place the main methodological milestones of the field on a timeline and say which data problem each one answered
- describe the five stages of a sequence analysis and name the decision made at each
- build and verify the software environment used by every example in this book
- compute the length, base composition and open reading frame content of a viral genome, and interpret the result

]
A clinical microbiologist in Bauchi receives sixty throat swabs during a suspected outbreak. By the end of the week she has sixty FASTQ files, together about ninety gigabytes, and a question her training did not prepare her for: are these sixty samples one outbreak or three unrelated introductions? No microscope answers that. No single piece of software answers it either. What answers it is a sequence of decisions --- which reads to discard, which reference to align to, which differences between genomes are real and which are sequencing noise, how many differences are too many to call two samples the same chain of transmission --- each of which has to be made deliberately, recorded, and defended when someone asks.

That sequence of decisions is bioinformatics. It is a working practice before it is a body of theory, which is why this book is organised around things you do rather than things you are told. By the end of this chapter you will have a working software environment on your own machine, the book's data on your disk, and a first completed analysis: the length, base composition and open reading frame content of the SARS-CoV-2 reference genome (`data/sequences/sars-cov-2-NC_045512.2.fasta`), with an interpretation of what those numbers mean. That analysis takes about ten minutes once the environment is installed, and everything after it in the book is built the same way.

Before the setup, three things are worth settling: what the discipline actually is, why the data forces you to compute rather than to look, and what an analysis looks like from end to end. Those three sections are short, and they exist because readers who skip them tend to produce results they cannot defend six months later.

== What bioinformatics is as a working discipline
<sec-01-what-it-is>
A dictionary will tell you that bioinformatics is the application of computational methods to biological data. That is true and nearly useless. A more serviceable description is this: #strong[bioinformatics is the practice of turning large, noisy, machine-generated measurements of biological molecules into statements a biologist can act on.] Three words in that sentence carry the weight. #emph[Large], because the volume removes the option of inspecting the data by eye. #emph[Noisy], because every instrument makes errors with its own characteristic signature, and distinguishing instrument from biology is most of the work. #emph[Act on], because the output is a claim --- this gene is differentially expressed, this variant is pathogenic, these two isolates are related --- and someone will make a decision on the strength of it.

The companion term, #strong[computational biology], is often used interchangeably, and in job advertisements it usually is. The distinction worth keeping is one of product. A bioinformatician's product is an answer about a specific dataset: these are the variants in this patient's exome, this is the phylogeny of these isolates. A computational biologist's product is a model or a method: a simulation of how a regulatory network behaves, an algorithm that aligns sequences faster, a statistical framework for estimating dispersion in count data. The same person frequently does both in the same week, and the same tools serve both. But the distinction changes what counts as success. A method is successful if it is correct and general; an analysis is successful if it is correct, reproducible and relevant to the sample in front of you. This book teaches the second, and borrows from the first only where a decision depends on knowing how a method works.

The questions the discipline answers fall into a small number of recurring shapes, and recognising the shape is most of knowing how to proceed.

- #strong[What is this sequence?] Identification by similarity: a contig from a metagenome, an unknown isolate, a gene of unclear function. Answered by alignment and database search \(Altschul et al. 1990).
- #strong[How do these sequences relate?] Evolutionary and epidemiological questions: phylogenies, transmission chains, gene families. Answered by multiple alignment and tree inference.
- #strong[What is different between these two groups?] Differential analysis: tumour versus normal, treated versus untreated, resistant versus susceptible. Answered by counting, modelling and multiple-testing correction.
- #strong[Where are the features in this genome?] Annotation: genes, regulatory elements, repeats, resistance determinants. Answered by pattern models trained on known examples.
- #strong[What does this variant do?] Interpretation: linking a change in sequence to a change in protein, to a phenotype, to a clinical recommendation.

Every chapter of this book sits under one of those five headings. When you meet an unfamiliar dataset, asking which of the five you are facing will usually tell you which chapter to reread.

