== How the field got here
<sec-01-history>
The history of bioinformatics is best read as a sequence of data problems and the methods invented to meet them. Told that way it stops being a list of dates and becomes an argument about why the tools look the way they do.

#strong[The 1960s and 1970s: sequences become comparable.] Margaret Dayhoff assembled the first systematic collection of protein sequences and, with it, the first substitution matrices \(Dayhoff et al. 1978) --- the insight that an alignment is a statistical statement about evolution, not a visual impression. The formal method arrived with the dynamic programming algorithm for global alignment of two sequences \(Needleman and Wunsch 1970), later adapted to find the best #emph[local] match rather than forcing an end-to-end one \(Smith and Waterman 1981). These algorithms are exact and quadratic in time, which made them usable when databases held hundreds of sequences.

#strong[The 1980s and 1990s: databases outgrow exact methods.] As collections grew, exact alignment against every entry became impossible, and heuristic search took over: first FASTA \(Pearson and Lipman 1988), then BLAST \(Altschul et al. 1990), which trades a guarantee of optimality for a speed-up of orders of magnitude and remains the most-used tool in biology. Scoring improved in parallel, with substitution matrices derived from alignments of conserved protein blocks. This is the period in which the field's characteristic bargain was struck: accept an approximation that is almost always right, in exchange for being able to search at all.

#strong[2001: the human genome, and the industrialisation of sequencing.] The publicly funded consortium \(Lander et al. 2001) and a competing private effort \(Venter et al. 2001) published draft human genome sequences within days of each other. The draft was not complete --- it was a mosaic with gaps in every centromere and most repeat arrays --- but it changed the unit of analysis from the gene to the genome, and it made reference-based analysis the default.

#strong[The 2000s and 2010s: short reads and the file-format era.] Massively parallel sequencing dropped the cost per base by several orders of magnitude and replaced long, accurate Sanger reads with hundreds of millions of short, error-prone ones. That shift produced the formats and tools that occupy most of this book: aligners that index a reference so that millions of reads can be placed against it, the SAM/BAM family for storing alignments and the VCF format for storing variants \(Danecek et al. 2021), and quality-control tools to summarise a run before anyone trusts it \(Ewels et al. 2016).

#strong[2022 and after: completion and pluralisation.] Long-read sequencing allowed the first truly complete human genome assembly, closing the gaps the draft had left, including the centromeric satellite arrays \(Nurk et al. 2022). Almost immediately the field moved past the idea of a single reference: a draft pangenome representing many individuals replaced one person's chromosome with a graph of alternatives \(Liao et al. 2023), because a single reference systematically mis-serves populations that are not represented in it. In parallel, protein structure prediction moved from a specialist pursuit to a routine step after a neural network began producing models of accuracy comparable to experiment \(Jumper et al. 2021), with the predictions themselves distributed as a public database.

Two lessons from that arc are worth carrying into your own work. The first is that methods are answers to data problems, so when the data changes the method usually must change too --- short-read tools applied to long reads give poor results, and a human-reference pipeline applied to a bacterium is nonsense. The second is that the field repeatedly discovers that its reference points are provisional. A reference genome, an annotation, a variant classification: all are current best estimates with version numbers, and treating them as fixed truth is one of the commonest sources of irreproducible results.

