<fig-01-pipeline>


Make it concrete with a named example: SARS-CoV-2 genomic surveillance, the analysis that the microbiologist in the opening paragraph needs, and the one that was performed hundreds of thousands of times between 2020 and 2023 \(Hu et al. 2020).

#strong[Raw data.] An amplicon protocol tiles the 30 kb genome with overlapping PCR products, which are then sequenced \(Quick et al. 2017). The output is one pair of FASTQ files per sample. #emph[Decision:] which protocol and primer scheme, because the primer positions must be removed later and the wrong scheme silently leaves primer sequence in your consensus.

#strong[Quality control.] Read quality is summarised per sample and across the run \(Ewels et al. 2016), adapters and low-quality tails are trimmed. #emph[Decision:] the quality threshold, and what to do with samples whose coverage is too low --- including them produces consensus sequences full of ambiguity codes that will distort a phylogeny.

#strong[Processing.] Reads are aligned to the reference genome, primer sequence is removed, and a consensus genome is called at positions with sufficient depth. #emph[Decision:] the minimum depth to call a base. Too low and sequencing errors become variants; too high and real genomes become mostly N.

#strong[Statistics and inference.] Consensus genomes are aligned, a phylogeny is estimated, and each genome is assigned to a named lineage \(Rambaut et al. 2020). #emph[Decision:] how many differences separate two samples before you stop calling them the same transmission chain --- a question with an epidemiological answer, not a computational one.

#strong[Interpretation.] The tree, the lineage assignments and the sample metadata are read together, often in a visualisation designed for the purpose \(Hadfield et al. 2017), and reported to the people who will act. Regional surveillance networks in Africa were built on exactly this chain of steps \(Tegally et al. 2022). #emph[Decision:] what to report, how to state the uncertainty, and what not to claim.

Notice that every stage has a decision and that none of the decisions is obviously right. This is why the output of a bioinformatics analysis is not a number but a number plus the record of how it was obtained.

== Why this book insists on scripts, environments and version control
<sec-01-reproducibility>
Suppose the microbiologist reports that two of the sixty samples differ by a single nucleotide and therefore represent one transmission event. Six months later a reviewer asks whether that conclusion holds if the minimum depth had been 20 rather than 10. If the analysis was done by clicking through a graphical tool, the honest answer is "I do not know, and finding out means starting again". If it was done with a script, the answer is a one-line change and a re-run.

That is the entire argument for reproducibility, and it is a practical argument rather than a moral one. The standard statements of the practice \(Sandve et al. 2013; Wilson et al. 2017) all reduce to the same three habits, which this book applies from the first chapter.

#strong[Everything in a script.] A command typed into a terminal and not saved has been lost. Keep an executable file for every step, even when the step is one line. The documents that make up this book are themselves scripts: the prose and the code live in the same file and the figures are regenerated every time it is built, an approach that goes back to the idea of literate programming \(Knuth 1984) and that modern tools make routine.

#strong[Everything in a pinned environment.] Results depend on software versions. An aligner changes a default; a statistical package changes how it estimates dispersion; your numbers move and you cannot say why. The answer is to declare the software your analysis needs in a file, install it from that file, and keep the file with the project. The packaging system this book uses distributes bioinformatics software through a community-maintained channel \(Grüning et al. 2018), and the next section uses it to build an environment named `pablb` containing the exact tools used in every chapter. For multi-step pipelines, workflow managers extend the same principle to the whole analysis \(Köster and Rahmann 2012)\; the project chapter of this book builds one.

#strong[Everything under version control.] Git records what changed, when and why. Its real value is not backup but the ability to answer "what did the analysis look like when I produced the figure in the paper?". Initialising a repository on the first day of a project costs about thirty seconds; recovering an un-versioned analysis costs weeks.

