The robust alternative is to let the parser do it. Biopython converts GenBank's 1-based inclusive locations to 0-based half-open `SeqFeature` locations when it reads the file, and `extract` applies them correctly, including reverse complementation for minus-strand features. That is why the #emph[TP53] translation earlier in this chapter needed no arithmetic at all, and why the recommendation throughout this book is to parse annotation rather than to slice by hand.

== Summary
<sec-02-summary>
- A FASTA record stores one strand with no record of which; strand must be carried explicitly in your code, and the reverse complement --- not the complement --- is what every file format expects.
- Translation never fails, so a plausible-looking protein is not evidence of a correct reading frame. Check for an initial methionine, a single terminal stop, and no internal stops.
- An ORF scan proposes candidates. Compared with the SARS-CoV-2 annotation, a 100-codon threshold recovers the long genes, misses four real genes that are shorter, and produces a chance hit on the empty strand.
- GenBank #emph[join] locations record real biology, such as the ribosomal frameshift that makes two polyproteins from one stretch of the SARS-CoV-2 genome; a parser that ignores them will translate the wrong product.
- A human gene's genomic span, its mRNA and its coding sequence are three different lengths: roughly 19 kb, 2,512 nt and 1,182 nt for #emph[TP53]. Coordinates in one are not coordinates in another.
- Translating the #emph[TP53] CDS reproduces UniProt P04637 exactly, which is both a demonstration of the central dogma and the cheapest available test of a nucleotide-to-protein pipeline.
- Coding density separates analysis strategies: about 95% coding for compact microbial genomes against 6% for a human locus, which is why eukaryotic annotation and alignment need intron-aware methods.
- Variant vocabulary divides into what changed in the DNA (SNV, indel, CNV) and what it does to the protein (synonymous, missense, nonsense, frameshift); a one-base deletion in #emph[TP53] reduces a 393-residue protein to a short fragment.
- 1-based inclusive and 0-based half-open coordinates differ by one at the start and never announce themselves. Verify any hand-made conversion against a sequence you already know.

