== Three kinds of resource
<sec-db-kinds>
A #strong[primary archive] stores submissions. GenBank, the European Nucleotide Archive (ENA), the DNA Data Bank of Japan (DDBJ), the Sequence Read Archive (SRA) and the Protein Data Bank (PDB) all work this way: a submitter uploads data and metadata, the archive performs format and consistency checks, assigns an accession, and from that moment the record is permanent. Archives do not correct biology. If a submitter annotated a pseudogene as a protein-coding gene, or recorded the wrong organism, the error stays in the archive until the submitter themselves revises it. The archive's promise is narrow and valuable: #emph[this is exactly what was deposited, and it will still be here in twenty years].

A #strong[curated knowledgebase] stores conclusions. UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot, RefSeq, ClinVar's reviewed assertions and Reactome contain records that a human curator has assembled by reading primary literature, reconciling conflicting reports and attaching evidence to each statement. A curated record is an argument, not an observation. It can be wrong, but it is wrong in a way that someone is accountable for, and it usually tells you what the claim rests on.

A #strong[derived resource] computes. STRING scores protein associations from co-expression, co-occurrence, text mining and imported interactions \(Szklarczyk et al. 2022)\; the AlphaFold Protein Structure Database holds predicted structures rather than measured ones \(Varadi et al. 2021)\; gnomAD aggregates allele frequencies across tens of thousands of exomes and genomes \(Karczewski et al. 2020). Derived resources are often the most immediately useful thing on your screen and the most dangerous to quote without qualification, because the number you are reading is a model output whose assumptions live in a methods paper you have not read.

The practical consequence is a habit. Before you use a record, ask which of the three you are holding, and let the answer set the sentence you are allowed to write. From an archive you may write "a sequence deposited as #emph[X] has property #emph[Y]". From a knowledgebase you may write "#emph[X] is annotated as #emph[Y], on the evidence of #emph[Z]". From a derived resource you may write "#emph[X] is predicted to be #emph[Y] by method #emph[M]". Those three sentences are not interchangeable, and reviewers notice.

