== Structures, genomes and the browser model
<sec-db-pdb-ensembl>
The Protein Data Bank is the archive of experimentally determined three-dimensional structures, now jointly managed as the worldwide PDB \(Berman 2000; Burley et al. 2022). A PDB entry is a four-character code such as `1TUP` and contains atomic coordinates together with the experimental conditions that produced them. Two attributes of a PDB entry govern how much you should trust any particular atom: the method (X-ray crystallography, nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy or cryo-electron microscopy) and, for crystal structures, the resolution. A 1.5 Å structure resolves individual side-chain positions; a 3.5 Å structure resolves the backbone and little else, and side-chain orientations in it are largely modelled rather than observed. The structural bioinformatics chapter takes this up in detail using the bundled `1tup.pdb` and `1mbn.pdb` files.

Ensembl takes a different approach from an archive \(Cunningham et al. 2021). It is built around a genome assembly as a coordinate system, with every other kind of data --- gene models, transcripts, regulatory features, variants, comparative alignments --- attached to intervals on that assembly. This is the #strong[genome browser model], and it has one consequence that beginners trip over constantly: #emph[every coordinate is meaningful only with respect to an assembly]. Position 7,676,154 on chromosome 17 is a different base in GRCh37 and in GRCh38. Ensembl labels each release with its assembly, and so must you.

A variant list, a BED file or a set of peak calls is therefore meaningless without the assembly it was called against, and converting between assemblies with a liftover tool is lossy: some intervals fail to map and a few map to the wrong place. Record the assembly name in the file, in the filename and in the methods section, and if you receive coordinates with no assembly stated, ask before you use them.

The browser model also explains the Ensembl identifier scheme. `ENSG` prefixes a gene, `ENST` a transcript, `ENSP` a protein and `ENSE` an exon, each with its own version suffix because a gene model can be revised without the gene being redefined. Species other than human carry a species code in the middle: `ENSMUSG` for mouse.

== Specialised and derived resources
<sec-db-specialised>
Beyond the general archives sit resources organised around one kind of question. You will not use all of them, but you need to know which one to reach for.

For human variation, #strong[dbSNP] accessions every submitted variant with an `rs` identifier \(Sherry 2001), and is a registry rather than an assessment: an `rs` number says a variant has been observed, not that it matters. #strong[ClinVar] records clinical interpretations of variants, each with a submitter, a review status and a date \(Landrum et al. 2017)\; its "review status" stars are the field you must read, because a one-star assertion from a single laboratory and a four-star expert-panel assertion are very different objects. The bundled `data/variants/tp53_clinvar.vcf` holds 3,888 ClinVar records across #emph[TP53] and is used in the clinical genomics chapter. Interpretation of a clinical variant follows the ACMG/AMP framework rather than a database lookup \(Richards et al. 2015). #strong[OMIM] is the long-running catalogue of Mendelian phenotypes and the genes behind them \(Amberger et al. 2018), and remains the best narrative account of what is known about a disease gene.

For functional genomics data, #strong[GEO] archives processed expression datasets with their experimental design \(Barrett et al. 2012), while #strong[SRA] and #strong[ENA] archive the raw reads underneath. The division matters when you want to reanalyse something: GEO gives you the authors' processed matrix, and the archives give you the reads you need in order to disagree with it.

For pathways and interactions, #strong[Reactome] is a curated, peer-reviewed pathway knowledgebase \(Milacic et al. 2023)\; #strong[KEGG] organises molecular interaction networks into maps with their own identifier space \(Kanehisa 2000)\; #strong[STRING] scores functional associations and is explicitly a derived resource \(Szklarczyk et al. 2022)\; and the #strong[Gene Ontology] supplies the controlled vocabulary that most functional analysis depends on \(Ashburner et al. 2000). The bundled `data/networks/p53_string_network.tsv` and `data/annotation/human_go_annotations.tsv` are drawn from the last two and are used in the systems biology chapter.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
Aggregate resources such as gnomAD publish allele frequencies precisely because individual-level human genomes cannot be published freely. Controlled-access repositories exist for the individual-level data, and access to them is granted for a stated purpose under a data access agreement that binds you personally. Downloading human genomic data because a URL happens to work is not consent, and the question of what you may do with it is legal as well as ethical. The chapter on ethical, legal and social issues sets out the framework; the obligation applies from the first file you download.

]
