== Heuristic database search with BLAST
<sec-aln-blast>
Smith--Waterman is optimal and far too slow for a database search: comparing one 300-residue query against a database of $10^8$ residues means filling $3 times 10^10$ cells. The Basic Local Alignment Search Tool trades that guarantee for speed \(Altschul et al. 1990). Its reasoning is that any alignment worth reporting almost certainly contains a short exact or near-exact match, so instead of examining every cell it looks only at the neighbourhoods of such #strong[seeds].

The procedure has three stages. #emph[Seeding]: the query is broken into overlapping words --- three residues for protein, eleven bases for nucleotide by default --- and for proteins a neighbourhood of similar words scoring above a threshold is generated for each. #emph[Extension]: each database position matching a seed word is extended outwards, and extension stops when the running score falls too far below the best value seen, which is what makes the search sublinear in practice. #emph[Evaluation]: surviving high-scoring segment pairs are assembled, gapped alignments are computed around them, and the results are scored statistically \(Altschul 1997). The original FASTA algorithm uses the same seed-and-extend idea with a different seeding rule and remains a useful cross-check \(Pearson and Lipman 1988).

The flavours differ only in what is translated before the comparison: `blastn` compares nucleotide against nucleotide, `blastp` protein against protein, `blastx` a translated nucleotide query against proteins, `tblastn` a protein query against a translated nucleotide database, and `tblastx` both translated. The decision follows from what you have and what you are looking for; when in doubt, comparing at the protein level detects homology at considerably greater evolutionary distance than comparing at the nucleotide level, because synonymous changes are invisible to it.

Building a searchable database from the bundled sequences takes one command.

#block[
```
from pathlib import Path

