Searching it with sperm whale myoglobin --- the protein whose structure was the first ever solved \(Kendrew et al. 1958) --- exercises every part of the output at once.

#block[
```
COLUMNS = ["qseqid", "sseqid", "pident", "length", "mismatch",
           "gapopen", "evalue", "bitscore", "score"]

query_fasta = Path(work_dir) / "query.fasta"
SeqIO.write([record for record in records if "MYG_PHYMC" in record.id],
            query_fasta, "fasta")
#> 1

completed = subprocess.run(
    ["blastp", "-query", str(query_fasta), "-db", str(database),
     "-outfmt", "6 " + " ".join(COLUMNS), "-evalue", "10"],
    check=True, capture_output=True, text=True)

hits = pd.read_csv(io.StringIO(completed.stdout), sep="\t", names=COLUMNS)
hits["sseqid"] = hits["sseqid"].str.split("|").str[2]
print(hits[["sseqid", "pident", "length", "evalue", "bitscore"]].to_string(index=False))
#>    sseqid  pident  length        evalue  bitscore
#> MYG_PHYMC 100.000     154 1.330000e-113     309.0
#> MYG_HUMAN  84.416     154  1.860000e-99     273.0
#> HBA_HUMAN  25.676     148  9.180000e-10      44.3
#> P53_FELCA  32.000      25  4.700000e-01      19.6
#> P53_HUMAN  32.000      25  1.000000e+00      18.5
#> P53_HUMAN  40.000      25  3.600000e+00      16.9
#> P53_BOVIN  33.333      27  1.800000e+00      17.7
#>   P53_RAT  28.000      25  2.200000e+00      17.3
#> P53_MOUSE  46.154      13  3.400000e+00      16.9
#> P53_DANRE  62.500       8  9.700000e+00      15.4
```

]
Read that table from the top. The first hit is the query against itself: 100% identity over all 154 residues, with an E-value of $1.33 times 10^(- 113)$. That self-hit is the sanity check --- if it is missing or imperfect, the database or the query is wrong, and nothing below it can be trusted. Human myoglobin follows at 84.4%, an orthologue. Human haemoglobin alpha is next at 25.7% identity over 148 residues with an E-value of $9.18 times 10^(- 10)$: a paralogue, in the twilight zone by identity alone, but with an E-value that leaves no doubt. Then the table falls off a cliff. The p53 entries match over 8 to 27 residues with E-values between 0.47 and 9.7, and those are noise --- exactly the short high-identity accidents the shuffling experiment produced earlier. Human haemoglobin beta does not appear at all at this threshold, which is an honest reminder that absence from a BLAST report is not absence of homology.

=== The statistics, and why the database size is in them
<sec-aln-blast-stats>
Three numbers describe each hit. The #strong[raw score] is the sum of substitution and gap scores for the alignment, and it is uninterpretable on its own because it depends on the matrix and gap costs used. The #strong[bit score] normalises the raw score by those parameters, $S' = \( lambda S - ln K \) \/ ln 2$, so that bit scores computed with different scoring schemes are comparable and a bit score of 40 means the same thing in any search. The #strong[E-value] is the number of hits with at least this score that you would expect to see by chance in a search of this size:

$ E = frac(m n, 2^(S')) \, $

where $m$ is the query length and $n$ the total number of residues in the database. The bit score is a property of the alignment; the E-value is a property of the alignment #emph[and the database you searched]. Doubling the database doubles the E-value of every hit without changing a single alignment. BLAST+ lets you set the effective database size directly, which turns that statement into a demonstration.

#block[
```
def search(dbsize=None):
    command = ["blastp", "-query", str(query_fasta), "-db", str(database),
               "-outfmt", "6 " + " ".join(COLUMNS), "-evalue", "10"]
    if dbsize is not None:
        command += ["-dbsize", str(dbsize)]
    output = subprocess.run(command, check=True, capture_output=True, text=True).stdout
    table = pd.read_csv(io.StringIO(output), sep="\t", names=COLUMNS)
    return table.loc[table["sseqid"].str.contains("HBA_HUMAN")].iloc[0]

actual_residues = sum(len(record.seq) for record in records)
for dbsize in (None, 1_000_000, 100_000_000):
    hit = search(dbsize)
    label = f"{actual_residues:,} (actual)" if dbsize is None else f"{dbsize:,}"
    print(f"database size {label:>22}  bit score {hit['bitscore']:5.1f}  "
          f"E-value {hit['evalue']:.2e}")
#> database size         3,661 (actual)  bit score  44.3  E-value 9.18e-10
#> database size              1,000,000  bit score  44.3  E-value 2.51e-07
#> database size            100,000,000  bit score  44.3  E-value 2.51e-05
```

]
The bit score is 44.3 in all three searches, because the alignment never changes. The E-value moves by more than four orders of magnitude, in exact proportion to the database size. This is why an E-value quoted without the database it came from is meaningless, why a hit that is convincing against a small curated database may be unremarkable against the whole of the non-redundant protein set, and why the common habit of reporting "E \< 1e-5" as though it were a universal threshold is unsound. The threshold you want depends on how many comparisons you made.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
Record the database and its version or date alongside every result, because the E-values are not reproducible without it. Check the self-hit before reading anything else. And look at the alignment, not just the table: a high-scoring hit confined to a low-complexity run of glutamines or a coiled-coil region is a compositional artefact, not evidence of shared ancestry, which is why BLAST filters such regions by default and why turning that filter off requires a reason.

]
, 
