== Multiple sequence alignment
<sec-aln-msa>
A pairwise alignment compares two sequences; a #strong[multiple sequence alignment (MSA)] arranges many sequences into a single set of columns, so that each column is a hypothesis that the residues in it descend from one ancestral position. That is a much stronger claim than any pairwise comparison makes, and everything downstream --- conservation analysis, profile construction, tree building, structure-guided mutagenesis --- depends on it being roughly right.

Extending dynamic programming to $N$ sequences is possible and useless: the table has $N$ dimensions and the cost grows exponentially. #strong[Progressive alignment], introduced in the form that ClustalW made standard \(Thompson et al. 1994), sidesteps this. It computes all pairwise distances, builds a quick guide tree from them, and then aligns sequences in the order that tree suggests, most similar first, merging alignments into larger alignments as it climbs. Each merge is a pairwise problem, so each is tractable.

The weakness of the approach is stated in its own name. Gaps placed early are never revisited: #emph[once a gap, always a gap]. An error made while aligning two close sequences propagates into every later merge, and the order in which sequences are supplied can change the result. Modern tools mitigate this with iterative refinement, which realigns subsets of the alignment and keeps changes that improve an objective function. MAFFT does this, and additionally uses a fast Fourier transform to find homologous segments quickly, which is what makes it practical on thousands of sequences \(Katoh and Standley 2013). MUSCLE \(Edgar 2004) and Clustal Omega \(Sievers et al. 2011) are the other two tools you will meet most often; Clustal Omega scales to the largest datasets, and MUSCLE's refinement is thorough on moderate ones.

The bundled p53 orthologues span 400 million years of vertebrate evolution, from human to medaka, which is enough divergence to make the alignment non-trivial.

#block[
```
from Bio import AlignIO

aligned_fasta = Path(work_dir) / "p53_mafft.fasta"
completed = subprocess.run(
    ["mafft", "--auto", "--quiet", book_data("proteins", "p53-orthologs.fasta")],
    check=True, capture_output=True, text=True)
aligned_fasta.write_text(completed.stdout)
#> 4209

alignment = AlignIO.read(aligned_fasta, "fasta")
for record in alignment:
    record.id = record.id.split("|")[2].replace("P53_", "")
print(f"{len(alignment)} sequences aligned into "
      f"{alignment.get_alignment_length()} columns")
#> 8 sequences aligned into 427 columns
print("unaligned lengths:",
      sorted(len(record.seq.replace("-", "")) for record in alignment))
#> unaligned lengths: [352, 373, 386, 386, 390, 391, 393, 393]
```

]
Eight proteins of 352 to 393 residues produce an alignment 427 columns long, so MAFFT has added 34 columns beyond the longest sequence to accommodate insertions that are present in some lineages and not others. Counting how the columns behave is the first thing to do with any alignment.

#block[
```
from collections import Counter

def column_consensus(alignment, index):
    """Fraction of sequences carrying the most common residue in one column."""
    column = [record.seq[index] for record in alignment]
    residues = [residue for residue in column if residue != "-"]
    if not residues:
        return 0.0
    return Counter(residues).most_common(1)[0][1] / len(alignment)


n_columns = alignment.get_alignment_length()
conservation = [column_consensus(alignment, index) for index in range(n_columns)]
gap_free = sum(1 for index in range(n_columns)
               if all(record.seq[index] != "-" for record in alignment))
invariant = sum(1 for index in range(n_columns)
                if len({record.seq[index] for record in alignment}) == 1
                and alignment[0].seq[index] != "-")

print(f"columns:            {n_columns}")
#> columns:            427
print(f"gap-free columns:   {gap_free}")
#> gap-free columns:   311
print(f"invariant columns:  {invariant} "
      f"({100 * invariant / n_columns:.0f}% of all columns)")
#> invariant columns:  128 (30% of all columns)
```

]
A hundred and twenty-eight columns are identical in all eight species, including zebrafish and medaka. Selection has held those positions fixed for hundreds of millions of years, which is strong evidence that they do something the protein cannot do without. Where those columns sit is the interesting question, and #ref(<fig-aln-conservation>, supplement: [Figure]) answers it by projecting conservation onto human p53 coordinates.

```
human = next(record for record in alignment if record.id == "HUMAN")

# Map alignment columns onto human residue numbers, skipping gaps in human.
human_positions, human_conservation = [], []
residue_number = 0
for index, residue in enumerate(human.seq):
    if residue != "-":
        residue_number += 1
        human_positions.append(residue_number)
        human_conservation.append(conservation[index])

window = 11
smoothed = pd.Series(human_conservation).rolling(window, center=True).mean()

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.6, 3.2))
ax.axvspan(102, 292, color=PALETTE[2], alpha=0.14)
#> <matplotlib.patches.Rectangle object at 0x178766ed0>
ax.plot(human_positions, human_conservation, color="#B0B8BF", linewidth=0.7)
#> [<matplotlib.lines.Line2D object at 0x149e7dd90>]
ax.plot(human_positions, smoothed, color=PALETTE[0], linewidth=1.8)
#> [<matplotlib.lines.Line2D object at 0x1784a12d0>]
