<fig-aln-tree>


The tree is partly convincing and partly not, and saying which is which is the whole point of putting numbers on the branches. The two fish group together in every replicate; so do mouse and rat; so do human and tree shrew, which is consistent with tree shrews being close relatives of primates. The branching order among cattle, cat and the primate pair is supported in 18% and 34% of replicates, which is to say the alignment does not resolve it. Reporting that tree without the numbers would present a resolution the data does not contain.

Three limitations of this approach deserve stating plainly. First, reducing an alignment to a distance matrix throws away which columns disagree, and the lost information is exactly what distinguishes competing topologies; character-based methods --- maximum likelihood as implemented in RAxML \(Stamatakis 2014) or IQ-TREE \(Minh et al. 2019), or Bayesian inference --- use it and are the standard for published phylogenies. Second, a similarity-derived distance is not corrected for multiple substitutions at the same site, so long branches are systematically underestimated; MEGA and comparable packages offer corrected models \(Tamura et al. 2021). Third, bootstrap support measures how consistently the #emph[data] support a branch under this method, not whether the method is right: a systematically biased analysis can produce 100% support for the wrong tree, and frequently has. Use distance methods for a quick look at a family, as here, and a likelihood method for anything you intend to publish.

