== Further reading
<sec-04-further-reading>
- Needleman and Wunsch (1970) is the original global alignment paper and is short enough to read in an afternoon; the recursion in it is the one implemented above.
- Smith and Waterman (1981) introduces local alignment in two pages and is the clearest statement of why the zero term changes the question.
- Henikoff and Henikoff (1992) describes how the BLOSUM matrices were constructed, including the clustering step that distinguishes them from PAM.
- Altschul (1997) is the gapped-BLAST and PSI-BLAST paper, and the place to read about the alignment statistics that produce the E-value.
- Katoh and Standley (2013) documents MAFFT's algorithms and, usefully, states which of its strategies is appropriate for which dataset size.
- Eddy (2011) explains profile hidden Markov models, which detect homology well beyond the range of pairwise methods and underlie Pfam \(Mistry et al. 2020).
- Felsenstein (1985) introduced the bootstrap to phylogenetics and remains the best account of what the numbers on a tree do and do not mean.
- Minh et al. (2019) describes IQ-TREE, a practical route from the alignment produced in this chapter to a maximum-likelihood tree with model selection.

#part[The bioinformatics toolchain]
= The command line as a working environment
<the-command-line-as-a-working-environment>
#block[
#strong[After working through this chapter you will be able to]

- navigate a Unix filesystem with absolute and relative paths and read the permission bits on a file
- connect to a remote server and move data to and from it without retyping passwords or losing work when the connection drops
- compose `grep`, `sed`, `awk`, `sort`, `uniq` and `join` into pipelines that answer real questions about FASTA, FASTQ, GFF3 and VCF files
- stream compressed files without decompressing them, and index a VCF for random access
- diagnose a silently failing pipeline using exit status and `set -euo pipefail`
- write a small shell script that takes arguments, validates them and fails loudly
- submit a job to a SLURM scheduler and request resources that match what the job actually needs

]
Almost every program a bioinformatician runs is a command-line program. There is no window to click in `bwa mem`, no menu in `bcftools`, and no graphical interface on the compute node where a 40-sample alignment will run overnight. That is not an accident of history. A graphical interface is built around one object at a time; the work in this field is built around thousands of objects --- reads, genes, variants, samples --- and around the composition of small transformations into a pipeline that can be written down, inspected and run again next year on a different dataset.

This chapter treats the shell as a programming environment rather than a list of commands to memorise. The central idea is the stream: a program reads text, transforms it, and writes text another program can read in turn. Once you hold that idea, `grep`, `cut`, `sort`, `awk` and the rest stop being thirty separate tools and become one toolkit with thirty attachments.

Everything here is worked against the data bundled with this book. You will count reads in `data/reads/mgen_sim_R1.fastq.gz`, measure sequence lengths in `data/proteins/p53-orthologs.fasta`, tabulate feature types in the #emph[Mycoplasmoides genitalium] annotation `data/annotation/mycoplasma-genitalium.gff`, summarise the clinical classes in the #emph[TP53] ClinVar slice `data/variants/tp53_clinvar.vcf`, and join a protein interaction table to a Gene Ontology annotation file. By the end you will have built a small, reusable command-line tool and run it over every FASTA file in the bundle in parallel.

Each command block is followed by a block holding the real output of that command. All commands are run from the book's root directory --- the one containing `data/` --- so that every path is relative to a single, predictable place.

== Why the command line
<sec-why-the-command-line>
Three properties make the shell the right environment for this work, and none of them is about speed of typing.

The first is #strong[composability]. A graphical tool does what its author anticipated. A command-line tool does one thing and hands its result to the next, so you can assemble an analysis its authors never imagined. The classic demonstration is a text-processing task, given to a programmer as a week's work, that Doug McIlroy solved in six lines of shell by piping existing tools together --- an exchange that arose from the same body of work as Knuth's writing on literate programming \(Knuth 1984). Here, it means going from a VCF to a table of variant classes without writing a program.

The second is #strong[reproducibility]. A command is a record of what you did; a sequence of clicks is not. Recording commands in a script, under version control, in a project with a predictable layout is the single highest-return habit in computational research \(Wilson et al. 2017; Sandve et al. 2013).

The third is #strong[scale]. The machine in front of you probably has 8 or 16 GB of memory, and a single whole-genome sequencing run does not fit in it. Command-line tools are built to stream --- holding a few lines in memory while terabytes flow past --- and the shell is how you drive them. When the data outgrows the laptop, you run the same commands on a server, which has no graphical interface at all.

A #strong[shell] is a program that reads what you type, works out which program you meant, starts it, and shows you what it wrote. The default on most Linux systems and on macOS is `bash` or `zsh`, both of which accept the same core syntax used in this chapter. The shell is also a programming language, with variables, conditionals, loops and functions, which is why a session can be turned into a script with very little editing.

== The shell, the filesystem and permissions
<sec-shell-and-filesystem>
A Unix filesystem is a single tree. Its root is `/`, and everything --- your home directory, an attached hard disk, a shared project volume on a cluster --- hangs somewhere beneath it. A path that starts at the root, such as `/home/ahmad/projects/pathogens/data`, is #strong[absolute]: it means the same thing from anywhere. A path that does not, such as `data/reads`, is #strong[relative]: it is interpreted from your current directory. Two shorthands appear constantly: `.` is the current directory and `..` is its parent.

Relative paths are what make a project portable. If every command in your scripts says `data/reads/mgen_sim_R1.fastq.gz`, the project can be copied to a cluster and will still work; if one says `/Users/ahmad/Desktop/data/reads/mgen_sim_R1.fastq.gz`, it works on exactly one machine, for as long as that desktop is tidy.

A dozen commands cover almost all day-to-day navigation and file handling.

