== Further reading
<sec-05-further-reading>
- Wilson et al. (2017) is the shortest defensible statement of how to organise a computational project --- file layout, naming, version control --- and is worth reading before your next project rather than after it.
- Sandve et al. (2013) gives ten rules for reproducible computational research; rules 1, 4 and 6 are directly about recording commands and intermediate results, which is what this chapter's scripts are for.
- Danecek et al. (2021) documents SAMtools and BCFtools, including `bcftools query` format strings and the BGZF/tabix indexing used in #ref(<sec-compression-and-streaming>, supplement: [Section])\; it is the reference to keep open when working with VCF or BAM.
- Danecek et al. (2011) is the original VCF specification paper, and is the place to confirm what each column and INFO key is allowed to contain before writing a parser of your own.
- Köster and Rahmann (2012) and Di Tommaso et al. (2017) describe Snakemake and Nextflow, the two workflow managers most used in bioinformatics; read one of them when your shell loops acquire dependencies between steps.
- Grüning et al. (2018) explains Bioconda, which is how most of the command-line tools in this book are installed reproducibly on a laptop or a cluster without administrator rights.
- Ewels et al. (2016) describes MultiQC, the standard way to turn per-sample tool reports into one document; it pairs with the shell habits here because it consumes exactly the log files your pipelines produce.

= Python for bioinformatics
<python-for-bioinformatics>
#block[
#strong[After working through this chapter you will be able to]

- choose between the interpreter, a script and a notebook for a given piece of work
- manipulate sequence data with string and `Seq` operations, and explain why coordinates differ between Python and GFF3
- select the right collection --- list, tuple, dictionary or set --- for a biological question and justify the choice
- write a documented function with a default argument and defensive input checking
- parse FASTA and FASTQ files both by hand and with Biopython's `SeqIO`
- vectorise a per-base quality calculation with NumPy and summarise a variant table with pandas
- package a chapter's worth of functions into a tested, reusable module with a command-line interface

]
Python has become the language in which most new bioinformatics software is written, and the language most bioinformaticians reach for when no existing tool does what they need. The reason is not elegance but reach: the same language parses a FASTQ file, fits a model, queries NCBI, draws a figure and drives a pipeline, so an analysis need not be stitched together from four languages that disagree about what a missing value is.

This chapter teaches the language through biological data from the first line. There are no shopping lists and no `foo` variables. You will read the SARS-CoV-2 reference genome (`data/sequences/sars-cov-2-NC_045512.2.fasta`), translate the #emph[TP53] coding sequence from its GenBank record (`data/sequences/TP53-NM_000546.6.gb`), build a dictionary of p53 orthologues from `data/proteins/p53-orthologs.fasta`, compute per-base quality profiles from 20,000 simulated reads in `data/reads/mgen_sim_R1.fastq.gz`, tabulate the #emph[Mycoplasmoides genitalium] annotation in `data/annotation/mycoplasma-genitalium.gff`, and summarise 3,888 ClinVar records over #emph[TP53] from `data/variants/tp53_clinvar.vcf`.

The chapter ends with something you can keep: a small module of tested functions with a command-line interface, assembled from the pieces built along the way. That progression --- from an expression typed at a prompt, to a function, to a module another person can use --- is the one that turns someone who can read Python into someone who can be trusted to write it.

== Running Python
<sec-running-python>
Python can be used in three ways, and choosing badly between them is a common source of lost work.

The #strong[interpreter] is an interactive prompt: you type an expression, it answers. It is the right place to check what a function returns, inspect an unfamiliar object or test a one-line idea. Nothing is saved, which is precisely the point. Start it by typing `python` in a shell and leave it with `exit()`.

A #strong[script] is a text file of statements run from top to bottom by `python analysis.py`. It is the right form for anything that must be repeated or cited: a script is a record of what you did, in the sense discussed in the Unix chapter, and it is the only form that belongs in a pipeline.

A #strong[notebook] interleaves code, output and prose in one document. It suits exploration and teaching, and it is how most people meet Python \(Perez and Granger 2007). It also has a well-known hazard: cells can be run in any order, so a notebook that looks correct on screen may not run correctly from top to bottom, and the state it depends on may include a variable defined in a cell since deleted. Restart the kernel and run everything in order before you believe a result or send it to anyone --- the version of record is the one that runs from a clean state \(Sandve et al. 2013).

Whichever form you use, the packages must come from somewhere reproducible. Install Biopython, NumPy, pandas and the rest into a named environment with `conda` or `mamba` from the Bioconda and conda-forge channels \(Grüning et al. 2018), and export that environment to a file alongside your code. A script that runs today and not next year is usually a script whose dependencies were never written down.

Every chapter of this book begins with the same setup, which makes the bundled data reachable and applies the book's plotting style.

#block[
```
import sys; sys.path.insert(0, "..")
from pybfx import book_data, style
style()
```

]
