== Further reading
<sec-06-further-reading>
- Cock et al. (2009) is the Biopython reference; the project's own tutorial is the best route into `SeqIO`, `SeqRecord` and `Entrez`, and is kept current with the library.
- McKinney (2010) is the original description of pandas by its author, and remains the clearest statement of why a labelled, typed table is a better structure for data analysis than an array.
- Harris et al. (2020) describes NumPy's array model, and its section on broadcasting explains the rules that govern every operation combining arrays of different shapes.
- Hunter (2007) introduces Matplotlib; read it for the figure-axes model, which is what makes multi-panel figures manageable.
- Sayers et al. (2021) documents the NCBI databases and E-utilities that `Bio.Entrez` wraps, including the rate limits and the API-key mechanism.
- Anders et al. (2014) shows what a well-designed bioinformatics Python package looks like from the inside, and is worth reading as code as well as for its counting functions.
- Virtanen et al. (2020) covers SciPy, which you will need as soon as the analysis requires a statistical test rather than a summary statistic.
- Wilson et al. (2017) gives the project-level habits --- one directory per project, raw data read-only, code under version control --- that make the module built in #ref(<sec-python-tool>, supplement: [Section]) reusable rather than merely reused.

= R and Bioconductor
<r-and-bioconductor>
#block[
#strong[After working through this chapter you will be able to]

- explain where R's design makes statistical and genomic work shorter than the equivalent Python, and where it does not
- predict the result of R's recycling and coercion rules instead of discovering them in a wrong answer
- diagnose the three errors factors cause, and convert a factor to its labels safely
- subset a data frame in base R and with dplyr, and read both idioms in other people's code
- reshape a count matrix between wide and long form, and build a ggplot2 figure one layer at a time
- construct `Biostrings`, `GRanges` and `SummarizedExperiment` objects from bundled data and say what each class guarantees
- assemble a `SummarizedExperiment` in which sample annotation cannot become detached from its counts

]
R was written by statisticians, for statistics, and it shows in every corner of the language. There is no scalar type: a single number is a vector of length one. Missing data has its own value, `NA`, which propagates through arithmetic instead of quietly becoming zero. A linear model, a #emph[t]-test and a false discovery rate correction are in the base installation, not in a package. None of that is an accident, and none of it is how Python works. The consequence for bioinformatics is that a great deal of what you want to do with a count matrix, a table of variants or a set of genomic intervals is one line in R and a loop in most other languages.

The second reason to learn R is Bioconductor: a coordinated collection of more than two thousand packages, released twice a year as a tested set, built on shared data structures for sequences, genomic intervals and assay matrices. Almost every published RNA-seq, ChIP-seq, methylation and microarray analysis of the last twenty years passed through it. You cannot work in genomics for long without meeting a `GRanges` object or a `SummarizedExperiment`, and once you can read those two classes most Bioconductor documentation becomes intelligible.

This chapter uses five bundled datasets. The RNA interference experiment of Brooks et al. (2010) supplies a count matrix of 14,599 #emph[Drosophila] genes across seven samples (`data/expression/pasilla_gene_counts.tsv`) and its sample annotation (`data/expression/pasilla_sample_annotation.csv`); these carry the data-handling, reshaping and plotting sections, and the `SummarizedExperiment` you build at the end is the object the RNA-seq chapter uses for its DESeq2 analysis. The #emph[Mycoplasmoides genitalium] G37 genome (`data/sequences/mycoplasma-genitalium-NC_000908.2.fasta`) and its RefSeq annotation (`data/annotation/mycoplasma-genitalium.gff`) carry `Biostrings` and `GRanges`, and the eight p53 orthologues in `data/proteins/p53-orthologs.fasta` give a protein sequence set to work on.

The Python chapter covered some of this ground in another language, and this one does not repeat it. Where the two differ in how they think --- about vectors, about missing values, about where metadata lives --- the difference is the lesson.

== Why R, and where it wins
<sec-07-why-r>
R is an open implementation of the S language, which was designed at Bell Laboratories for interactive data analysis \(R Core Team 2024). Three features of that design matter daily.

#strong[Everything is a vector.] `length(3.14)` is 1. Arithmetic, comparison and most functions operate on whole vectors at once, so the loop you would write in another language is usually absent in R. That is not merely cosmetic: a loop you do not write is a loop whose index you cannot get wrong.

#strong[Statistics is in the language, not bolted on.] `t.test`, `lm`, `p.adjust`, `quantile` and the full family of probability distributions ship with R. When an analysis reaches the point of needing a #emph[p]-value, there is nothing to install.

#strong[Packages are a first-class concern.] CRAN holds over twenty thousand packages, each required to pass automated checks on several platforms before release; Bioconductor adds a biological layer on top with its own, stricter, review and a twice-yearly coordinated release.

Where R loses is equally worth knowing. It is slower than Python for text processing and for explicit loops, it has no equivalent of Biopython's parsers for the long tail of file formats, and its tooling for general software engineering is thinner. The honest division of labour is that pipelines and parsers tend to be written in Python and in shell, and the statistical analysis of what they produce tends to be written in R.

Every R chapter of this book opens with the same setup, which makes the bundled data reachable and sets the plotting style.

#block[
```
source(file.path("..", "R", "_common.R"))
cat(R.version.string, "\n")
#> R version 4.4.3 (2025-02-28)
```

]
== Vectors, vectorisation and recycling
<sec-07-vectors>
Read the count matrix with base R first, because the base functions are what you will meet in older scripts and in package source code.

#block[
```
counts_df <- read.delim(book_data("expression", "pasilla_gene_counts.tsv"))
count_matrix <- as.matrix(counts_df[, -1])
rownames(count_matrix) <- counts_df$gene_id

dim(count_matrix)
#> [1] 14599     7
count_matrix[1:3, 1:4]
#>             untreated1 untreated2 untreated3 untreated4
#> FBgn0000003          0          0          0          0
#> FBgn0000008         92        161         76         70
#> FBgn0000014          5          1          0          0
```

]
`read.delim` returns a data frame; dropping the first column with `[, -1]` and calling `as.matrix` gives a numeric matrix, and the gene identifiers move from a column to the row names. A matrix holds one type of value, which is what allows `colSums` and friends to be fast.

A row of that matrix is a named numeric vector, and most of what you want to know about it is a single function call.

#block[
```
pasilla_gene <- count_matrix["FBgn0000008", ]
pasilla_gene
#> untreated1 untreated2 untreated3 untreated4   treated1   treated2 
#>         92        161         76         70        140         88 
#>   treated3 
#>         70
c(mean = mean(pasilla_gene), sd = sd(pasilla_gene), median = median(pasilla_gene))
#>   mean     sd median 
#>  99.57  36.29  88.00
```

]
#strong[Vectorisation] means an operation applies to every element without a loop. Comparison returns a logical vector, and logical vectors are how you select.

#block[
```
pasilla_gene > 100
#> untreated1 untreated2 untreated3 untreated4   treated1   treated2 
#>      FALSE       TRUE      FALSE      FALSE       TRUE      FALSE 
#>   treated3 
#>      FALSE
names(which(pasilla_gene > 100))
#> [1] "untreated2" "treated1"
sum(pasilla_gene > 100)
#> [1] 2
```

]
`sum` on a logical vector counts the `TRUE` values, because `TRUE` becomes 1 when a numeric answer is required. That is #strong[coercion], R's rule for mixing types: a vector holds one type, so combining types silently promotes everything to the most general one, in the order logical → integer → double → character. `c(1, 2, "three")` is a character vector in which the `1` is now the text `"1"`. A column of counts arriving as character because one spreadsheet cell said `not done` is this rule in action, and the symptom is arithmetic refusing to work on data that looks numeric.

Normalising counts to counts per million exposes the second rule, #strong[recycling]: when two vectors of different lengths meet, R repeats the shorter one until it matches the longer.

#block[
```
c(10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60) / c(1, 2)
#> [1] 10 10 30 20 50 30
library_size <- colSums(count_matrix)
library_size
#> untreated1 untreated2 untreated3 untreated4   treated1   treated2 
#>   13972512   21911438    8358426    9841335   18670279    9571826 
#>   treated3 
#>   10343856
```

]
Recycling is convenient when the short vector has length one --- `count_matrix / 1000` divides everything by 1,000 --- and dangerous otherwise. Dividing the whole matrix by the vector of library sizes looks correct and is not.

#block[
```
wrong_cpm <- 1e6 * count_matrix / library_size
right_cpm <- 1e6 * t(t(count_matrix) / library_size)

round(wrong_cpm["FBgn0000008", ], 2)
#> untreated1 untreated2 untreated3 untreated4   treated1   treated2 
#>       4.20      16.82       9.09       6.77      14.23       6.30 
#>   treated3 
#>       3.75
round(right_cpm["FBgn0000008", ], 2)
#> untreated1 untreated2 untreated3 untreated4   treated1   treated2 
#>       6.58       7.35       9.09       7.11       7.50       9.19 
#>   treated3 
#>       6.77
round(colSums(right_cpm))
#> untreated1 untreated2 untreated3 untreated4   treated1   treated2 
#>      1e+06      1e+06      1e+06      1e+06      1e+06      1e+06 
#>   treated3 
#>      1e+06
```

]
A matrix in R is a vector with a dimension attribute, filled column by column, so dividing it by a length-7 vector recycles that vector #emph[down the rows], not across the columns. Each count is divided by whichever library size happens to land on it. The result is numerically plausible --- all seven values are small positive numbers of the right order of magnitude, and one of them, `untreated3`, is even correct by coincidence --- which is exactly why this is a dangerous bug rather than an obvious one. Transposing twice, as `right_cpm` does, aligns the vector with the columns; the check that proves it is that every column of the corrected matrix now sums to exactly one million.

Missing values follow their own rule. `NA` is contagious: any arithmetic involving it returns `NA`, so that a missing measurement cannot be silently treated as zero.

#block[
```
measurements <- c(12, 7, NA, 31)
mean(measurements)
#> [1] NA
mean(measurements, na.rm = TRUE)
#> [1] 16.67
sum(is.na(measurements))
#> [1] 1
```

]
The design decision is deliberate, and it is one of the places R is clearly better than a language in which a missing value becomes an empty string or a zero. If you want the mean of what you have, you must say so with `na.rm = TRUE`, and saying so is a decision recorded in the code.

== Factors, and the trouble they cause
<sec-07-factors>
A #strong[factor] is R's type for a categorical variable: it stores the data as integer codes plus a table of #strong[levels], the permitted categories. Statistical functions need this --- `lm` and `t.test` must know that `treated` and `untreated` are two levels of one variable rather than two arbitrary strings --- and the level order sets the reference category, which decides the sign of every coefficient a model reports.

#block[
```
condition <- factor(c("untreated", "treated", "treated", "untreated"))
condition
#> [1] untreated treated   treated   untreated
#> Levels: treated untreated
levels(condition)
#> [1] "treated"   "untreated"
as.integer(condition)
#> [1] 2 1 1 2
```

]
The levels came out in alphabetical order, so `treated` is the reference and a model fitted on this variable would report changes #emph[relative to the treated samples] --- the opposite of what the experiment means. Set the order explicitly whenever it matters.

#block[
```
condition <- factor(c("untreated", "treated", "treated", "untreated"),
                    levels = c("untreated", "treated"))
levels(condition)
#> [1] "untreated" "treated"
```

]
Three failure modes follow from the integer-plus-levels design, and all three appear in real analyses.

#block[
```
lane_counts <- factor(c("5", "2", "2", "2", "6", "2", "2"))
as.numeric(lane_counts)
#> [1] 2 1 1 1 3 1 1
as.numeric(as.character(lane_counts))
#> [1] 5 2 2 2 6 2 2

treated_only <- condition[condition == "treated"]
table(treated_only)
#> treated_only
#> untreated   treated 
#>         0         2
table(droplevels(treated_only))
#> 
#> treated 
#>       2
```

]
The first trap is the serious one: `as.numeric` on a factor returns the internal codes, not the labels, so a column of lane counts converted this way becomes a different set of numbers with no error anywhere. The route through `as.character` is the only safe one. The second trap is that subsetting keeps levels that no longer occur, so `table` reports a category with zero members and `ggplot2` draws an empty box for it; `droplevels` removes them. The third is that combining two factors with different levels works on the integer codes rather than the labels, so convert to character, combine, and re-factor.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
Before R 4.0, `read.csv` turned every character column into a factor by default, and a decade of analyses carry bugs from it. The default is now `stringsAsFactors = FALSE`, so modern code gets character columns --- but data read with older code, loaded from an `.RData` file, or returned by a package that still converts, may be factors. When a numeric column behaves strangely, check `class()` before anything else, and convert with `as.numeric(as.character(x))`, never `as.numeric(x)`.

]
, 
