== Further reading
<sec-07-further-reading>
- Wickham et al. (2019) is the paper that introduced the tidyverse as a coherent set; read it for the design principles that explain why the verbs are shaped as they are.
- Gentleman et al. (2004) is the original Bioconductor paper and is still the clearest statement of why shared classes, rather than shared functions, were the right thing to standardise.
- Huber et al. (2015) describes the modern Bioconductor infrastructure, including `SummarizedExperiment`, and is the reference to cite when you use the class.
- Lawrence et al. (2013) sets out the range-based classes and the algorithms behind `findOverlaps`\; worth reading before you write any interval arithmetic of your own.
- Love et al. (2014) is the DESeq2 paper, and its methods section explains why the naive #emph[t]-test in #ref(<sec-07-iteration>, supplement: [Section]) is the wrong model for count data.
- Xie (2016) documents `knitr`, the engine that executes the chunks in this book; its chapter on chunk options answers most questions about controlling output.
- Yu et al. (2012) is a well-built Bioconductor package to read as code: it takes a gene list and returns an enrichment result, using the shared classes throughout.
- Cock et al. (2009) is the Biopython reference, worth comparing with `Biostrings`: the same operations, a different design, and an illustration of how much of a library's shape follows from its host language.

#part[Genomes, reads and structures]
= From assembled sequence to annotated genome
<from-assembled-sequence-to-annotated-genome>
#block[
#strong[After working through this chapter you will be able to]

- distinguish contigs, scaffolds and chromosomes, and explain why N50 alone is a poor summary of assembly quality
- compute length, base composition and coding density for an assembled genome before committing to an annotation
- run a prokaryotic gene finder and justify the genetic code and model options you chose
- compare two annotations of the same genome and classify their disagreements as gene-level or boundary-level
- explain why eukaryotic gene prediction needs external evidence that prokaryotic prediction does not
- parse GFF3 column by column, including the attributes column and parent/child relationships, without mis-handling coordinates
- judge whether a functional assignment is supported by evidence or inherited from a previous guess

]
An assembler hands you a FASTA file. That file is a hypothesis about the order of bases in an organism's genome, and nothing more: it contains no genes, no operons, no promoters and no names. Annotation is the process of turning that string of letters into a set of claims about biology --- this region is transcribed, that one encodes a protein, this protein probably hydrolyses ATP --- each of which can be right, wrong, or right for the wrong reason. The claims are what everyone downstream will actually use. A differential expression analysis counts reads against annotated gene models; a variant effect predictor decides whether a substitution is synonymous by consulting annotated reading frames; a pathway enrichment test inherits every functional label the annotator assigned. Errors introduced here do not stay here.

This chapter works through annotation on a genome small enough to hold in your head and real enough to behave badly: #emph[Mycoplasmoides genitalium] G37 \(Fraser et al. 1995), bundled as `data/sequences/mycoplasma-genitalium-NC_000908.2.fasta`. At 580 kb it is among the smallest genomes of any free-living organism, which means it can be annotated completely in under a second on a laptop and its annotation can be checked by hand. Alongside it sits the current RefSeq annotation, `data/annotation/mycoplasma-genitalium.gff`, produced by a curated pipeline. You will measure the genome, predict its genes yourself with Prodigal, and then compare your predictions with RefSeq's --- not to decide who is right, but to learn what the disagreements between two competent annotations look like and where they come from.

By the end you will have produced a gene set, quantified its agreement with a reference annotation at both the gene level and the start-codon level, measured coding density along the chromosome, and dissected the GFF3 file format closely enough that you can debug one when a downstream tool refuses to read it. The habits that matter are smaller than any of that: check the genome before you annotate it, record which evidence supports which claim, and never let an inherited label masquerade as a measurement.

