== Judging an assembly before you annotate it
<sec-08-assembly-quality>
An assembler takes reads and reports #strong[contigs]: contiguous stretches of sequence that the evidence supports without interruption. If paired-end or long-range linking information is available, contigs can be ordered and oriented into #strong[scaffolds], in which the gaps of unknown sequence between contigs are represented by runs of `N`. A scaffold is therefore a weaker claim than a contig --- it asserts adjacency and approximate distance, not sequence. Only when scaffolds are placed on a genetic or physical map, or assembled telomere to telomere, do they become #strong[chromosomes] \(Nurk et al. 2022). Short-read assemblers such as SPAdes \(Bankevich et al. 2012) fragment at repeats longer than the read pairs can span; long-read assemblers such as Canu \(Koren et al. 2016) and Flye \(Kolmogorov et al. 2018) resolve most of those repeats but inherit the higher per-base error of the underlying chemistry.

The statistic you will see quoted first is almost always #strong[N50]: the length of the contig such that contigs of that length or longer contain at least half of the assembly's total bases. It is a weighted median, and that is exactly its weakness.

#block[
```
def n50(lengths):
    """Length of the contig at which half the assembly's bases are reached."""
    ordered = sorted(lengths, reverse=True)
    half = sum(ordered) / 2
    running = 0
    for length in ordered:
        running += length
        if running >= half:
            return length
    raise ValueError("empty assembly")

careful = [900_000, 800_000, 700_000, 600_000, 500_000] + [1_000] * 500
reckless = [2_500_000] + [1_000] * 1_500
print(f"careful : {len(careful):4d} contigs, {sum(careful):,} bp, N50 = {n50(careful):,}")
#> careful :  505 contigs, 4,000,000 bp, N50 = 700,000
print(f"reckless: {len(reckless):4d} contigs, {sum(reckless):,} bp, N50 = {n50(reckless):,}")
#> reckless: 1501 contigs, 4,000,000 bp, N50 = 2,500,000
```

]
Both assemblies have the same total length. The second has a much larger N50 and is much worse: it was produced by an assembler that resolved an ambiguity aggressively and fused regions that the data did not justify joining. N50 rewards exactly that behaviour, because it measures contiguity and says nothing about correctness. It is also not comparable between assemblies of different total size, which is why #strong[NG50], computed against an expected genome size rather than the assembled total, is preferred when a reference size is known. QUAST \(Gurevich et al. 2013) reports both, along with misassembly counts when a reference is available.

Two further checks matter more than contiguity. #strong[Completeness] is usually estimated with BUSCO \(Manni et al. 2021), which searches the assembly for a lineage-specific set of genes present in single copy in nearly all members of that lineage and reports each as complete, duplicated, fragmented or missing. A high fragmented count points at an under-assembled or error-rich draft; a high duplicated count points at uncollapsed haplotypes or genuine polyploidy. #strong[Contamination] is the other check: Kraken 2 \(Wood et al. 2019) classifies contigs taxonomically, and contigs whose taxonomy disagrees with the rest of the assembly are candidates for removal. Annotating a contaminated assembly produces genes that are real but belong to the wrong organism, and they propagate into every database that mirrors your submission.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
Record total length, number of contigs and BUSCO completeness in your laboratory notebook before the annotation step, and again after. If the gene count surprises you later, these three numbers tell you immediately whether to suspect the assembly or the gene finder.

]
