== Measuring the genome in hand
<sec-08-genome-stats>
The bundled #emph[M. genitalium] assembly is a finished, circular, single-contig genome, so contiguity statistics are trivial: N50 equals the genome length. What is worth measuring is composition, because composition constrains everything that follows.

#block[
```
from collections import Counter
from Bio import SeqIO

record = SeqIO.read(book_data("sequences", "mycoplasma-genitalium-NC_000908.2.fasta"),
                    "fasta")
genome = str(record.seq).upper()
counts = Counter(genome)
gc_percent = 100 * (counts["G"] + counts["C"]) / len(genome)

print(f"{record.id}: {len(genome):,} bp")
#> NC_000908.2: 580,076 bp
print(f"A={counts['A']:,}  C={counts['C']:,}  G={counts['G']:,}  T={counts['T']:,}")
#> A=200,544  C=91,515  G=92,306  T=195,711
print(f"ambiguous bases: {len(genome) - sum(counts[b] for b in 'ACGT')}")
#> ambiguous bases: 0
print(f"GC content: {gc_percent:.2f}%")
#> GC content: 31.69%
```

]
Three things follow from that output. There are no ambiguous bases, so this is a finished sequence rather than a draft with gaps. The GC content of 31.69% is low, which matters for gene finding because codon usage and start-codon preference both shift with composition, and because a naive open-reading-frame scan in an AT-rich genome produces more spurious long ORFs by chance. And at 580,076 bp the genome is small enough that an exhaustive six-frame analysis costs nothing.

#emph[M. genitalium] belongs to the Mollicutes, which use #strong[genetic code 4]: the codon TGA encodes tryptophan rather than termination. Getting this wrong is not a subtlety. Under the bacterial default, code 11, every internal TGA is read as a stop, and genes are chopped into fragments.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
Translation tables differ between bacterial clades, between nuclear and mitochondrial genomes, and between some ciliates and everything else. A gene finder run with the wrong table does not fail --- it returns a plausible-looking answer with roughly twice as many, roughly half as long, genes. You will see this quantified in #ref(<sec-08-prokaryotic-prediction>, supplement: [Section]). Always look up the translation table for your organism before the first run, and record it in your methods.

]
