== Gene prediction in eukaryotes
<sec-08-eukaryotic>
Everything above depended on the assumption that a gene is one uninterrupted reading frame. In eukaryotes it is not, and every part of the problem becomes harder.

A eukaryotic protein-coding gene is transcribed as a pre-mRNA containing #strong[exons] separated by #strong[introns] that are spliced out. The spliceosome recognises short, degenerate signals: a GT at the intron's 5' end, an AG at its 3' end, a branch point and a polypyrimidine tract. Those motifs are far too short to locate reliably on their own --- a genome contains a GT every 16 bp by chance --- so a predictor must combine them with coding-potential models on both sides and with constraints on intron length. Human introns range from under 100 bp to over a megabase, so the search space is enormous. #strong[Alternative splicing] then means that the correct answer is not one gene model but several: the same locus can produce transcripts with different exon content, different reading frames and different protein products. A tool asked to report a single model per locus is being asked the wrong question.

#emph[Ab initio] eukaryotic predictors such as AUGUSTUS \(Stanke et al. 2006) use generalised hidden Markov models whose states correspond to the parts of a gene --- intergenic, 5' UTR, initial exon, intron, internal exon, terminal exon --- and whose parameters must be trained on a set of known genes from a related species. Run on a novel genome with mis-matched training parameters, they produce gene sets whose counts can be wrong by a factor of two.

Modern practice is therefore #strong[evidence-based annotation]: predictions are made in the presence of data that directly observes transcription. RNA-seq reads aligned with a spliced aligner give exon boundaries and splice junctions empirically --- a junction supported by 200 reads is not a hypothesis --- and full-length long-read cDNA gives complete models. Proteins from related species, aligned to the genome, confirm coding regions and reading frames. A combiner reconciles all three into a consensus gene set, so the annotation's quality is bounded by the tissue coverage of the evidence: a gene expressed only in a developmental stage you did not sample will be annotated badly or not at all.

