== From a predicted protein to a biological function
<sec-08-functional>
Structural annotation says where the genes are. #strong[Functional annotation] says what they do, and it is almost entirely an exercise in inference by similarity.

The simplest form is #strong[homology transfer]: search the predicted protein against a curated database with BLAST \(Altschul et al. 1990) or a more sensitive profile method, and copy the description of the best hit. This works when the hit is strong, nearly full length and to a protein whose function was experimentally determined. It fails in characteristic ways. Similarity over a shared domain does not imply shared overall function; a protein kinase domain tells you the protein phosphorylates something, not what. Enzymes in the same superfamily can differ in substrate. And best-hit transfer confuses #strong[orthologues] --- genes in different species that descend from a single ancestral gene via speciation, and usually do keep their function --- with #strong[paralogues], which arose by duplication within a lineage and frequently diverge in function. Orthology-aware resources exist because the best BLAST hit is often a paralogue.

#strong[Domain annotation] is more conservative and more durable. Pfam \(Mistry et al. 2020) models protein families and domains as profile hidden Markov models built from curated seed alignments, and searching a protein against them with HMMER \(Eddy 2011) reports which domains are present, in what order, and over which residues. InterPro \(Paysan-Lafosse et al. 2022) integrates Pfam with a dozen other signature databases and maps the result onto Gene Ontology terms. A domain architecture is a statement about the protein's parts and is far less likely to be wrong than a transferred description, which is why curated resources show domains and evidence rather than a single functional label.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
When a protein is annotated by copying the description of its best database hit, and that description was itself copied from an earlier best hit, the chain of inference can be many steps long and no step is recorded. A single mis-annotation in a well-populated family can be re-copied into hundreds of genomes within a few years, and the resulting consensus looks like independent corroboration. The defences are specific: prefer domain-level and orthology-based assignments to best-hit transfer; demand alignment coverage of at least 70--80% of both query and subject before transferring a description; treat a hit whose own annotation carries an electronic evidence code as a hypothesis, not a fact; and propagate the evidence alongside the label so the next person can re-evaluate it.

]
, 
