=== Controlled vocabularies and evidence codes
<controlled-vocabularies-and-evidence-codes>
Free-text product descriptions cannot be computed on: `DNA polymerase III subunit beta`, `DNA pol III beta` and `beta clamp` are the same thing to a reader and three different strings to a program. Controlled vocabularies solve this by assigning stable identifiers to concepts.

The #strong[Gene Ontology] \(Ashburner et al. 2000; Aleksander et al. 2023) is the most widely used. It provides three independent hierarchies --- molecular function, biological process and cellular component --- organised as a directed acyclic graph in which a term may have several parents and in which an annotation to a term implies annotation to all its ancestors. A gene annotated to `GO:0003887` (DNA-directed DNA polymerase activity) is thereby also annotated to the broader nucleotidyltransferase and catalytic activity terms, which is what makes enrichment analysis possible. #strong[EC numbers] provide a complementary four-level classification of enzymatic reactions (`2.7.7.7` is DNA-directed DNA polymerase), and KEGG \(Kanehisa 2000) maps gene products onto metabolic and signalling pathways.

Every GO annotation carries an #strong[evidence code] stating how it was arrived at. `EXP` and its children mark experimental evidence; `IDA` is a direct assay; `IMP` is inference from a mutant phenotype; `ISS` is inference from sequence similarity, assigned by a curator; `IEA` is inferred from electronic annotation, assigned automatically with no curator review. The RefSeq GFF carries these in its `go_function`, `go_process` and `go_component` attributes.

#block[
```
evidence = Counter()
with_go = 0
for feature in reference_cds:
    has_go = False
    for key in ("go_function", "go_process", "go_component"):
        if key in feature["attributes"]:
            has_go = True
            for item in feature["attributes"][key].split(","):
                parts = item.split("|")
                if len(parts) >= 4:
                    evidence[parts[3]] += 1
    with_go += has_go

print(f"CDS with at least one GO term: {with_go} of {len(reference_cds)}")
#> CDS with at least one GO term: 331 of 524
print("evidence codes:", dict(evidence))
#> evidence codes: {'IEA': 985}
```

]
Every one of the 985 GO annotations in this file carries the code `IEA`. Not one was reviewed by a curator for this organism. This is entirely normal --- the overwhelming majority of GO annotations in any genome are IEA --- but it changes how you should read an enrichment result. An enrichment of a GO term among your differentially expressed genes, computed against an IEA-only background, is a statement about which sequence features your gene set shares, filtered through a transfer pipeline. It is a hypothesis-generating observation, not evidence that the process is active.

