== Further reading
<sec-08-further-reading>
- Hyatt et al. (2010) is the Prodigal paper; its description of how the start-site model is trained explains most of the boundary disagreements seen in #ref(<sec-08-agreement>, supplement: [Section]).
- Seemann (2014) describes Prokka, which wraps Prodigal together with tRNA, rRNA and functional annotation steps and is the practical starting point for annotating a bacterial draft.
- Tatusova et al. (2016) documents the NCBI pipeline that produced the RefSeq annotation used here, and is worth reading before you submit a genome and receive an annotation you did not make.
- Stanke et al. (2006) introduces AUGUSTUS and, more usefully, sets out clearly why eukaryotic gene prediction needs training data and external evidence.
- Manni et al. (2021) explains what BUSCO does and does not measure; read it before quoting a completeness percentage in a manuscript.
- Paysan-Lafosse et al. (2022) describes InterPro and the integration of member databases; it is the right reference for understanding what a domain-based functional assignment is actually claiming.
- Ashburner et al. (2000) and Aleksander et al. (2023) together give the original rationale for the Gene Ontology and its current state, including the evidence-code system that governs how much weight an annotation deserves.

= Next-generation sequencing data from reads to results
<next-generation-sequencing-data-from-reads-to-results>
#block[
#strong[After working through this chapter you will be able to]

- relate the error profile of Illumina, Nanopore and PacBio HiFi data to the analysis decisions each one forces on you
- explain which artefacts in a dataset were created by library preparation rather than by biology
- decode a FASTQ record by hand, including its Phred scores, and compute the expected number of errors in a read
- reproduce the diagnostic plots of a quality report from the raw file and decide, with reasons, whether to trim
- interpret the eleven mandatory SAM fields, decode a FLAG, read a CIGAR string and explain what a MAPQ of zero means
- dissect a VCF file, distinguish INFO from FORMAT fields and design a filtering strategy that is defensible
- run a complete differential expression analysis with DESeq2 and state precisely what its adjusted #emph[p]-values do and do not promise

]
A sequencing run produces no biology. It produces a few gigabytes of text in which every line is a machine's opinion about which base sat at which position in which fragment of DNA, with a number saying how sure it was. Everything such an experiment eventually claims --- this sample carries a pathogenic variant, this gene is induced three-fold --- is an inference built on those opinions through a chain of transformations, every one of which can be done well or badly.

Three bundled datasets carry the work. The first is a pair of simulated read files, `data/reads/mgen_sim_R1.fastq.gz` and `data/reads/mgen_sim_R2.fastq.gz`: 20,000 paired 150 bp reads drawn from the #emph[Mycoplasmoides genitalium] G37 genome \(Fraser et al. 1995) that the previous chapter annotated, with position-dependent quality decay and matching sequencing errors. Because they were simulated, every read name records the coordinate the fragment came from --- a luxury no real dataset offers, because it lets you check an alignment against the truth rather than against another program's opinion. The second is `data/variants/tp53_clinvar.vcf`, a slice of ClinVar \(Landrum et al. 2017) covering 3,888 variants in #emph[TP53]. The third is `data/expression/pasilla_gene_counts.tsv`, an RNA interference knockdown of the #emph[Drosophila] splicing factor #emph[pasilla] \(Brooks et al. 2010), on which the final section runs a differential expression analysis from raw counts to an interpreted gene list.

By the end you will have parsed a FASTQ file without a specialised package, rebuilt the central panels of a quality report, aligned reads and checked the result against ground truth, decoded SAM flags and CIGAR strings by hand, filtered variants, and taken a count matrix through normalisation, dispersion estimation, testing and false discovery control to a defensible gene list. The habit being built is smaller than any of those steps: look at the data at every stage, and know which numbers were measured and which were assumed.

== Setting up
<sec-09-setup>
#block[
```
source(file.path("..", "R", "_common.R"))
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(ggplot2)
```

]
== Sequencing chemistries and the errors they make
<sec-09-chemistries>
An analyst does not need to be able to build a sequencer, but does need to know what each chemistry does when it is uncertain, because that determines which artefacts are plausible and which are not.

#strong[Illumina sequencing by synthesis (SBS)] amplifies each library fragment in place on a flow cell into a cluster of perhaps a thousand copies, then sequences one base per cycle with fluorescently labelled, reversibly terminated nucleotides, imaging the cell between cycles. Two consequences follow. The signal is averaged over a whole cluster, so the method depends on every copy staying in step; molecules that fail to incorporate fall behind (phasing) and molecules that incorporate twice run ahead (pre-phasing), the fraction out of step grows every cycle, and base-call confidence decays along the read. Every Illumina per-position quality plot slopes downwards for this reason, and it is not a fault. And because the error mechanism is a mis-read signal rather than a mis-counted base, the errors are overwhelmingly #strong[substitutions] --- about one base in a thousand early in a read, rising towards one in a hundred at the end --- with indels rare. Reads are 50--300 bp, usually taken from both ends of a fragment.

#strong[Oxford Nanopore] draws a single strand through a protein pore and records the ionic current, which a neural network converts to bases. Nothing is amplified and there is no cycle, so there is no decay along the read and no length limit; tens of kilobases are routine. The errors are #strong[insertions and deletions], concentrated in homopolymer runs where six and seven identical bases give nearly the same current. The devices are portable, which is why they became central to outbreak response \(Quick et al. 2017).

#strong[PacBio HiFi] reads a circularised fragment round and round with one polymerase and reports the consensus of the passes. The per-pass error rate is high but random, so averaging over ten or more passes drives the consensus below one error in a thousand while retaining 15--25 kb reads.

The chemistry decides much of what follows. Short, accurate, substitution-dominated reads suit variant calling, RNA-seq counting and population surveys; long, noisy reads resolve repeats and full-length transcripts and need a caller that models indel noise; long, accurate reads are the current basis for reference-quality assembly. The question is never which chemistry is best but which error mode you can tolerate. A homopolymer-length difference, for instance, is weak evidence from Nanopore data and strong evidence from Illumina or HiFi data.

== What library preparation leaves behind
<sec-09-library>
Between the sample and the flow cell sits the library: DNA fragmented to a chosen size, with synthetic adapters ligated to both ends, often amplified by PCR, and tagged with an index so that many samples can share a run. Each step leaves a signature, and recognising a signature as preparation rather than biology saves wasted interpretation.

#strong[Adapters] are a handle, not genome. If a fragment (the #strong[insert]) is longer than the read, the read stops before reaching the far adapter; if it is shorter, the polymerase reads off the end of the insert into the adapter beyond --- #strong[read-through] --- and the tail of the read is synthetic sequence that will not align. Adapter contamination therefore rises as insert size falls.

#strong[Duplicates] come from PCR, which copies some fragments more than others, every copy carrying the same start and end coordinates. They inflate apparent depth without adding information, because a variant seen in ten reads that are copies of one molecule has been observed once; #ref(<sec-09-sam>, supplement: [Section]) marks them. #strong[Insert size] reads out the size selection step, and a long tail or a second mode in a bacterial isolate usually means a structural difference or a contaminant.

#strong[Strandedness] matters only for RNA: an unstranded library loses which strand a transcript came from, so reads from overlapping genes on opposite strands cannot be assigned. Getting the setting wrong when counting is a quiet failure --- most genes look plausible and the ones with antisense neighbours are wrong.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
Marking duplicates is correct for whole-genome and exome resequencing, where fragments are randomly sheared and identical start points are improbable. It is wrong for amplicon sequencing, where every read starts at the same primer by design, and usually wrong for RNA-seq, where a highly expressed transcript genuinely produces many fragments with identical ends. Unique molecular identifiers --- random barcodes added before amplification --- are the only way to tell a PCR copy from a coincidence with certainty.

]
, 
title: 
[
Do not remove duplicates reflexively
]
