]
The `CIGAR` string says how the read lines up, as a run-length encoded list of operations: `M` for an aligned column (match or mismatch), `I` and `D` for insertion into and deletion from the reference, `S` for soft-clipping (bases in `SEQ` but not aligned), `H` for hard-clipping (bases absent from `SEQ`) and `N` for a skipped reference region, which is what a spliced aligner emits across an intron. Thirteen of the 40,000 real alignments carry a CIGAR other than `150M`\; the commonest is dissected below.

#block[
```
parse_cigar <- function(cigar) {
  parts <- regmatches(cigar, gregexpr("[0-9]+[MIDNSHP=X]", cigar))[[1]]
  data.frame(length = as.integer(sub("[A-Z=]$", "", parts)),
             operation = sub("^[0-9]+", "", parts))
}

consumes_reference <- c("M", "D", "N", "=", "X")
consumes_query <- c("M", "I", "S", "=", "X")

cigar <- "3S147M"
operations <- parse_cigar(cigar)
print(operations)
#>   length operation
#> 1      3         S
#> 2    147         M
cat(sprintf("read bases accounted for   : %d\n",
            sum(operations$length[operations$operation %in% consumes_query])))
#> read bases accounted for   : 150
cat(sprintf("reference bases spanned    : %d\n",
            sum(operations$length[operations$operation %in% consumes_reference])))
#> reference bases spanned    : 147
cat(sprintf("alignment ends at reference: %d\n",
            35397 + sum(operations$length[operations$operation %in% consumes_reference]) - 1))
#> alignment ends at reference: 35543
```

]
The read is 150 bp and `POS` is 35,397, but only 147 bases align: the first three were soft-clipped because they did not match, almost certainly simulated errors. Two rules follow. Operations consuming the query must sum to the read length or the record is malformed; operations consuming the reference give the alignment's span, which is how an end coordinate is computed --- `POS + length(SEQ)` is wrong whenever the CIGAR contains `I`, `S`, `N` or `D`.

=== Marking duplicates
<marking-duplicates>
Duplicate marking compares the outer coordinates of each pair and flags all but one copy of each set that agrees. The samtools route needs a name-sorted pass first, to fill in mate coordinates before they are compared.

```
samtools sort -n -o namesort.bam mgen_sim.bam
samtools fixmate -m namesort.bam fixmate.bam
samtools sort -o positionsort.bam fixmate.bam
samtools markdup -s positionsort.bam mgen_sim.markdup.bam
```

The `-s` flag asks for statistics, and the real run reported:

```
READ: 40000
WRITTEN: 40000
EXCLUDED: 0
EXAMINED: 40000
PAIRED: 40000
DUPLICATE PAIR: 708
DUPLICATE SINGLE: 0
DUPLICATE PAIR OPTICAL: 0
DUPLICATE TOTAL: 708
ESTIMATED_LIBRARY_SIZE: 558285
```

Note what marking does: 40,000 reads in, 40,000 out, duplicates flagged by bit 1024 rather than deleted. Every one of those 708 flagged reads --- 354 pairs --- is a coincidence rather than a PCR copy, because the simulator performed no amplification, and the birthday problem predicts how many to expect.

#block[
```
fragments <- 20000
start_positions <- 580076 - 350
distinct_expected <- start_positions * (1 - (1 - 1 / start_positions)^fragments)
cat(sprintf("fragments simulated          : %d\n", fragments))
#> fragments simulated          : 20000
cat(sprintf("distinct start points expected: %.0f\n", distinct_expected))
#> distinct start points expected: 19659
cat(sprintf("coincidental duplicate pairs  : %.0f\n", fragments - distinct_expected))
#> coincidental duplicate pairs  : 341
```

]
About 340 predicted against the 354 samtools marked. On a 580 kb genome collisions are common; on a 3 Gb genome with the same number of fragments they would be negligible. Duplicate rate is therefore uninterpretable without the target size and the depth, which is why "our duplicate rate was 18%" means nothing on its own.

== From alignments to variants
<sec-09-variants>
A variant caller turns alignments into a statement about genotype. At each position it assembles the #strong[pileup] --- every base from every overlapping read, with its base quality and its read's mapping quality --- and asks which genotype best explains the evidence. The calculation is a likelihood, not a vote: three alternative bases among thirty reads mean one thing if those three are low quality and quite another if they are not. bcftools \(Danecek et al. 2021) does this directly; GATK's HaplotypeCaller \(McKenna et al. 2010) first reassembles each active region into candidate haplotypes, which is what lets it call indels and clustered substitutions that a position-by-position model mishandles \(DePristo et al. 2011).

Running bcftools on the alignment from #ref(<sec-09-alignment>, supplement: [Section]) is instructive precisely because there is nothing to find: the reads came from the reference, so every candidate is an artefact of sequencing error.

```
bcftools mpileup -f mgen.fasta -q 20 -Q 20 -a FORMAT/AD,FORMAT/DP mgen_sim.markdup.bam \
  | bcftools call -mv -Ov -o raw_calls.vcf
bcftools view -H raw_calls.vcf
```

The single record it produced, verbatim:

```
NC_000908.2 430570  .   T   C   3.07051 .   DP=7;VDB=0.92;SGB=-0.453602;
RPBZ=-0.387298;MQBZ=0;MQSBZ=0;BQBZ=-0.401918;SCBZ=0;MQ0F=0;AC=1;AN=2;
DP4=5,0,0,2;MQ=60   GT:PL:DP:AD 0/1:34,0,110:7:5,2
```

One false positive in 580 kb at roughly ten-fold coverage, and it announces itself. `QUAL` is 3.07, so the caller is barely more confident in the variant than in the reference; `DP=7` is below the average depth; and `DP4=5,0,0,2` --- reference-forward, reference-reverse, alternative-forward, alternative-reverse --- puts both alternative reads on one strand, the classic strand-bias signature of an artefact. Filtering on `QUAL >= 30 && INFO/DP >= 10` leaves an empty call set, the correct answer here.

The VCF format \(Danecek et al. 2011) is where most of the day-to-day difficulty lives. A VCF has a header of `##` lines defining every key used below, a `#CHROM` line naming the columns, then one line per site with eight fixed fields --- `CHROM`, `POS`, `ID`, `REF`, `ALT`, `QUAL`, `FILTER`, `INFO` --- optionally followed by `FORMAT` and one column per sample. The bundled ClinVar slice is #emph[sites-only]: it describes variants in the abstract rather than genotypes in an individual, so it has no sample columns.

#block[
```
suppressPackageStartupMessages(library(VariantAnnotation))

clinvar <- readVcf(book_data("variants", "tp53_clinvar.vcf"), genome = "hg38")
cat(sprintf("%d variant records, %d sample columns\n", nrow(clinvar), ncol(clinvar)))
#> 3888 variant records, 0 sample columns
cat("INFO keys:", paste(colnames(info(clinvar)), collapse = ", "), "\n")
#> INFO keys: AF_TGP, ALLELEID, CLNDN, CLNREVSTAT, CLNSIG, CLNVC, GENEINFO, MC
head(rowRanges(clinvar)[, c("REF", "ALT")], 3)
#> GRanges object with 3 ranges and 2 metadata columns:
#>           seqnames    ranges strand |            REF                ALT
#>              <Rle> <IRanges>  <Rle> | <DNAStringSet> <DNAStringSetList>
#>     35555       17   7668434      * |              T                  G
#>   3051986       17   7668440      * |              T                  C
#>    325619       17   7668449      * |              A                  C
#>   -------
#>   seqinfo: 1 sequence from hg38 genome
```

]
`rowRanges` returns the fixed fields as a `GRanges` object \(Lawrence et al. 2013), so interval operations apply to a VCF without conversion. Note that `ALT` is a list, because a site may carry several alternative alleles; code assuming one alternative per line mis-handles multi-allelic sites, which is why splitting them with `bcftools norm -m -any` before comparing call sets is a standard and much-skipped step. The `INFO` column is a semicolon-separated set of `key=value` pairs defined in the header, describing the #strong[site].

#block[
```
clinical <- sapply(info(clinvar)$CLNSIG, paste, collapse = ",")
review <- sapply(info(clinvar)$CLNREVSTAT, paste, collapse = ",")
consequence <- sapply(info(clinvar)$MC, function(x) sub(".*\\|", "", x[1]))

head(sort(table(clinical), decreasing = TRUE), 6)
#> clinical
#>                       Uncertain_significance 
#>                                          956 
#>                                Likely_benign 
#>                                          761 
#>                                   Pathogenic 
#>                                          743 
#> Conflicting_classifications_of_pathogenicity 
#>                                          532 
#>                         Benign/Likely_benign 
#>                                          273 
#>                 Pathogenic/Likely_pathogenic 
#>                                          211
```

]
The commonest classification in #emph[TP53] is `Uncertain_significance`, which is the most important fact about clinical variant databases: most observed variation in even the most studied gene in cancer has no interpretation. Reporting such a variant as though it were pathogenic is a clinical error, and the ACMG/AMP framework \(Richards et al. 2015) keeps the distinction auditable. The review status field records how much scrutiny each classification received.

#block[
```
star_level <- dplyr::case_when(
  grepl("practice_guideline", review)           ~ "4 stars: practice guideline",
  grepl("reviewed_by_expert_panel", review)     ~ "3 stars: expert panel",
  grepl("no_conflicts", review)                 ~ "2 stars: multiple, no conflict",
  grepl("^criteria_provided", review)           ~ "1 star: criteria provided",
  TRUE                                          ~ "0 stars: no assertion criteria")
print(sort(table(star_level), decreasing = TRUE))
#> star_level
#>      1 star: criteria provided 2 stars: multiple, no conflict 
#>                           2261                           1145 
#> 0 stars: no assertion criteria          3 stars: expert panel 
#>                            291                            191
```

]
A filtering strategy follows from those two fields, and it has the form every filtering strategy should take: a few criteria, each with a stated reason, applied in order.

#block[
```
confident <- data.frame(clinical, star_level, consequence,
                        position = start(rowRanges(clinvar))) |>
  filter(grepl("^Pathogenic|^Likely_pathogenic", clinical),
         !grepl("^0 stars", star_level))

cat(sprintf("pathogenic or likely pathogenic, with assertion criteria: %d of %d\n",
            nrow(confident), nrow(clinvar)))
#> pathogenic or likely pathogenic, with assertion criteria: 1080 of 3888
print(head(sort(table(confident$consequence), decreasing = TRUE), 5))
#> 
#>      frameshift_variant        missense_variant                nonsense 
#>                     470                     247                     113 
#>    splice_donor_variant splice_acceptor_variant 
#>                      73                      69
```

]
Truncating changes dominate --- frameshifts, nonsense variants and splice-site disruptions together are the majority --- with missense variants next. That is what the biology of a tumour suppressor predicts: #emph[TP53] is inactivated both by losing the protein and by substitutions in the DNA-binding domain that leave it intact but unable to bind its response elements, the structural basis for which is the next chapter's subject. Note too that the anchor in `^criteria_provided` matters: the string `no_assertion_criteria_provided` contains `criteria_provided`, and an unanchored match would promote 189 unreviewed records to one star.

INFO describes the site; `FORMAT` describes a sample. The `FORMAT` column is a colon-separated list of keys --- `GT:PL:DP:AD` in the bcftools record above --- and each sample column carries the matching values in the same order: `GT` the genotype (`0/1` heterozygous, `1/1` homozygous alternative, `./.` no call), `DP` the depth at that site in that sample, `AD` the per-allele depth, `PL` the Phred-scaled genotype likelihoods. When call sets are merged, INFO must be recomputed while FORMAT is carried through per sample; tools that concatenate VCFs without understanding this produce files whose INFO describes a different cohort from their genotypes.

Filtering a call set takes one of two approaches. #strong[Hard filtering] applies thresholds to INFO fields --- depth, quality by depth, strand bias, mapping quality --- and suits small call sets and organisms with no truth set. #strong[Variant quality score recalibration] trains a model on known-true variants and is more sensitive, but needs a large call set and a trustworthy truth set, so it is a human-genomics technique. Either way, look at what survives: inspecting the pileup at a few called positions in IGV \(Thorvaldsdottir et al. 2012) catches classes of error no filter expresses.

== RNA-seq from experimental design to differential expression
<sec-09-rnaseq>
Everything so far has been about locating reads. RNA-seq asks how much of each transcript was present, and the answer is a count, which brings its own statistics.

=== Design, replication and confounding
<design-replication-and-confounding>
Design decisions are made before sequencing and cannot be repaired afterwards. #strong[Biological replicates] are independent samples under the same condition; #strong[technical replicates] are one library sequenced repeatedly. Only biological replicates estimate the variability a differential expression test compares against, and technical variability in modern RNA-seq is small enough to be rarely worth buying. Three biological replicates per group is the practical minimum; once depth is adequate, more replicates beat more reads per replicate, because the limiting quantity is the estimate of biological variance.

#strong[Confounding] is the failure analysis cannot fix: if every treated sample was prepared on Monday and every control on Tuesday, batch and condition are one variable and no method can separate them \(Leek et al. 2010). The defence is randomising samples across batches and days, and recording every technical covariate so it can be modelled if it matters. The bundled pasilla dataset is not perfectly designed --- seven samples, three treated with RNAi against #emph[pasilla] and four untreated, sequenced with two library protocols.

#block[
```
counts <- as.matrix(read.delim(book_data("expression", "pasilla_gene_counts.tsv"),
                               row.names = 1))
samples <- read.csv(book_data("expression", "pasilla_sample_annotation.csv"))
samples$sample <- sub("fb$", "", samples$file)
samples <- samples[match(colnames(counts), samples$sample), ]
samples$condition <- factor(samples$condition, levels = c("untreated", "treated"))
samples$type <- factor(sub("-", "_", samples$type))
print(table(samples$condition, samples$type))
#>            
#>             paired_end single_read
#>   untreated          2           2
#>   treated            2           1
cat(sprintf("%d genes x %d samples; library sizes %.1f-%.1f million reads\n",
            nrow(counts), ncol(counts),
            min(colSums(counts)) / 1e6, max(colSums(counts)) / 1e6))
#> 14599 genes x 7 samples; library sizes 8.4-21.9 million reads
```

]
Library type is partially confounded with condition --- two of three treated samples are paired-end against two of four untreated --- but not completely, so the protocol effect can be estimated and removed rather than merely worried about. That is what putting `type` in the design formula does.

=== Counting, and what a count is
<counting-and-what-a-count-is>
Counting assigns each alignment to a gene. featureCounts \(Liao et al. 2013) does this against a GFF3 or GTF annotation, and needs three deliberate decisions: which feature type to count over (`exon`, summarised to `gene_id`), what to do with a read overlapping two genes (discard it, in the standard union model), and whether the library is stranded. Lightweight quantifiers such as salmon \(Patro et al. 2017) skip genome alignment altogether, assigning reads to transcripts probabilistically against a transcriptome index, which is faster and handles reads shared between isoforms properly.

Three properties of the resulting integer matrix drive everything that follows. Counts are discrete, so a continuous model misbehaves at low values. Their variance grows strongly with their mean, so an analysis assuming constant variance is dominated by the most highly expressed genes. And they are #strong[compositional]: library size is fixed, so when one transcript takes a larger share every other count falls although nothing happened.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
TPM and FPKM divide counts by transcript length and library size to make values comparable across genes within a sample. They answer "is this gene expressed?" and are the wrong input for differential expression, because the division destroys the count nature of the data and with it the information about how precisely each value was measured. DESeq2 \(Love et al. 2014) and edgeR \(Robinson et al. 2009) require raw counts and handle library size internally. If a collaborator sends TPMs and asks for a differential expression analysis, ask for the counts.

]
, 
