=== Building the dataset and normalising it
<building-the-dataset-and-normalising-it>
#block[
```
suppressPackageStartupMessages(library(DESeq2))

dds <- DESeqDataSetFromMatrix(countData = counts, colData = samples,
                              design = ~ type + condition)
keep <- rowSums(counts(dds) >= 10) >= 3
dds <- dds[keep, ]
cat(sprintf("%d genes retained of %d after requiring 10+ reads in 3+ samples\n",
            nrow(dds), length(keep)))
#> 8148 genes retained of 14599 after requiring 10+ reads in 3+ samples
```

]
Pre-filtering is not a statistical step --- DESeq2's independent filtering would handle these genes anyway --- but it speeds everything up. In the formula `~ type + condition` the variable of interest goes #strong[last], the convention `results()` follows when no contrast is named.

Normalisation corrects for libraries sequenced to different depths and compositions. Dividing by total count is the obvious approach and the wrong one, because a few very highly expressed genes take a large share of a library and drag every other gene down. DESeq2 uses #strong[median-of-ratios] instead, which is simple enough to compute by hand.

#block[
```
log_counts <- log(counts(dds))
reference <- rowMeans(log_counts)                     # geometric mean across samples
usable <- is.finite(reference)                        # drop genes with any zero
ratios <- log_counts[usable, ] - reference[usable]
manual_size_factors <- exp(apply(ratios, 2, median))

dds <- estimateSizeFactors(dds)
comparison <- rbind(manual = manual_size_factors, DESeq2 = sizeFactors(dds))
print(round(comparison, 4))
#>        untreated1 untreated2 untreated3 untreated4 treated1 treated2
#> manual      1.144      1.791      0.646     0.7507     1.63   0.7612
#> DESeq2      1.144      1.791      0.646     0.7507     1.63   0.7612
#>        treated3
#> manual   0.8303
#> DESeq2   0.8303
```

]
The two rows agree. In words: for each gene take the ratio of its count in a sample to its geometric mean across samples, then for each sample take the median of those ratios. If a sample differed from the average only in depth, every ratio would be the same number --- the depth factor. The median makes the estimate robust: genes that genuinely changed shift their own ratios, but as long as #strong[most genes are not differentially expressed] the median is unaffected. That assumption fails in a global transcriptional shutdown, where spike-in controls become necessary.

=== Dispersion, and why it must be shrunk
<dispersion-and-why-it-must-be-shrunk>
Counts from replicate samples vary more than a Poisson process allows. The extra variability --- between individuals, cages, cultures --- is captured by a #strong[negative binomial] model with a per-gene #strong[dispersion] $alpha$, giving variance $mu + alpha mu^2$. With three or four replicates the per-gene estimate of $alpha$ is very noisy, and a gene whose replicates happen to agree closely gets a dispersion near zero, which would make every difference look significant. DESeq2 therefore borrows strength across genes: it fits a smooth trend of dispersion against mean expression, then pulls each gene's noisy estimate towards that trend by an amount reflecting how uncertain the individual estimate is. #ref(<fig-09-dispersion>, supplement: [Figure]) shows all three quantities.

```
dds <- estimateDispersions(dds)
plotDispEsts(dds, main = "Dispersion before and after shrinkage")
```

#figure([
#box(image("part5/09-ngs-data-analysis_files/figure-typst/fig-09-dispersion-1.png", width: 90.0%))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Dispersion estimates for the pasilla dataset. Black points are the per-gene maximum-likelihood estimates, the red line is the fitted mean--dispersion trend, and blue points are the final shrunken values used in testing. Points circled in blue are dispersion outliers, which are left unshrunken so that a genuinely variable gene is not forced to look reproducible.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-09-dispersion>


Two features carry the message. Dispersion falls with mean expression, which is why one variance assumption for all genes would be wrong. And the blue points sit much closer to the red line than the black ones: each gene's variance estimate is now informed by the thousands of genes expressed at a similar level. The exception is deliberate --- genes whose own estimate is far above the trend keep it, because shrinking those would manufacture false positives.

=== Testing, and what the adjusted #emph[p]-value promises
<testing-and-what-the-adjusted-p-value-promises>
#block[
```
dds <- nbinomWaldTest(dds)
res <- results(dds, contrast = c("condition", "treated", "untreated"), alpha = 0.05)
summary(res)
#> 
#> out of 8148 with nonzero total read count
#> adjusted p-value < 0.05
#> LFC > 0 (up)       : 508, 6.2%
#> LFC < 0 (down)     : 600, 7.4%
#> outliers [1]       : 0, 0%
#> low counts [2]     : 0, 0%
#> (mean count < 5)
#> [1] see 'cooksCutoff' argument of ?results
#> [2] see 'independentFiltering' argument of ?results
```

]
The Wald test divides the estimated log2 fold change by its standard error and refers the result to a normal distribution. The estimate is itself shrunk --- a two-fold change supported by four reads is reported as a smaller change than the same ratio supported by four thousand --- so fold changes for weakly expressed genes are conservative by design.

Testing 8,148 genes at $p < 0.05$ would yield around 407 significant results if nothing were differentially expressed at all. The Benjamini--Hochberg procedure \(Benjamini and Hochberg 1995) controls the #strong[false discovery rate] by adjusting the #emph[p]-values so that, among genes called significant at `padj < 0.05`, the expected proportion of false positives is 5%.

#block[
```
cat(sprintf("raw p < 0.05            : %d genes\n", sum(res$pvalue < 0.05, na.rm = TRUE)))
#> raw p < 0.05            : 1928 genes
cat(sprintf("expected by chance alone: %.0f genes\n", 0.05 * nrow(res)))
#> expected by chance alone: 407 genes
cat(sprintf("BH-adjusted padj < 0.05 : %d genes\n", sum(res$padj < 0.05, na.rm = TRUE)))
#> BH-adjusted padj < 0.05 : 1108 genes
cat(sprintf("  of those, |log2FC| > 1: %d genes\n",
            sum(res$padj < 0.05 & abs(res$log2FoldChange) > 1, na.rm = TRUE)))
#>   of those, |log2FC| > 1: 224 genes
```

]
#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
It promises that #emph[the list as a whole] is expected to contain 5% false positives. It says nothing about any individual gene: one with `padj = 0.049` is not "95% likely to be real". It is not a statement about importance --- a gene can be highly significant and change by 10%, because significance measures evidence against the null, not effect size. It does not survive changing the gene set, since adding or removing genes changes every adjusted #emph[p]-value, which is why filtering must be decided before testing and never after seeing the results. And it assumes the #emph[p]-values were valid, which they are not if the design was confounded. Choosing a list by `padj` #emph[and] a fold-change threshold is a pragmatic compromise, but the threshold is a judgement and should be stated as one.

]
, 
