== From sequence to fold
<sec-10-levels>
The #strong[primary structure] is the covalent sequence of amino acids, written from the free amino terminus to the free carboxyl terminus. It is the only level encoded directly in the gene, and in principle it determines all the others.

#strong[Secondary structure] is local, regular backbone geometry stabilised by hydrogen bonds between backbone amide and carbonyl groups. The α-helix has 3.6 residues per turn and a hydrogen bond from the carbonyl of residue #emph[i] to the amide of residue #emph[i] + 4; the β-strand is extended, and strands hydrogen-bond to each other side by side to form parallel or antiparallel sheets. Turns and loops connect them. Because secondary structure is defined by hydrogen-bond patterns rather than by sequence, it is assigned from coordinates, and the standard assignment algorithm is DSSP \(Kabsch and Sander 1983).

#strong[Tertiary structure] is the three-dimensional arrangement of the whole chain, and the forces holding it together are weak individually and decisive in aggregate. The hydrophobic effect dominates: burying non-polar side chains away from water increases the entropy of the solvent, and this is what drives collapse into a compact globule with a non-polar core. Hydrogen bonds and salt bridges give specificity, selecting one fold among the many compact arrangements available. Disulphide bonds between cysteines are the only covalent cross-links, and are largely confined to secreted and extracellular proteins because the cytoplasm is reducing. Metal coordination can be structural in the same way, as it is in p53: #ref(<sec-10-measuring>, supplement: [Section]) measures the zinc site that holds its DNA-binding surface in shape.

#strong[Quaternary structure] is the assembly of several chains into a functional complex. Full-length p53 works as a tetramer, and 1TUP contains three copies of its DNA-binding domain in the crystal's asymmetric unit --- which, as #ref(<sec-10-superposition>, supplement: [Section]) shows, is not the same statement as "p53 is a trimer".

== How structures are determined, and how to judge one
<sec-10-quality>
#strong[X-ray crystallography] measures the diffraction pattern of a crystal and reconstructs an electron density map, into which a model is built and refined. It remains the source of most structures and of nearly all high-resolution ones. #strong[Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR)] spectroscopy measures distance and angle restraints on a protein in solution and reports an ensemble of models consistent with them, which is why an NMR entry contains many models rather than one. #strong[Cryo-electron microscopy] images many copies of a particle frozen in vitreous ice and averages them computationally; it has become the method of choice for large assemblies and for anything that will not crystallise.

What matters for an analyst is not the method but the quality metrics each one reports, because a structure is a model fitted to data and models can be fitted badly. For a crystal structure, four numbers and one list carry most of the information.

#strong[Resolution], in ångströms, states the finest spacing of diffraction data used. At 1.5 Å individual atoms are separable and water molecules are placed confidently; at 2.5 Å side-chain positions are inferred as much as observed; beyond 3.5 Å only the backbone fold and the largest side chains are reliable. Smaller is better, which inverts the usual reading of a number.

#strong[R factor] measures the disagreement between the diffraction amplitudes the model predicts and those observed. #strong[R-free] is the same quantity computed on a few per cent of reflections deliberately withheld from refinement, and it is the trustworthy one: the working R can always be driven down by adding parameters, whereas R-free rises when a model is over-fitted. A modern structure at 2 Å should have R-free near 0.20--0.25, and an R-free more than about 0.07 above the working R is a warning sign.

#block[
```
def crystallographic_metadata(path):
    """Pull the fields a reader needs to judge a crystal structure from its header."""
    wanted = {"EXPDTA": None, "RESOLUTION": None, "R VALUE": None, "FREE R VALUE": None}
    for line in open(path):
        if line.startswith("EXPDTA"):
            wanted["EXPDTA"] = line[10:].strip()
        elif line.startswith("REMARK   2 RESOLUTION"):
            wanted["RESOLUTION"] = line[23:].strip()
        elif line.startswith("REMARK   3   R VALUE            (WORKING SET)"):
            wanted["R VALUE"] = line.split(":")[-1].strip()
        elif line.startswith("REMARK   3   FREE R VALUE  ") and wanted["FREE R VALUE"] is None:
            wanted["FREE R VALUE"] = line.split(":")[-1].strip()
    return wanted

for name in ("1tup.pdb", "1mbn.pdb"):
    print(name, crystallographic_metadata(book_data("structures", name)))
#> 1tup.pdb {'EXPDTA': 'X-RAY DIFFRACTION', 'RESOLUTION': '2.20 ANGSTROMS.', 'R VALUE': '0.202', 'FREE R VALUE': 'NULL'}
#> 1mbn.pdb {'EXPDTA': 'X-RAY DIFFRACTION', 'RESOLUTION': '2.00 ANGSTROMS.', 'R VALUE': 'NULL', 'FREE R VALUE': 'NULL'}
```

]
Both entries are X-ray structures, 1TUP at 2.20 Å and 1MBN at 2.00 Å, and both illustrate that the metadata you want is sometimes simply absent. 1TUP reports a working R of 0.202 and no R-free at all, because it was deposited in 1995, when cross-validation was new. 1MBN, deposited in 1973, reports no R factor whatsoever. Neither omission makes the structures wrong, and 1MBN is one of the most important models ever built; but it does mean that a claim resting on a precise atomic position in either file cannot be checked in the usual way. Date of deposition is part of a structure's quality assessment.

#strong[B factors] --- the temperature or displacement factors --- record how much each atom's position is smeared out, by thermal motion or by disorder between copies in the crystal. They are per-atom, they are in Å², and they are the most under-used field in the file.

#strong[Missing residues] are the fifth thing to check, and they are recorded in `REMARK 465` rather than in the coordinates, so code that only reads `ATOM` records will never see them. A residue absent from the model was not absent from the protein: it was mobile enough that no density could be assigned to it.

#block[
```
def missing_residues(path):
    """Residues listed in REMARK 465 as present in the protein but not in the model."""
    missing, in_table = [], False
    for line in open(path):
        if line.startswith("REMARK 465   M RES C SSSEQI"):
            in_table = True
        elif in_table and line.startswith("REMARK 465"):
            fields = line[15:].split()
            if len(fields) >= 3:
                missing.append((fields[1], int(fields[2])))
    return missing

gaps = missing_residues(book_data("structures", "1tup.pdb"))
print(f"{len(gaps)} residues modelled nowhere in 1TUP")
#> 72 residues modelled nowhere in 1TUP
for chain in "ABC":
    numbers = sorted(n for c, n in gaps if c == chain)
    print(f"  chain {chain}: {len(numbers):2d} missing; "
          f"N-terminal {[n for n in numbers if n < 150]}, "
          f"C-terminal {numbers[-1] - len([n for n in numbers if n >= 150]) + 1}"
          f"-{numbers[-1]}")
#>   chain A: 23 missing; N-terminal [], C-terminal 290-312
#>   chain B: 25 missing; N-terminal [94, 95], C-terminal 290-312
#>   chain C: 24 missing; N-terminal [94], C-terminal 290-312
```

]
Seventy-two residues are missing across the three protein chains, all of them at the chain termini: the construct extends beyond the ordered domain and those tails are disordered in the crystal. #ref(<sec-10-prediction>, supplement: [Section]) returns to this, because the AlphaFold model of the same protein assigns exactly those regions its lowest confidence, which is not a coincidence.

