<fig-10-contact>


The map is almost entirely off-diagonal bands running at right angles to the main diagonal. Each such band is a pair of antiparallel β-strands: residue #emph[i] contacts residue #emph[j], residue #emph[i] + 1 contacts #emph[j] − 1, and so on. A parallel sheet would give bands running parallel to the diagonal instead, and an α-helical protein gives a thick diagonal with little else. The DNA-contacting positions marked in red are scattered across the sequence and adjacent in space, which is the general lesson of a contact map: a binding site is a property of the fold, not of a stretch of sequence.

Secondary-structure content can be estimated from the backbone dihedral angles φ and ψ, which is what #ref(<fig-10-ramachandran>, supplement: [Figure]) plots. This is an approximation to what DSSP computes from hydrogen bonds, and it is reported here as one.

```
import math

def dihedrals(chain):
    """(phi, psi) in degrees for every residue with both angles defined."""
    angles = []
    for peptide in PPBuilder().build_peptides(chain):
        for phi, psi in peptide.get_phi_psi_list():
            if phi is not None and psi is not None:
                angles.append((math.degrees(phi), math.degrees(psi)))
    return np.array(angles)

myoglobin = parser.get_structure("1mbn", book_data("structures", "1mbn.pdb"))[0]
p53_angles = dihedrals(model["B"])
mb_angles = dihedrals(myoglobin["A"])

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5, 4.6))
ax.scatter(*p53_angles.T, s=9, alpha=0.7, color=PALETTE[0], label="p53 DBD (1TUP B)")
ax.scatter(*mb_angles.T, s=9, alpha=0.7, color=PALETTE[2], marker="^",
           label="myoglobin (1MBN A)")
ax.axhline(0, color="grey", linewidth=0.5)
ax.axvline(0, color="grey", linewidth=0.5)
ax.set_xlim(-180, 180); ax.set_ylim(-180, 180)
#> (-180.0, 180.0)
#> (-180.0, 180.0)
ax.set_xlabel("phi (degrees)"); ax.set_ylabel("psi (degrees)")
ax.set_title("Backbone conformation")
ax.legend(loc="upper right", fontsize=8)
fig.tight_layout()
```

