== Further reading
<sec-10-further-reading>
- Berman (2000) and Burley et al. (2022) describe the Protein Data Bank at its founding and as it now operates; read the second for what the archive validates and what it does not.
- Kabsch and Sander (1983) is the DSSP paper, and the definition of secondary structure that most software still implements.
- Jumper et al. (2021) is the AlphaFold paper; its supplementary material is where pLDDT and predicted aligned error are actually defined.
- Varadi et al. (2021) describes the AlphaFold database and is the right citation when you use a downloaded model, together with the UniProt accession and model version.
- Baek et al. (2021) solves the same problem by a different route, and reading it alongside Jumper et al. (2021) separates what is essential to the approach from what is particular to one implementation.
- Trott and Olson (2009) introduces AutoDock Vina and is candid about what its scoring function can and cannot do; read it before interpreting any docking score.
- Cho et al. (1994) is the structure used throughout this chapter, and its discussion of why particular residues are cancer hotspots is a model of reading biology out of coordinates.
- Pettersen et al. (2020) describes ChimeraX, which is the practical tool for looking at everything computed here.

#part[Systems biology and data science]
= Biological networks and pathway analysis
<biological-networks-and-pathway-analysis>
#block[
#strong[After working through this chapter you will be able to]

- distinguish protein--protein interaction, regulatory, metabolic and co-expression networks by the evidence that supports an edge in each
- compute degree, betweenness, clustering coefficient, shortest paths and components on a real interaction network with igraph
- judge what a degree distribution does and does not establish about network architecture
- detect modules with two community-detection algorithms and compare their answers honestly
- draw a network so that it can be read, and recognise when a drawing has stopped carrying information
- implement over-representation analysis with the hypergeometric test and control the false discovery rate
- explain why a significant enrichment term is a hypothesis rather than a finding

]
A differential expression analysis ends with a list: four hundred genes, each with a fold change and an adjusted #emph[p]-value computed by a tool such as DESeq2 \(Love et al. 2014). The list is a measurement, not an explanation. Nothing in it says which of those genes act together, which are upstream of which, or whether the four hundred represent one disturbed process or nine unrelated ones. The work of this chapter is to take that kind of list and place it in a structure --- a network, a pathway, an annotation hierarchy --- that can support an interpretation, while being candid about how much of that structure is measurement and how much is inference from the literature.

Two bundled datasets carry the chapter. The first, `data/networks/p53_string_network.tsv`, is a high-confidence slice of the STRING database \(Szklarczyk et al. 2022) covering 30 human cell-cycle and DNA-damage genes, with each interaction broken down into the separate evidence channels that produced it. The second, `data/annotation/human_go_annotations.tsv`, is a UniProtKB export \(Bateman et al. 2022) of 20,431 reviewed human proteins and their Gene Ontology terms, which serves as the background universe for enrichment testing.

By the end you will have measured the topology of a real network, partitioned it into modules with two algorithms that disagree, written an over-representation test from first principles rather than calling a package, corrected it for multiple testing, implemented the running-sum statistic that underlies gene set enrichment analysis, and then taken the resulting table apart to see which of its 511 significant terms survive contact with a more careful background choice.

== Setting up
