== Finding modules
<sec-11-communities>
A #strong[community] or #strong[module] is a group of nodes with more edges inside the group than to the rest of the network. #strong[Modularity], written #emph[Q], scores a partition by comparing its within-group edge count to the count expected if edges were rewired at random; #emph[Q] above roughly 0.3 is usually taken as meaningful structure. Community detection algorithms search for partitions with high #emph[Q].

Two popular algorithms take different routes. The Louvain method \(Blondel et al. 2008) moves nodes between communities greedily and then collapses communities into nodes, repeating until #emph[Q] stops improving. Fast greedy modularity optimisation instead merges the pair of communities that most increases #emph[Q], building a dendrogram and cutting it at the maximum. Running both on the same graph is the minimum honest practice.

#block[
```
set.seed(20250101)
louvain  <- cluster_louvain(p53_net)
greedy   <- cluster_fast_greedy(p53_net)
walktrap <- cluster_walktrap(p53_net)

partitions <- list(Louvain = louvain, FastGreedy = greedy, Walktrap = walktrap)
for (method in names(partitions)) {
  result <- partitions[[method]]
  cat(method, ": k =", length(result),
      " Q =", round(modularity(result), 3),
      " sizes =", paste(sizes(result), collapse = ","), "\n")
}
#> Louvain : k = 3  Q = 0.137  sizes = 11,13,6 
#> FastGreedy : k = 3  Q = 0.135  sizes = 12,7,11 
#> Walktrap : k = 4  Q = 0.109  sizes = 15,4,10,1
```

]
Louvain finds three communities with #emph[Q] = 0.137; fast greedy also finds three, with #emph[Q] = 0.135; walktrap finds four with #emph[Q] = 0.109. All three values are far below 0.3. The correct conclusion is that this network has no strong modular structure --- which is what you should expect from a dense, hand-picked set of genes that all participate in one broad process. The algorithms still return a partition, because they always do. An algorithm that cannot say "no structure here" will never say it.

How much do the two best partitions agree? The adjusted Rand index compares two clusterings, scoring 1 for identical partitions and about 0 for the agreement expected by chance.

#block[
```
cat("adjusted Rand index, Louvain vs fast greedy:",
    round(compare(louvain, greedy, method = "adjusted.rand"), 3), "\n")
#> adjusted Rand index, Louvain vs fast greedy: 0.7
table(Louvain = membership(louvain), FastGreedy = membership(greedy))
#>        FastGreedy
#> Louvain  1  2  3
#>       1 10  1  0
#>       2  2  0 11
#>       3  0  6  0
```

]
An adjusted Rand index of 0.700 means substantial but incomplete agreement: the cross-tabulation shows one 10-gene core assigned together by both methods, an 11-gene group likewise, and three genes that move. Those three genes are the interesting ones --- they are the genes whose module membership is an artefact of the algorithm, and no biological story should be built on them.

A third algorithm illustrates how badly the choice can go. The Girvan--Newman method \(Girvan and Newman 2002), available as `cluster_edge_betweenness()`, removes the highest-betweenness edge repeatedly and is elegant but quadratic in the number of edges. On this dense graph it returns 20 communities, 19 of them singletons, with #emph[Q] = 0.009. Dense graphs defeat it, and the method suits sparse networks with genuine bottlenecks instead.

== Drawing a network that can be read
<sec-11-visualisation>
Network figures fail in a characteristic way. Past a few hundred edges, a force-directed layout produces an undifferentiated mass --- a "hairball" --- in which every node is adjacent to a dark region and nothing can be traced. The failure is not aesthetic. A hairball transmits no information, so a figure that is one is a figure that should have been a table.

#ref(<fig-11-network>, supplement: [Figure]) makes the comparison directly. The left panel places the same 30 nodes at random; the right panel uses the Fruchterman--Reingold force-directed layout, sizes nodes by degree and colours them by Louvain community.

```
module_colour <- book_palette[membership(louvain)]

par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(0.5, 0.5, 2, 0.5))

set.seed(20250101)
plot(p53_net, layout = layout_randomly, vertex.label = NA,
     vertex.size = 6, vertex.color = "grey70",
     edge.color = "grey75", main = "Random layout")

set.seed(20250101)
plot(p53_net, layout = layout_with_fr,
     vertex.size = 4 + 0.6 * node_degree,
     vertex.color = module_colour, vertex.frame.color = "white",
     vertex.label.cex = 0.6, vertex.label.color = "black",
     vertex.label.family = "sans",
     edge.color = adjustcolor("grey60", alpha.f = 0.5),
     main = "Force-directed, sized and coloured")
```

#figure([
#box(image("part6/11-systems-biology-and-network-analysis_files/figure-typst/fig-11-network-1.png", width: 90.0%))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
The same 30-gene STRING subnetwork drawn two ways. Left: a random layout, in which edge density alone prevents any structure from being read. Right: a Fruchterman-Reingold layout with node area proportional to degree and colour by Louvain community; TP53 sits at the centre because it is adjacent to every other node. Even the right-hand panel is close to the density at which force-directed drawings stop being informative.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-11-network>


The right-hand panel is legible only because there are 30 nodes. Three habits keep larger networks readable. First, filter before drawing: raise the confidence threshold, keep the largest component, or show only the subgraph induced by the genes you will discuss. Second, encode at most two variables visually --- size for one quantity, colour for another --- and put everything else in a table. Third, label selectively; a label on every node is a label on none. When filtering cannot rescue the figure, present the topology as numbers and show only the subnetwork around the genes under discussion.

== Pathway resources and over-representation analysis
<sec-11-ora>
Three resources dominate functional interpretation. The #strong[Gene Ontology (GO)] is a structured vocabulary of terms in three namespaces --- biological process, molecular function and cellular component --- arranged as a directed acyclic graph in which a term implies all its ancestors \(Ashburner et al. 2000; Aleksander et al. 2023). #strong[KEGG] curates metabolic and signalling pathway maps with defined reactions and topology \(Kanehisa 2000). #strong[Reactome] curates reactions at finer granularity, with explicit inputs, outputs, catalysts and literature evidence for each step \(Milacic et al. 2023). GO annotates genes with terms; KEGG and Reactome place gene products inside a described mechanism. GO has the widest coverage, Reactome the most explicit evidence trail.

#strong[Over-representation analysis (ORA)] asks a single question: given a background universe of genes, a gene set defined by an annotation term, and a query list, are more query genes annotated to that term than you would expect by chance? The exact test is the hypergeometric distribution --- the distribution of the number of white balls drawn when sampling #emph[n] balls without replacement from an urn holding #emph[K] white and #emph[N] − #emph[K] black. Here #emph[N] is the universe, #emph[K] the term's size, #emph[n] the query list size and #emph[k] the observed overlap.

The annotation table must first be reshaped from one row per protein to one row per gene--term pair --- the form every enrichment tool uses internally. The `separate_longer_delim()` call in the previous section did exactly this, and the tidyverse idiom generalises to any semicolon-delimited annotation column \(Wickham et al. 2019).

#block[
```
universe <- unique(gene_to_go$gene)
term_sizes <- count(gene_to_go, go, name = "K")

cat("universe:", length(universe), "genes;",
    nrow(term_sizes), "distinct GO terms;",
    nrow(gene_to_go), "gene-term pairs\n")
#> universe: 19248 genes; 17553 distinct GO terms; 247239 gene-term pairs
```

]
The query list is the 30 network genes, all of which map to the universe. The test itself is one call to `phyper()`. Note the `k - 1` and `lower.tail = FALSE`: you want P(X ≥ k), and `phyper(q, ...)` with the upper tail returns P(X \> q).

#block[
```
query_genes <- V(p53_net)$name
n_query <- length(query_genes)
N <- length(universe)

enrichment <- gene_to_go |>
  filter(gene %in% query_genes) |>
  count(go, name = "k") |>
  inner_join(term_sizes, by = "go") |>
  filter(K >= 5, K <= 2000) |>          # drop terms too small or too vague to test
  mutate(expected = round(n_query * K / N, 2),
         p_value  = phyper(k - 1, K, N - K, n_query, lower.tail = FALSE),
         p_adj    = p.adjust(p_value, method = "BH")) |>
  arrange(p_value)

cat("terms tested:", nrow(enrichment),
    " significant at BH < 0.05:", sum(enrichment$p_adj < 0.05), "\n")
#> terms tested: 830  significant at BH < 0.05: 511
head(enrichment, 6)
#> # A tibble: 6 x 6
#>   go             k     K expected  p_value    p_adj
#>   <chr>      <int> <int>    <dbl>    <dbl>    <dbl>
#> 1 GO:0042802    21  1744     2.72 7.23e-16 6.00e-13
#> 2 GO:0006974    12   303     0.47 1.25e-14 5.19e-12
#> 3 GO:0008630     7    39     0.06 1.56e-13 4.32e-11
#> 4 GO:0032991    13   641     1    3.91e-12 8.11e-10
#> 5 GO:0090399     5    13     0.02 8.26e-12 1.37e- 9
#> 6 GO:0051726     9   195     0.3  1.11e-11 1.54e- 9
```

]
That is over-representation analysis in its entirety. A package such as clusterProfiler \(Yu et al. 2012) adds term-name lookup, ontology-aware filtering, plotting and support for many organisms, but the statistic underneath is the call you just made. Knowing this matters when a tool and your own calculation disagree: the difference will be in the universe, the term-size filter or the ontology propagation, never in the distribution.

Two filtering decisions were made above and both are conventional rather than principled. Terms with fewer than five annotated genes produce unstable estimates and dominate the tail of small #emph[p]-values for trivial reasons. Terms with more than 2,000 genes --- "protein binding", "cytoplasm" --- are too vague to interpret even when significant. State both bounds in your methods; they change the answer.

=== Correcting for multiple testing
<correcting-for-multiple-testing>
Testing 830 terms at a 5% threshold would be expected to yield about 42 false positives by chance alone. The #strong[Bonferroni] correction divides the threshold by the number of tests, controlling the probability of even one false positive but discarding real signal. The #strong[Benjamini--Hochberg] procedure instead controls the #strong[false discovery rate (FDR)]: the expected proportion of false positives among the terms you call significant \(Benjamini and Hochberg 1995). For exploratory enrichment, where the output is a list of hypotheses rather than a single decision, FDR control is the right trade-off; `p.adjust(method = "BH")` applies it.

#block[
```
cat("raw p < 0.05:        ", sum(enrichment$p_value < 0.05), "\n")
#> raw p < 0.05:         617
cat("Benjamini-Hochberg:  ", sum(enrichment$p_adj < 0.05), "\n")
#> Benjamini-Hochberg:   511
cat("Bonferroni:          ",
    sum(p.adjust(enrichment$p_value, "bonferroni") < 0.05), "\n")
#> Bonferroni:           82
```

]
617 terms pass uncorrected, 511 survive FDR control and 82 survive Bonferroni. The gap between 511 and 82 is the price of the two error rates, and the fact that 511 of 830 tested terms are "significant" is itself a warning that the chapter returns to below. An alternative to BH is Storey's #emph[q]-value, which estimates the proportion of true null hypotheses rather than assuming it is one, and typically calls more terms significant \(Storey and Tibshirani 2003).

