Two differences from ORA matter. GSEA uses every gene, so a set whose members are all modestly shifted in the same direction can be significant even though no single member passes a differential expression threshold --- a pattern ORA cannot see. And the null distribution is built by permutation rather than from a closed form, which means the result depends on what you permute. Shuffling gene labels, as above, asks whether this set is more concentrated at the top than a random set of the same size --- a #emph[competitive] null. Shuffling sample labels and recomputing the statistic asks whether the set responds to the treatment --- a #emph[self-contained] null, and the one GSEA uses when sample numbers allow. The two answer different questions and frequently disagree.

