== Further reading
<sec-11-further-reading>
- Szklarczyk et al. (2022) documents STRING's evidence channels and scoring scheme; read it before using a combined score as though it were a single kind of evidence.
- Aleksander et al. (2023) is the current description of the Gene Ontology knowledgebase, including how annotations are assigned, evidence-coded and propagated.
- Milacic et al. (2023) describes Reactome's curation model, which is the clearest published account of what a curated pathway database does and does not contain.
- Subramanian et al. (2005) introduced gene set enrichment analysis; the methods section explains the weighting and permutation choices that the simplified implementation above leaves out.
- Benjamini and Hochberg (1995) is the original false discovery rate paper and remains the shortest route to understanding what an adjusted #emph[p]-value means.
- Yu et al. (2012) describes clusterProfiler, the package you will use in practice once you understand the hypergeometric test underneath it.
- Kanehisa (2000) introduces KEGG and its pathway map abstraction, which differs from both GO's vocabulary and Reactome's reaction-level curation.
- Huber et al. (2015) surveys the Bioconductor ecosystem in which most R-based enrichment and network workflows are assembled.
- Robertson et al. (2017) is a large tumour characterisation study whose pathway-level figures are worth reading critically against the cautions in this chapter.

= Statistics and machine learning on omics data
<statistics-and-machine-learning-on-omics-data>
#block[
#strong[After working through this chapter you will be able to]

- distinguish technical from biological replication and say which one licences a claim about a population
- normalise a count matrix and demonstrate why raw counts cannot be compared across libraries
- diagnose a batch effect, and quantify how much of a confounded comparison it explains
- interpret a principal component analysis, including variance explained and loadings
- choose and defend a number of clusters, and test whether a partition is stable
- build a cross-validated classifier in scikit-learn without leaking information between folds
- evaluate a model with precision, recall and the curves that remain informative under class imbalance

]
A count matrix is not a result. Between the matrix and a defensible statement about biology sit a dozen decisions --- which genes to keep, how to make libraries comparable, what to do about the fact that samples were prepared in two different ways, how to correct thousands of simultaneous tests, whether an apparent cluster is real, and whether a classifier that reports 100% accuracy has learned anything at all. Each decision can turn a real effect into a null result or, more often, turn noise into a publishable figure.

This chapter works through those decisions on the bundled #emph[Drosophila] RNA-seq dataset in `data/expression/pasilla_gene_counts.tsv`: 14,599 genes measured in seven samples, three in which the gene #emph[pasilla] was knocked down by RNA interference and four untreated controls, with the sample metadata in `data/expression/pasilla_sample_annotation.csv`, from the study that first reported it \(Brooks et al. 2010). The dataset is small enough to hold in your head and real enough to misbehave. In particular, its libraries were built two different ways --- three single-read, four paired-end --- and that difference is almost as large a source of variation as the treatment itself, which makes it an unusually honest teaching set.

The single most valuable result in this chapter is in the section on supervised learning. Selecting informative genes before cross-validation rather than inside it produces a classifier that reports perfect accuracy on data whose labels have been randomly shuffled. You will run that demonstration and see the number.

