== Designing an experiment that can answer the question
<sec-12-design>
No amount of analysis repairs a design that cannot answer the question, and the most common failure is not a shortage of data but the wrong kind of replication.

A #strong[technical replicate] repeats the measurement on the same biological material: the same RNA extract sequenced twice, the same library loaded on two lanes. It estimates the variability of the measurement process. A #strong[biological replicate] repeats the whole experiment on independent material: a different fly culture, a different patient, a different mouse. It estimates the variability of the biological population, which is what almost every scientific claim is about.

Technical replicates cannot substitute for biological ones. Sequencing one RNA sample across twenty lanes gives twenty highly concordant measurements of that one sample, a reassuringly small standard error, and no information whatsoever about whether another fly would have behaved the same way. The resulting #emph[p]-value answers a question nobody asked. The bundled metadata records that some samples were run across five or six lanes; those lanes were summed into one count column, which is the correct treatment, because they were never independent observations of biology.

