== Further reading
<sec-12-further-reading>
- Love et al. (2014) explains the negative binomial model, dispersion shrinkage and size factors behind DESeq2, and is the clearest account of why count data need their own methods.
- Leek et al. (2010) is a short, blunt treatment of batch effects with real examples of published conclusions that turned out to be processing artefacts.
- Benjamini and Hochberg (1995) remains the shortest route to understanding what an adjusted #emph[p]-value does and does not promise.
- Pedregosa et al. (2011) documents scikit-learn; the user guide sections on pipelines and model selection are the practical complement to this chapter.
- Libbrecht and Noble (2015) surveys machine learning in genomics with unusual attention to what goes wrong and how to evaluate claims.
- Maaten and Hinton (2008) introduced t-SNE; reading the original makes clear how much of what people infer from these plots the method never promised.
- Zou et al. (2018) is a compact primer on deep learning for biologists, and is honest about where simpler models remain competitive.
- Wickham et al. (2019) sets out the tidy data principles that make the reshaping steps in this chapter routine rather than ad hoc.

#part[Applications and responsible practice]
= Bioinformatics in disease and healthcare
<bioinformatics-in-disease-and-healthcare>
#block[
#strong[After working through this chapter you will be able to]

- distinguish somatic from germline variation and driver from passenger mutations, and state the statistical logic that separates them
- explain what a mutational signature is and what kind of claim it supports
- parse a clinical variant file and summarise its clinical significance, consequence and review-status structure
- map a genomic variant to a codon and a protein domain, and test whether pathogenic variants concentrate there
- apply the ACMG/AMP evidence codes to a real variant and justify the resulting classification
- distinguish a pathogenic variant from an actionable one, and describe the pharmacogenomic evidence frameworks
- outline a pathogen genomic surveillance workflow and the constraints it meets in practice

]
Clinical genomics asks a question that research genomics can avoid: what should be done about this person. The analytical steps look familiar --- align, call variants, annotate, filter --- but the standard of evidence is different, the error modes have consequences for patients, and the output is a classification that somebody will act on. A variant list is not a diagnosis, a significant gene is not a drug target, and a high-scoring prediction is not a clinical finding.

This chapter works through the main settings in which genomic data meets medicine: cancer genomics, rare disease diagnosis, clinical variant interpretation, pharmacogenomics and pathogen surveillance. The computational work uses the bundled ClinVar slice for #emph[TP53] (`data/variants/tp53_clinvar.vcf`, 3,888 records on GRCh38), the UniProt record for human p53 (`data/proteins/P04637.txt`), and the RefSeq transcript `data/sequences/TP53-NM_000546.6.gb`. From those three files you will build a variant table, translate genomic coordinates into codons and amino acid changes, show that pathogenic missense variants concentrate in the DNA-binding domain, and reconstruct the evidence for a real clinical classification.

#emph[TP53] is the right gene for this. It is the most frequently mutated gene in human cancer, it causes a dominantly inherited cancer predisposition syndrome when disrupted in the germline, and its ClinVar entry is unusually well curated, including variants assessed by an expert panel. It is also the gene whose interaction network and structure appear elsewhere in this book, so the same protein is seen from four directions.

