== Drivers, passengers and the resources that find them
<sec-13-drivers>
A tumour genome carries thousands of somatic mutations, and almost all of them are irrelevant. A #strong[driver] mutation confers a selective advantage on the cell that carries it; a #strong[passenger] is carried along because it happened to be in a cell that acquired a driver. Both are real mutations. Only drivers are mechanistically interesting, and only some drivers are therapeutically relevant.

The statistical logic of driver identification is a comparison against a background mutation rate. If mutations accumulate at a rate determined by replication errors and exposure, then the expected number of mutations in a gene depends on its length, its base composition, its replication timing and the local mutational processes. A gene that carries far more mutations than that expectation across a cohort has been under positive selection. Three refinements make this work in practice.

#strong[The background rate varies enormously.] Mutation rate differs by orders of magnitude across the genome with replication timing and chromatin state, and across patients with exposure and repair capacity. A method that assumes a uniform rate reports long, late-replicating genes as drivers; this produced a generation of false positives in early cancer genome studies.

#strong[Position matters as much as count.] Oncogenes are activated by specific substitutions at specific residues, so their mutations cluster at a handful of codons. Tumour suppressors are inactivated by anything that breaks the protein, so their mutations are dispersed and enriched for nonsense, frameshift and splice variants. Clustering and consequence-type enrichment are therefore evidence of selection in their own right, and you will measure both in this chapter.

#strong[Recurrence across patients is the signal.] One patient with a mutated gene is an anecdote. The statistical power comes from cohorts, which is why the public cancer genome resources matter.

Four resources carry most of the field. The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) profiled more than 10,000 tumours across 33 cancer types with matched normals and multi-omic assays \(Weinstein et al. 2013), and remains the reference cohort for most tumour types --- including the muscle-invasive bladder cancer cohort whose molecular subtypes were defined from it \(Robertson et al. 2017). The Pan-Cancer Analysis of Whole Genomes (PCAWG), from the International Cancer Genome Consortium, extended this to whole genomes and so to the non-coding and structural variation that exome sequencing cannot see \(Aaltonen et al. 2020). COSMIC curates somatic mutations from the literature and from these cohorts, and maintains the Cancer Gene Census of genes with documented causal roles \(Tate et al. 2018). cBioPortal provides a query-and-visualise interface over most published cohorts and is the fastest route from a gene name to its mutation profile across thousands of tumours \(Cerami et al. 2012).

