== Mutational signatures and clonal evolution
<sec-13-signatures>
Each mutational process leaves a characteristic pattern. Ultraviolet light produces C\>T substitutions at dipyrimidines; tobacco smoke produces C\>A transversions; defective mismatch repair produces small insertions and deletions at repeats; APOBEC enzymes produce clustered C\>T and C\>G at TCW motifs. A #strong[mutational signature] is the probability distribution over mutation types that one such process generates, and the standard representation uses 96 classes: six substitution types in pyrimidine orientation, each in 16 trinucleotide contexts. A tumour's observed spectrum is modelled as a mixture of signatures, and the fitted weights estimate how much each process contributed \(Alexandrov et al. 2020).

The chapter's data cannot demonstrate signature fitting honestly --- ClinVar holds ascertained germline variants, not a somatic catalogue, and trinucleotide context needs the reference genome, which is not bundled. What it can demonstrate is the first step of the construction: collapsing substitutions into the six pyrimidine-oriented classes.

#block[
```
complement = {"A": "T", "C": "G", "G": "C", "T": "A"}

def pyrimidine_class(ref, alt):
    """Express a substitution with a pyrimidine reference base, as signatures do."""
    if ref in "CT":
        return f"{ref}>{alt}"
    return f"{complement[ref]}>{complement[alt]}"

snvs = variants[(variants.variant_type == "single_nucleotide_variant")
                & variants.ref.isin(list("ACGT"))
                & variants.alt.isin(list("ACGT"))].copy()
snvs["substitution"] = [pyrimidine_class(r, a) for r, a in zip(snvs.ref, snvs.alt)]

print(f"{len(snvs):,} single-nucleotide variants")
#> 2,744 single-nucleotide variants
print(snvs.substitution.value_counts().to_string())
#> substitution
#> C>T    810
#> T>C    489
#> C>G    454
#> C>A    424
#> T>G    296
#> T>A    271
```

]
Why six classes rather than twelve? Because a C\>T change on one strand is a G\>A change on the other, and a variant caller reporting the plus strand would split one biological process across two categories. Collapsing to the pyrimidine reference removes an arbitrary strand convention.

The spectrum here is dominated by C\>T, which in germline data reflects deamination of methylated cytosine at CpG dinucleotides --- the dominant source of human germline point mutation --- overlaid on ClinVar's ascertainment, which collects variants that somebody chose to submit. #ref(<fig-13-spectrum>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the spectrum split by clinical class, and the split is instructive.

```
def clinical_class(label):
    if label.startswith(("Pathogenic", "Likely_pathogenic")):
        return "pathogenic / likely"
    if label.startswith(("Benign", "Likely_benign")):
        return "benign / likely"
    if label.startswith("Uncertain"):
        return "uncertain"
    if label.startswith("Conflicting"):
        return "conflicting"
    return "other"

variants["class"] = variants.clnsig.map(clinical_class)
snvs["class"] = snvs.clnsig.map(clinical_class)

spectrum = pd.crosstab(snvs.substitution, snvs["class"])
spectrum = spectrum[["pathogenic / likely", "benign / likely", "uncertain",
                     "conflicting"]]

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 3.6))
spectrum.plot(kind="bar", stacked=True, ax=ax,
              color=[PALETTE[3], PALETTE[1], PALETTE[7], PALETTE[2]])
ax.set_xlabel("Substitution (pyrimidine orientation)")
ax.set_ylabel("Variants")
ax.grid(axis="x", visible=False)
ax.legend(fontsize=7, title=None)
plt.xticks(rotation=0)
#> (array([0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]), [Text(0, 0, 'C>A'), Text(1, 0, 'C>G'), Text(2, 0, 'C>T'), Text(3, 0, 'T>A'), Text(4, 0, 'T>C'), Text(5, 0, 'T>G')])
