The upper panel is the structural argument for pathogenicity made visible. p53 works by binding specific DNA sequences and activating transcription; a substitution in the domain that contacts DNA, or in the residues that hold that domain's fold together, destroys the function. A substitution in the disordered C-terminal region usually does not. This is the empirical content of the ACMG code PM1 --- "located in a mutational hot spot or well-studied functional domain without benign variation" --- and the analysis above is how a laboratory would justify applying it to a specific gene.

== Rare disease diagnostics
<sec-13-rare-disease>
A child presents with developmental delay, seizures and dysmorphic features. Nothing in the pattern matches a textbook syndrome. The family then enters what clinicians call the #strong[diagnostic odyssey]: a sequence of specialist referrals, single-gene tests, panels and imaging that commonly runs for years and frequently ends without an answer. Exome or genome sequencing compresses the odyssey by testing every gene at once, and currently resolves between a quarter and a half of cases depending on phenotype and prior testing.

The analysis has a standard shape. #strong[Trio sequencing] --- proband plus both parents --- is the single most effective design, because it identifies #emph[de novo] variants directly. A variant present in the child and absent from both parents is a strong candidate when the phenotype is severe, sporadic and reproductively limiting, because such conditions cannot persist in a population by inheritance. The trio also resolves phase: two variants in the same gene matter very differently depending on whether they sit on the same chromosome or on opposite ones.

Filtering then proceeds in roughly this order. Remove variants common in population databases, since a severe, rare, early-onset condition cannot be caused by a variant carried by 1% of people --- gnomAD is the standard reference, and reports allele frequencies per ancestry group alongside constraint metrics for every gene \(Karczewski et al. 2020). Keep variants predicted to alter the protein or disrupt splicing. Restrict to genes whose known disease associations overlap the phenotype, using OMIM \(Amberger et al. 2018) and phenotype ontologies. Apply the inheritance model: #emph[de novo], homozygous recessive, compound heterozygous or X-linked. The product is usually between five and fifty candidate variants for manual review.

Each step has a characteristic failure mode, and all of them are ways of discarding the answer.

#strong[Population frequency filters fail where the population is unrepresented.] If the reference database contains few genomes from the patient's ancestry group, a locally common benign variant appears rare and survives the filter, while a genuinely rare pathogenic variant may be indistinguishable from population variation. Non-European ancestries remain substantially under-represented in reference databases, which makes diagnostic rates lower and VUS rates higher for those patients \(Sirugo et al. 2019; Popejoy and Fullerton 2016). This is a data problem with a clinical consequence, and the response is more diverse reference data, which is what initiatives such as H3Africa were created to produce \(Matovu et al. 2014).

#strong[Gene panels fail for genes not yet associated with disease.] Restricting to known disease genes is efficient and makes the novel-gene case invisible by construction. Re-analysing undiagnosed cases against updated gene--disease knowledge yields new diagnoses every year, which is why unsolved exomes should be revisited rather than closed.

#strong[Coverage failures are silent.] A variant in a region with no reads is simply absent from the output, and absence looks identical to reference. Exome capture is uneven, GC-rich first exons are frequently poorly covered, and a negative result is only as strong as the coverage behind it.

#strong[Structural and non-coding variation is missed.] Exome sequencing sees neither deep intronic variants that create splice sites nor most copy number and structural changes. A proportion of unsolved exomes are solved by genome sequencing for exactly this reason.

