== Classifying a variant under ACMG/AMP
<sec-13-acmg>
The ACMG/AMP framework is the standard for classifying germline variants for clinical use \(Richards et al. 2015). It replaces ad hoc judgement with a set of named evidence codes, each with a strength, combined by stated rules into one of five classes: pathogenic, likely pathogenic, uncertain significance, likely benign, benign.

Pathogenic evidence codes are lettered by strength. #strong[PVS1] is very strong, and applies to a null variant --- nonsense, frameshift, canonical splice site, initiation codon loss --- in a gene where loss of function is an established disease mechanism. #strong[PS] codes are strong: PS1 (same amino acid change as an established pathogenic variant), PS2 (confirmed #emph[de novo] with parentage verified), PS3 (well-established functional studies showing a damaging effect), PS4 (significantly increased prevalence in affected individuals). #strong[PM] codes are moderate: PM1 (located in a mutational hot spot or well-studied functional domain), PM2 (absent or extremely rare in population databases), PM5 (novel missense change at a residue where a different pathogenic missense change has been seen). #strong[PP] codes are supporting: PP3 (multiple computational lines of evidence predicting a deleterious effect), PP5 (reputable source reports it as pathogenic, now deprecated in practice). Benign codes mirror these: #strong[BA1] (allele frequency too high for the disease), #strong[BS] strong and #strong[BP] supporting.

Take a real record from the bundled file.

#block[
```
example = variants[(variants.pos == 7674221) & (variants.ref == "G")
                   & (variants.alt == "A")].iloc[0]

print("ClinVar variation ID:", example.variation_id)
print("protein change:      ", amino_acid_change(example.pos, example.ref, example.alt))
print("codon:               ", int(example.codon),
      "(DNA-binding domain:", DOMAIN_START <= example.codon <= DOMAIN_END, ")")
print("classification:      ", example.clnsig)
print("review status:       ", example.review)
print("population frequency:", "absent from 1000 Genomes"
      if np.isnan(example.af_tgp) else example.af_tgp)
print("recorded conditions: ", example.disease.replace("|", "; ")[:90])
```

]
This is R248W, ClinVar variation 12347, classified pathogenic with three-star expert-panel review. Reconstructing the evidence, in the order a laboratory would assemble it:

#strong[PM1 applies.] Codon 248 lies at position 248 of the DNA-binding domain annotated by UniProt, and the analysis in the previous section established that pathogenic missense variants in this gene concentrate there at an odds ratio of 45 against benign ones. Arginine 248 contacts the DNA minor groove directly.

#strong[PM2 applies.] The record carries no 1000 Genomes allele frequency, and the variant is not observed at appreciable frequency in population databases.

#strong[PS3 applies.] Transactivation assays across all possible #emph[TP53] missense substitutions have established that R248W abolishes sequence-specific transcriptional activation \(Kato et al. 2003; Giacomelli et al. 2018). Functional evidence of this quality is unusual; most genes have nothing comparable, which is a large part of why VUS rates are high elsewhere.

#strong[PS4 applies in the relevant setting.] Codon 248 is among the most frequently mutated positions in human cancer and recurs in Li-Fraumeni families, so its prevalence in affected individuals is far above population expectation.

#strong[PP3 applies.] Computational predictors agree, though this evidence is supporting and substantially correlated with PM1 --- the predictors use domain position and conservation as inputs, so the two codes are not independent.

Two strong plus two moderate codes meet the ACMG combining rule for pathogenic. The classification is not controversial.

Contrast it with the codon 72 record.

#block[
```
polymorphism = variants[(variants.pos == 7676154) & (variants.alt == "C")].iloc[0]

print("protein change:", amino_acid_change(polymorphism.pos, polymorphism.ref,
                                           polymorphism.alt))
print("classification:", polymorphism.clnsig,
      "| 1000 Genomes allele frequency:", polymorphism.af_tgp)
```

]
P72R sits at an allele frequency of 0.543 --- the majority allele in the 1000 Genomes sample \(Auton et al. 2015). BA1 applies as stand-alone benign evidence: no variant causing a rare dominant cancer predisposition syndrome can be carried by half the population. One frequency observation outweighs any amount of in silico prediction, which is why BA1 is the first filter applied and the most common reason a "damaging" prediction is wrong.

