== Properties that make genomic data exceptional
<sec-14-exceptionalism>
Data-protection law was written with names, addresses and account numbers in mind. Those identifiers share a convenient property: they can be changed. A compromised credit card is reissued; a genome is not. Four properties set genomic data apart, and each one breaks a control that works for ordinary personal data.

#strong[It is identifying in itself.] A genotype is not data #emph[about] a person that happens to be linked to an identifier --- it #emph[is] an identifier, and a better one than a name. You will show below that a few dozen common single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) are enough to pick one person out of a population the size of a city, and that the same calculation extended to the whole species needs only a few more markers. This is why forensic systems work at all, and it is why stripping names from a variant file does not make it anonymous. The genotype is the name.

#strong[It is immutable.] The usual response to a breach --- revoke the credential and issue a new one --- has no analogue. A genome disclosed in 2015 is still disclosing in 2045, and it will be disclosing against a background of analytical methods that did not exist when it was released. Risk assessments made at the time of deposit therefore decay in one direction only. A dataset judged safe to post openly in 2008 can become re-identifiable in 2013 without anyone touching the file, which is precisely what happened.

#strong[It is shared with relatives.] Consent is individual; inheritance is not. A person who deposits their own exome discloses, probabilistically, about half of each of their parents' and children's variants and a quarter of each sibling's, and discloses the Y haplotype of every patrilineal male relative. No consent framework built around individual autonomy handles this cleanly, because the people most affected by a disclosure may never have been asked. Long-range familial search, the technique that has since been used to identify suspects in cold cases from third-cousin matches in consumer genealogy databases \(Erlich et al. 2018), is the operational form of this property: you do not need a person in a database to find them, only a relative.

#strong[It is predictive, incompletely.] A genotype supports probabilistic statements about future disease, drug response and traits whose social meaning is contested. Those predictions are weak for most common disease and strong for a handful of monogenic conditions, and they are easy to over-read by employers, insurers and relatives who cannot distinguish a relative risk of 1.2 from a diagnosis. The asymmetry is the harm: too weak to act on clinically, quite strong enough to be socially damaging.

