== Consent models and the return of results
<sec-14-consent>
A consent form is a contract between a participant and a project about future uses that neither can fully foresee. Three designs dominate, and each resolves that tension differently.

#strong[Specific consent] names the study and its question. It is the clearest ethically and the most restrictive computationally: data collected to study tuberculosis susceptibility cannot later be used to study drug metabolism without returning to the participants, which for a decade-old cohort is often impossible.

#strong[Broad consent] asks permission for future health-related research in general, with oversight delegated to a research ethics committee and a data access committee. It is the model under which most biobanks operate and the reason that large cohorts remain analytically useful for decades. Its cost is that the participant's agreement becomes thin: a person who consented to "health research" in 2010 did not contemplate polygenic embryo screening, or machine-learning models trained on their sequence and sold under licence. Knoppers (2014) sets out the framework under which broad consent is usually defended --- that the participant consents to a #emph[governance process], not to a list of projects --- which is coherent, and which depends entirely on the governance process actually working.

#strong[Tiered consent] decomposes the question: the participant chooses separately whether to permit commercial use, whether to allow deposition in international archives, whether to be re-contacted, and whether to receive findings. It respects participants' heterogeneous preferences, and it creates the operational problem that your cohort fragments into subsets with different permissions. If you have ever had to compute an association on the 2,100 of 3,000 participants whose consent permits industry collaboration, you know that the consent matrix has to be a column in your sample table from day one, enforced in code, not a PDF in a filing cabinet.

#strong[Dynamic consent] adds a channel: participants hold an account through which they see what has been done with their data and can change their permissions at any time. It is the most responsive model and the most demanding to run, requiring sustained software, identity management and the ability to withdraw data from published analyses; it is adopted by well-resourced cohorts and promised by many more.

Choosing between these is not a matter of picking the most ethical-sounding option but of matching the consent to the uses you can actually honour, and then enforcing it in the pipeline. A tiered consent that nobody checks is worse than a broad consent honestly obtained.

=== Findings you did not go looking for
<findings-you-did-not-go-looking-for>
An exome sequenced to study epilepsy also contains #emph[BRCA1]. Whether to look, and whether to tell, is the problem of #strong[incidental] or #strong[secondary findings] --- the distinction being that incidental findings turn up unsought, whereas secondary findings are those a project deliberately screens for beyond its primary question.

The argument for return rests on beneficence: a pathogenic #emph[BRCA1] variant in a woman who does not know about it is an actionable, preventable risk, and withholding it from someone whose sample you hold is hard to defend. The argument against rests on four practical facts. Research-grade sequencing is not clinically validated, so calls may be wrong. Interpretation shifts, and Richards et al. (2015) exists precisely because classifying a variant is an evidence-weighing exercise rather than a lookup. Returning a result imposes a care pathway --- confirmatory testing, counselling, surveillance --- that many projects cannot fund and many health systems cannot absorb. And the participant may not have agreed to receive it.

The consensus that has emerged is narrow and defensible: return findings that are clinically validated in an accredited laboratory, that concern conditions which are serious and actionable, and that the participant agreed in advance to receive. Everything outside that intersection is not returned. Specify the intersection in the protocol #emph[before] sequencing, because deciding after you have seen a pathogenic variant is deciding under pressure, and specify who makes the call, because it should not be the bioinformatician who noticed it at midnight.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
If you find a variant with apparent clinical significance in research data, do not contact the participant, do not tell the clinician informally, and do not mention it to the laboratory group. Escalate through the route your protocol specifies --- usually the principal investigator and the research ethics committee --- and record what you found and when. The route exists so that the decision is made by people with the clinical and legal standing to make it, and so that the participant's prior wishes about receiving results are honoured rather than overridden by your discovery.

]
