== Governance and controlled access
<sec-14-governance>
Because individual-level human genomic data cannot be anonymised, the governance model is not anonymity but #strong[controlled access]: the data stay in a managed repository, and investigators apply for permission to use them for a stated purpose.

The two principal archives are the database of Genotypes and Phenotypes (dbGaP) at NCBI and the European Genome-phenome Archive (EGA) at EMBL-EBI and the Centre for Genomic Regulation. Both hold summary metadata openly --- study design, variables measured, sample counts --- so that a researcher can discover that a dataset exists and judge whether it suits their question. Individual-level data sit behind an application. Sayers et al. (2021) describes the NCBI side of this arrangement; the archives are also the reason that a paper's data availability statement reading "available in dbGaP under accession phsXXXXXX" is a real commitment rather than a brush-off, since the accession can be checked.

The application is assessed by a #strong[data access committee] (DAC), whose members are usually a mixture of study investigators, ethicists and, in better-designed cases, community representatives. A DAC asks whether the proposed use falls within the consent the participants gave, whether the applicant's institution has signed an agreement accepting the obligations, and whether the requested data are the minimum needed. Decisions take weeks to months. Plan for it: a project that assumes dbGaP access will be granted in time for a conference deadline is a project that will not have data.

A useful way to think about the whole arrangement is the #strong[five safes] framework, developed for official statistics and now widely used for health data. It decomposes "is this access safe?" into five questions that can be answered separately:

#figure([
#table(
  columns: (50%, 50%),
  align: (auto,auto,),
  table.header([Dimension], [Question the framework asks],),
  table.hline(),
  [Safe projects], [Is the proposed use appropriate, lawful and within consent?],
  [Safe people], [Is the applicant trained, accountable and bound by an agreement?],
  [Safe settings], [Does the technical environment prevent copying, leakage and onward transfer?],
  [Safe data], [Have the data been minimised and coarsened as far as the question allows?],
  [Safe outputs], [Have the results been checked so they do not disclose individuals?],
)
], caption: figure.caption(
position: top, 
[
The five safes applied to genomic data access. Each dimension can be strengthened to compensate for weakness in another, which is what makes the framework useful in negotiation.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-tbl", 
supplement: "Table", 
)
<tbl-14-five-safes>


#ref(<tbl-14-five-safes>, supplement: [Table]) reframes the negotiation, because a weakness in one dimension can be compensated elsewhere. A committee that cannot make the data safe enough to release can instead make the setting safer, by requiring analysis inside a trusted research environment that the data never leave --- the direction most national initiatives have taken. Federated analysis pushes this further: the Beacon protocol and the standards of the Global Alliance for Genomics and Health let a researcher ask whether a variant is present in a dataset and receive an answer without the record moving \(Fiume et al. 2019), so that the question is the only thing that travels. Even then, a sequence of carefully chosen beacon queries is itself a membership attack, which is why beacons now apply query budgets and noise.

Be clear, against all this, about what "de-identified" guarantees: that direct identifiers have been removed, and nothing more. It does not guarantee that re-identification is impossible, that the data may be posted publicly, or that a regulator will treat the data as outside its scope. De-identification is one layer among the five safes, not a solution.

== The legal landscape
<sec-14-legal>
Four instruments shape most genomic work. What follows describes their structure and the obligations they create; it is not legal advice, and the application of any of them to a specific project is a question for your institution's data protection officer and research ethics committee.

#strong[The General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)] governs personal data in the European Union and European Economic Area, and reaches any project processing data about people in that territory regardless of where the analysis runs. Three features matter for bioinformatics. Genetic and health data are #emph[special category] data, requiring an additional condition for processing beyond the usual legal basis --- in research, typically explicit consent or a public-interest/scientific-research basis under Article 9(2)(j) combined with safeguards. Pseudonymised data remain personal data, so a de-identified VCF is in scope. And transfer outside the EEA requires a legal mechanism such as an adequacy decision or standard contractual clauses, which is why European consortia often cannot simply deposit in a US archive or compute in an arbitrary cloud region. The practical consequence for you is that the region your storage bucket sits in is a compliance decision, not an infrastructure preference.

#strong[The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA)] is narrower than it is usually assumed to be. It applies in the United States to covered entities --- health plans, clearing houses and providers that transmit health information electronically --- and to their business associates. A university research laboratory is frequently #emph[not] a covered entity, so HIPAA may not apply to its research data at all, while the hospital that supplied the samples certainly is. HIPAA's Safe Harbor de-identification standard enumerates eighteen identifier types to remove; genomic sequence is not among them, which is a known gap rather than a licence, and the alternative expert determination route is where genomic data is actually assessed. The separate statute that prohibits genetic discrimination by health insurers and employers in the US is the Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act of 2008, which notably does not extend to life, disability or long-term-care insurance.

#strong[Nigeria's data protection framework] will be the operative law for many readers of this book. The Nigeria Data Protection Regulation (NDPR), issued in 2019 by the National Information Technology Development Agency, established consent requirements, data subject rights and cross-border transfer restrictions along broadly GDPR-shaped lines; the Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023 put the regime on a statutory footing and created the Nigeria Data Protection Commission as regulator. Two provisions bite most often in genomic research: the lawful basis for processing sensitive personal data, and the restriction on transfer to countries without adequate protection --- relevant every time a Nigerian cohort's sequence data is uploaded to an archive hosted elsewhere. Ethical approval from a review committee is a separate requirement and does not substitute for the data-protection one.

#strong[The Nagoya Protocol] is not a privacy instrument at all, and it is the one most often overlooked by computational groups. It implements the Convention on Biological Diversity's provisions on access to genetic resources and the fair and equitable sharing of benefits arising from their utilisation. Its subject matter is genetic resources --- including, in the interpretation adopted by many parties, digital sequence information derived from them --- and its mechanism is prior informed consent from the provider country plus mutually agreed terms covering benefit sharing. In practice, if your project uses samples or sequences obtained from another country's biodiversity, including pathogens isolated there, you may need an access permit and a benefit-sharing agreement, and downloading a sequence from a public archive does not automatically discharge that obligation. The treatment of digital sequence information under the Protocol has been contested since it was adopted and remains the subject of international negotiation, so check the current position rather than relying on a textbook.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
Satisfying GDPR, the NDPR or an institutional review board tells you that your project is permitted. It does not tell you that it is fair to the participants, that the community that provided the samples will benefit, or that the result will generalise to them. Those are separate judgements, and they are the subject of the next two sections. A project can be entirely lawful and still be extractive.

]
