== Equity and the diversity deficit
<sec-14-equity>
The reference data on which human genomics rests is drawn overwhelmingly from people of European ancestry. Popejoy and Fullerton (2016) quantified the imbalance in the GWAS catalogue --- around four in five participants at the time of their analysis --- and Sirugo et al. (2019) traced the consequences through to the clinic. The imbalance has narrowed since, mainly through large East Asian cohorts, and remains severe for African, Indigenous American, Oceanian and admixed populations. This is not only a fairness problem. It is a technical defect that propagates into every result computed from those references, in three identifiable ways.

#strong[Polygenic scores do not transfer.] A polygenic score sums the effects of thousands of variants, with weights estimated in a discovery cohort. The weights depend on the linkage disequilibrium structure of that cohort, because most of the markers are tags for unobserved causal variants rather than causal themselves. Linkage patterns differ between populations, so a score trained in Europeans applies the wrong weights to the wrong tags elsewhere, and its predictive accuracy degrades roughly with genetic distance from the discovery cohort --- substantially so for African-ancestry individuals, in whom shorter haplotype blocks mean a European-derived tag may not track the causal variant at all. A score deployed clinically without ancestry-specific validation will therefore misclassify risk differentially by ancestry, which is the precise definition of an inequitable test.

#strong[Variant interpretation depends on allele frequency.] The ACMG/AMP framework for classifying sequence variants \(Richards et al. 2015) uses population frequency as evidence in both directions: a variant too common in any population to cause a severe dominant disorder is downgraded, and absence from population databases supports pathogenicity. Both judgements depend on having a well-powered reference for the patient's population. Where that reference is thin, benign population-specific variants are not recognised as common and accumulate as variants of uncertain significance (VUS) --- or worse, get reported as likely pathogenic. The rate of uncertain results is consistently higher for patients of non-European ancestry, and the mechanism is simply missing denominators. Expanded references such as gnomAD \(Karczewski et al. 2020) and the population panels of the 1000 Genomes Project \(Auton et al. 2015) are the direct remedy, and their later releases improved non-European representation specifically for this reason.

#strong[Discovery is skewed.] Variants that are common in under-represented populations and rare in Europeans are systematically absent from the discovery process, so the biology they point to is not found. The genes and pathways our textbooks name as disease-relevant are, in part, an artefact of who was sequenced.

The response that matters most for readers of this book is H3Africa, the Human Heredity and Health in Africa initiative, which from 2014 funded African-led genomic research with an explicit emphasis on building capacity on the continent rather than exporting samples \(Matovu et al. 2014). Its significance is structural rather than merely numerical. H3Africa established African-based biorepositories, a pan-African bioinformatics network, and data-sharing policies written by African investigators, which changed who sets the terms of collaboration. The pathogen-genomics response to SARS-CoV-2 across Africa showed what the resulting capacity can do when it exists: sequencing, analysis and variant reporting carried out in-country and in near real time \(Tegally et al. 2022), on infrastructure that would not have been there a decade earlier.

== Data sovereignty and benefit sharing
<sec-14-sovereignty>
#strong[Helicopter research] --- also called parachute or extractive research --- is the practice in which investigators from a well-resourced institution collect samples or data in a lower-income setting, take them home for analysis, publish with local collaborators relegated to the acknowledgements or a middle authorship, and leave nothing behind. It is common enough to have a literature of its own, and genomics is particularly prone to it because the valuable object is small, portable and infinitely copyable: a hard drive of sequence leaves the country and the capacity to analyse it does not arrive.

The pattern is recognisable from the outside: samples or sequence leave for analysis that could in principle have been done locally; local scientists appear as sample providers rather than as authors with intellectual contributions; the code and processed data stay with the external partner; and outputs appear in venues the originating community cannot access, with no training, equipment or co-authored follow-on work left behind. No single item proves bad faith --- sometimes the capacity genuinely is not there, which is the honest reason --- but a project matching the whole pattern is extracting, whatever its intentions.

#strong[Data sovereignty] is the counter-principle: that data about a people, or collected within a jurisdiction, is governed under that community's or country's authority. Its legal expression appears in frameworks such as the NDPR's transfer restrictions and in Nagoya-style access agreements; its community expression appears in Indigenous data governance principles \(Carroll et al. 2020), which assert collective rather than only individual rights over data and over the narratives built from it. The practical upshot is that "may I upload this dataset?" has a legal answer, an ethical answer and a community answer, and they are not always the same.

Benefit sharing is how a project answers the extraction charge concretely, and it has to be designed in at the proposal stage because that is when the budget is written. The forms that actually transfer value are prosaic: budget lines for local salaries and equipment rather than only consumables; authorship positions agreed in writing before the work starts, including first and senior positions for local investigators who do the corresponding work; analysis performed jointly, with the code and the intermediate data shared so that the capacity to repeat it stays behind; training of named individuals, not generic "capacity building"\; deposition of data under governance the originating institution participates in; and a commitment to return results to participants and communities in a form they can use. Portable sequencing protocols that let analysis happen where the samples are \(Quick et al. 2017) and open phylogenetic platforms that let anyone see the same tree the consortium sees \(Hadfield et al. 2017) change the economics of this, because they remove the technical excuse for centralising the work.

