== Dual use and sequence biosecurity
<sec-14-dual-use>
Sequence data is information, and some of it is information about how to cause harm. Dual-use research of concern is work that is conducted for legitimate purposes but could be directly misapplied --- the canonical examples being studies that increase a pathogen's transmissibility or host range, reconstructions of eradicated or extinct pathogens from sequence, and syntheses of select agents from published genomes. The relevance to bioinformatics is direct, because the sequence is the specification. The synthesis of an infectious virus from published sequence data has been demonstrated more than once, and the bottleneck is no longer informational.

Three practical obligations follow for a computational worker. First, know whether the organism you are analysing is a regulated agent in your jurisdiction before you start, not after; lists are maintained nationally and your institution's biosafety officer holds the current one. Second, treat gain-of-function-adjacent analyses --- predicting mutations that increase receptor binding, for instance --- as requiring prior institutional review even when no wet-lab work is involved, because publishing the prediction is itself the act of concern. Third, understand the screening regime that sits between a sequence and a physical construct: providers of synthetic DNA screen orders against sequences of concern and verify customers, and that screening is a genuine control point rather than a formality. Do not design around it, and do not treat a provider's screen as a judgement about whether your project is sound.

The tension with openness is real and should not be waved away. The same transparency that makes pathogen genomic surveillance work --- rapid, open deposition of outbreak sequences --- is what makes misuse conceivable, and the surveillance benefit has so far been judged, correctly in my view, to outweigh the risk for most pathogens. That judgement is made case by case by bodies constituted for it. Your obligation is to recognise when your project is one of the cases and to refer it, which is a much lower bar than deciding the question yourself.

== Professional practice and a project checklist
<sec-14-practice>
Most ethical failures in computational work are not dramatic. They are a results table that cannot be regenerated, a dataset whose provenance nobody recorded, a collaborator who was not credited, a licence that was never chosen. Four habits prevent most of them.

#strong[Write a data management plan, and write it first.] A DMP states, before data exist, what will be collected, where it will live, who may access it, how long it is retained, how it is backed up, what is deposited at publication and under what terms, and who is responsible after the grant ends. Funders increasingly require one, which is the weakest reason to write it; the strong reason is that writing it surfaces decisions --- such as a consent form that does not permit the deposition your target journal requires --- while they can still be changed. Wilson et al. (2017) gives the practical version for small projects, and its central advice holds: decide where raw data live, make them read-only, and never edit them in place.

#strong[Make outputs FAIR, and know what the word commits you to.] The FAIR principles \(Wilkinson et al. 2016) ask that data be findable, accessible, interoperable and reusable. Findable means a persistent identifier and rich metadata, not a laboratory web page. Accessible means retrievable by a standard protocol, with authentication where needed --- and here is the point most often missed: #emph[FAIR does not mean open]. Controlled-access human data is FAIR when its metadata are discoverable and the access procedure is documented. Interoperable means standard formats and vocabularies, which is why Gene Ontology terms belong in an annotation table rather than free-text descriptions. Reusable means a clear licence and enough provenance for someone to judge fitness for their purpose.

#strong[Choose a licence deliberately for each kind of output.] Permissive software licences such as MIT and BSD allow reuse with attribution; copyleft licences such as the GPL additionally require derivatives to be shared alike; Creative Commons licences suit text and figures, with CC0 or an open data licence the usual choice for databases. Note that a non-commercial clause excludes more reuse than most authors intend. The failure mode is not choosing at all: unlicensed code is by default not reusable, which undermines the rest of your reproducibility work.

#strong[Credit computational work properly.] Bioinformatics contributions are systematically under-credited, partly because the work is invisible in the final figure. Two corrections cost nothing: use a structured contribution statement, which forces an account of who conceived, coded, analysed and wrote; and make software citable in its own right, archiving the repository at the version used and citing tools by their methods papers rather than naming them in passing \(Grüning et al. 2018).

#strong[Publish what reproducibility actually requires.] A methods section that names the tools is not reproducible; the test is whether a competent stranger with your data can obtain your numbers. That means analysis code in a version-controlled repository archived at a citable version; an environment specification pinning versions, not a prose list; the parameters for every step, including those left at defaults; the random seeds; the exact reference genome and annotation release; and the intermediate files needed to re-enter the pipeline partway. Sandve et al. (2013) compresses this into ten rules, the hardest being to record every intermediate result and the provenance of every figure. The next chapter builds a project that meets this standard end to end.

=== A checklist to apply before the project starts
<a-checklist-to-apply-before-the-project-starts>
Work through these before data collection, not at submission. Items that cannot be answered are the project's real risks.

+ #strong[Question and consent.] Does the consent under which these data were collected cover the analysis I intend? If I cannot find the form, I do not have an answer.
+ #strong[Legal basis and approvals.] Which law governs these data, what is my lawful basis, which committee approved the work, and who is the data protection officer I would call?
+ #strong[Transfer.] Will any data cross a border, including into a cloud region, and is the permitting mechanism documented?
+ #strong[Identifiability.] What in my working files could identify a participant --- genotypes, dates, localities, free-text notes, file names --- and which of the five safes is carrying the weight?
+ #strong[Access control.] Who can read the raw data today, is that the list I would defend to the ethics committee, and does it shrink when someone leaves the group?
+ #strong[Representation.] Which populations are in my reference data, and does the result generalise to the people it will be applied to? If not, what do the limitations say?
+ #strong[Benefit and credit.] Who benefits from this result, in what form and when, and is the authorship and data governance agreement in writing?
+ #strong[Findings.] What happens if I see a clinically significant variant, who decides, and is that in the protocol?
+ #strong[Dual use.] Could this analysis, or the sequences in it, materially assist someone trying to cause harm? If the answer is not plainly no, whom do I refer it to?
+ #strong[Reproducibility and the end state.] Can a stranger regenerate every number from the repository as it stands? Where do the data live in five years, under what licence, and who is responsible?

== Summary
<sec-14-summary>
- Genomic data is identifying, immutable, familial and predictive; each property breaks a control that works for ordinary personal data, which is why removing names is not de-identification.
- A few dozen independent common markers make a genotype profile unique in a population of millions. The calculation is short, and it is the honest basis for treating any individual-level variant file as identifiable.
- Allele-frequency attacks work by accumulating sub-threshold evidence across many markers, so their power grows as panels densify. Published summary statistics are not automatically safe.
- Because individual human genomic data cannot be anonymised, governance relies on controlled access, data access committees and, increasingly, on moving the analysis to the data rather than the reverse.
- Consent models trade participant autonomy against future analytical usefulness. Whatever model is chosen must be enforced in the sample table and the pipeline, not only in the filing cabinet.
- GDPR, HIPAA, the NDPR and the Nagoya Protocol regulate different things --- personal data, covered health entities, national data processing and access to genetic resources --- and a project can be subject to several at once.
- The European-ancestry bias in reference data is a technical defect with clinical consequences: polygenic scores transfer poorly and uncertain variant classifications accumulate for under-represented populations.
- Benefit sharing and data sovereignty are design decisions taken at the proposal stage, when the budget and the authorship agreement are written, and they are unrecoverable afterwards.
- Reproducibility obliges you to publish code, pinned environments, parameters, seeds, reference versions and provenance --- not a prose description of what you did.

== Exercises
<sec-14-exercises>
+ Using the per-marker match probability formula in #ref(<sec-14-reidentification>, supplement: [Section]), calculate the random match probability for a panel of 20 markers all with minor allele frequency 0.5, and for 20 markers all with minor allele frequency 0.05. Explain in one sentence why forensic panels prefer common markers.
+ A collaborator proposes to publish a supplementary table of per-variant allele counts for 150 cases and 150 controls from a study of a stigmatised infection. State the attack this enables, name the three inputs the attacker needs, and suggest two modifications that preserve most of the scientific value.
+ Modify the uniqueness simulation to draw minor allele frequencies between 0.01 and 0.05 instead of 0.05 and 0.50. How many markers are now needed for every individual in the population of 10,000 to be unique? Explain the direction of the change.
+ Classify each of the following as pseudonymised or anonymous, and justify: a VCF with the sample column renamed to `S014`\; a table of mean expression per tissue across 400 donors; a BAM file with the read-group identifier removed; a figure showing a principal component analysis of 2,000 genotyped individuals.
+ Your project will sequence 500 participants recruited at a teaching hospital in Bauchi, analyse the data on a cloud instance in Europe, and deposit it in the EGA. List the approvals and agreements you need before the first sample is collected, and identify which of them constrains where the analysis may run.
+ Take the membership-attack simulation and reduce the pool from 200 individuals to 20, keeping 5,000 markers. Predict the effect on detection before you run it, then run it and explain the result in terms of how much each contributor influences the published frequencies.
+ A polygenic score for type 2 diabetes, trained in a European-ancestry cohort, is proposed for clinical use in a Nigerian hospital. Write the three sentences of the limitations section that the score's developers should have written, and state what evidence would be needed before deployment.
+ Read a data availability statement in a recent paper in your own area. Judge it against the FAIR principles, saying for each of the four letters whether the statement satisfies it and what specifically is missing.

== Solutions
<sec-14-solutions>
#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
#strong[\1.] Common markers are far more informative per marker: at a minor allele frequency of 0.5 the match probability is at its minimum of 0.375, whereas at 0.05 almost everyone is a homozygote for the major allele and the match probability is close to 0.82.

#block[
```
def match_probability(p):
    return (1 - p) ** 4 + 4 * p ** 2 * (1 - p) ** 2 + p ** 4

for frequency in (0.50, 0.05):
    print(f"MAF {frequency:.2f}: per-marker {match_probability(frequency):.4f}, "
          f"20 markers {match_probability(frequency) ** 20:.3e}")
#> MAF 0.50: per-marker 0.3750, 20 markers 3.024e-09
#> MAF 0.05: per-marker 0.8235, 20 markers 2.059e-02
```

]
#strong[\2.] The proposal enables an allele-frequency membership attack of the kind in #ref(<eq-14-homer>, supplement: [Equation]). The attacker needs the published case-group frequencies, allele frequencies for a matched reference population (freely available from gnomAD or the 1000 Genomes Project), and the genotypes of the individual they wish to test. Because the case group is defined by a stigmatised infection, confirming membership discloses the diagnosis. Defensible modifications: publish only the variants that reach the paper's significance threshold rather than the genome-wide table, which removes most of the accumulating evidence; or publish effect sizes and standard errors with the counts collapsed into bins; or place the full summary table behind the same controlled access as the individual data, with the accession cited in the paper.

#strong[\3.] Many more markers are needed, because rare markers are uninformative: almost every individual is a common-allele homozygote at a marker with a minor allele frequency of 0.03, so each marker partitions the population very unevenly.

#block[
```
rare_frequency = rng.uniform(0.01, 0.05, 200)
rare_genotypes = rng.binomial(2, rare_frequency, size=(10_000, 200)).astype(np.int8)
for markers in (30, 60, 100, 150, 200):
    print(f"{markers:4d} rare markers: {100 * fraction_unique(rare_genotypes[:, :markers]):6.2f}% unique")
#>   30 rare markers:  19.39% unique
#>   60 rare markers:  73.68% unique
#>  100 rare markers:  98.49% unique
#>  150 rare markers: 100.00% unique
#>  200 rare markers: 100.00% unique
```

]
#strong[\4.] The VCF is pseudonymised --- the genotypes themselves identify, and `S014` is a code, not an erasure. Mean expression per tissue over 400 donors is aggregate and, absent any individual-level outlier structure, is reasonably treated as anonymous, though expression data can carry genotype information through expression quantitative trait loci if reported per donor. The BAM is pseudonymised, and in fact barely changed: the reads contain the variants. The principal component figure is aggregate in appearance but each point is one individual, and coordinates read off a published plot can be matched against a reference panel, so treat it as pseudonymised and avoid publishing the underlying coordinates.

#strong[\5.] Required before the first sample: ethical approval from the hospital's health research ethics committee and, depending on scope, the national health research ethics committee; a consent form whose wording covers international deposition, secondary use and the return-of-results policy you intend; a data protection assessment and lawful basis under the NDPR and the Nigeria Data Protection Act, with a documented transfer mechanism for moving personal data outside Nigeria; a data processing or transfer agreement with the cloud provider and with any analytical partner; an EGA submission agreement naming the data access committee and its membership; and a written authorship and data governance agreement with the collaborating institution. The transfer mechanism and the consent wording jointly constrain where the analysis may run --- if neither permits export, the analysis must be done in-country or in a trusted research environment that the agreement names.

#strong[\6.] Detection improves. Each of 20 contributors moves the published frequencies about ten times as much as each of 200 does, so the signal each individual leaves in the summary is correspondingly larger. The general rule is that small pools leak more per member, which is why minimum cell-size rules exist in statistical disclosure control.

#block[
```
small_pool = pool_dosages[:20]
small_published = small_pool.mean(axis=0)
inside_small = np.array([membership_statistic(g, small_published, reference_frequency)
                         for g in small_pool])
outside_small = np.array([membership_statistic(g, small_published, reference_frequency)
                          for g in outside_dosages])
threshold = outside_small.mean() + 3 * outside_small.std()
print(f"pool of 20: {100 * (inside_small > threshold).mean():.0f}% detected, "
      f"{100 * (outside_small > threshold).mean():.1f}% false positives")
#> pool of 20: 100% detected, 0.0% false positives
```

]
#strong[\7.] An acceptable answer states that the score's weights were estimated in a cohort of European ancestry and that predictive accuracy is expected to decline with genetic distance from that cohort because the markers tag causal variants through population-specific linkage disequilibrium; that the score has not been validated in any West African population and its calibration --- the mapping from score to absolute risk --- is therefore unknown; and that applying it clinically risks differential misclassification by ancestry. The evidence needed before deployment is validation in a sufficiently powered cohort from the target population, reporting both discrimination and calibration, ideally with weights re-estimated or at least re-calibrated locally.

#strong[\8.] A statement satisfying all four letters names a repository and an accession or digital object identifier (findable), states the access route and, for controlled data, the committee and application procedure (accessible), confirms that standard formats and ontologies were used (interoperable), and gives the licence plus the processing provenance including reference build and tool versions (reusable). Statements reading "data are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request" fail on all four, since nothing is identified, no route is specified, no format is guaranteed and no licence is granted.

