= A complete project, start to finish
<a-complete-project-start-to-finish>
#block[
#strong[After working through this chapter you will be able to]

- convert a vague interest into a question that a specific dataset can answer
- lay out a project directory so that raw data, code and derived results cannot be confused
- capture a software environment precisely enough that someone else can reproduce your numbers
- write a Makefile that rebuilds only the parts of an analysis that are out of date
- diagnose an analysis that has failed silently, by comparing its output against an independent annotation
- state what a result does not show, and defend that statement to a reviewer

]
Every previous chapter taught one technique against one dataset. A real project is not a sequence of techniques; it is a question, a set of decisions made under uncertainty, a pile of intermediate files, and an argument. This chapter is the capstone: you will carry out one complete analysis, from the first vague interest to a result you could defend, using the bundled #emph[Mycoplasmoides genitalium] G37 genome (`data/sequences/mycoplasma-genitalium-NC_000908.2.fasta`) and its RefSeq annotation (`data/annotation/mycoplasma-genitalium.gff`).

The question is a practical one that anyone who annotates a new genome faces. #emph[Ab initio] gene prediction --- finding genes from sequence composition alone, with no homology evidence --- is fast and free, whereas curated annotation is slow and expensive. So: how much of a curated bacterial annotation does an #emph[ab initio] predictor actually recover, and where it disagrees, which method is wrong? #emph[M. genitalium] is the right organism to ask it of, because at 580 kb with around 500 protein-coding genes it is one of the smallest self-replicating genomes known \(Fraser et al. 1995), and because it contains a trap that will cause the first analysis in this chapter to fail. That failure is the most useful part of the chapter and it is left in deliberately.

What you will build along the way matters as much as the answer. A project directory whose structure makes mistakes visible. An environment file that pins the software. A Git history whose messages explain decisions rather than describing diffs. A Makefile that regenerates every result from the raw data with one command. These are the difference between an analysis that survives peer review and a folder of scripts that nobody, including you, can rerun in six months. The chapter ends with the write-up: the figures, the methods section, the availability statements, and a reviewer-style self-check you can apply to your own work before someone else applies a harsher version of it.

