== From interest to answerable question
<sec-15-question>
"I am interested in bacterial genome annotation" is not a project. It names a field, not a question, and it cannot be finished. Turning an interest into something answerable means forcing four things to be specific.

#strong[A comparison.] Almost every answerable question is a comparison: this against that, before against after, predicted against observed. An interest becomes a question when you can name the two things being compared. Here, they are an #emph[ab initio] prediction and a curated reference annotation of the same genome.

#strong[A measurable quantity.] The comparison has to produce a number. "How well does prediction agree with curation?" needs an operational definition of agreement, and choosing it is a scientific decision, not a formality. Two annotations of the same gene often share a stop codon but disagree about where translation starts, because the 3' end of an open reading frame is determined by the first in-frame stop whereas the 5' end depends on ribosome binding site models. So we will score agreement on the stop coordinate and strand, and treat the start separately. Had we scored exact coordinate identity, we would have measured mostly start-site prediction and called it annotation accuracy.

#strong[A dataset that can settle it.] The question must be answerable with data you can actually obtain. Here both annotations exist for the same assembly, which is the whole reason this comparison is cheap.

#strong[A statement of what would count as an answer.] Write it down before running anything: #emph["Prodigal recovers X% of RefSeq coding sequences by stop coordinate and predicts Y% more; the disagreements fall into these classes, for these reasons."] A pre-written answer template is the cheapest protection against the habit of running analyses until something looks interesting.

