)
<fig-15-map>


The disagreements cluster, and they cluster on the grey bands. Of the 26 unmatched predictions, 19 overlap one of the 20 loci that RefSeq annotates as pseudogenes, and the unmatched reference genes carry products named adhesin P1, adhesin P110 and MgpC cytadherence protein. These are the MgPa adhesin gene family \(Fraser et al. 1995): a set of repeated, recombining sequences that #emph[M. genitalium] uses for antigenic variation, present in the genome as a functional operon plus multiple partial copies. In a repeat family of this kind the question "where does the gene end?" has no single answer, because the locus is a mosaic of complete and degenerate copies. RefSeq resolves it with homology evidence and curator judgement; Prodigal resolves it with a composition model; and they resolve it differently.

That is the project's second result, and it is more interesting than the first. The 95% figure is not a uniform error rate of 5% spread across the genome. It is near-perfect agreement over the single-copy core and systematic disagreement in one repetitive, biologically unusual region, where the disagreement reflects genuine ambiguity rather than a mistake by either method.

== Interpretation and limits
<sec-15-interpretation>
What the analysis shows: on a small, GC-poor, gene-dense bacterial genome analysed under the correct translation table, #emph[ab initio] prediction reproduces the stop coordinates of 95.6% of curated coding sequences with 95.1% precision and identical start codons for 92.6% of the genes it recovers; its failures are concentrated in very short genes and in a repeat-expanded surface antigen family; and the single most consequential parameter is the genetic code, which moved sensitivity by 30 percentage points.

What it does not show is longer, and stating it is not modesty but method. A reader cannot use a result whose scope is undeclared.

- #strong[It is one genome.] Every number here is a property of #emph[M. genitalium] G37. A GC-rich actinobacterium, an organism with introns, or a draft assembly in 300 contigs will behave differently, and nothing above licenses a general claim about #emph[ab initio] prediction. Benchmarking would need a panel of genomes spanning GC content, assembly contiguity and taxonomic breadth.
- #strong[The reference is not truth.] RefSeq annotation is itself partly computational --- the NCBI pipeline uses a gene finder and then applies homology and curation \(Tatusova et al. 2016) --- so this is agreement between two methods that share assumptions, not accuracy against a gold standard. Only experimentally determined gene boundaries, from ribosome profiling or proteomics, would support the word "accuracy", and the disagreements in the MgPa region are exactly where the reference is least certain.
- #strong[Agreement was defined on stop coordinates.] That choice was deliberate and it shapes the headline figure. Scoring exact coordinate identity would have reported about 88% rather than 95.6%; scoring any overlap would have reported higher still. The metric is part of the result, which is why it must be in the abstract and not buried in the methods.
- #strong[Nothing here is about function.] Recovering a gene's coordinates says nothing about whether its product is correctly identified. Functional annotation is a separate pipeline --- protein similarity search \(Altschul et al. 1990), profile hidden Markov models against domain libraries \(Eddy 2011; Mistry et al. 2020) --- with its own and considerably higher error rate.
- #strong[No statistical uncertainty is attached.] With one genome there is no replication, so no confidence interval is available for any of these percentages. Resampling genes would measure sampling variation within this genome, not variation between genomes, and would be a misleading thing to report.

The honest one-sentence conclusion is therefore narrower than the one a press release would write: for compact bacterial genomes, #emph[ab initio] prediction is an adequate substitute for curated annotation when the translation table is correct and the question concerns the gene complement rather than exact protein termini, and it should not be trusted in repeat families or for genes under about 150 bp.

