== Writing it up, and a reviewer's self-check
<sec-15-writeup>
A project that is not written up has not happened. Three parts of the write-up are specific to computational work.

#strong[Figures carry the argument.] Each figure in this chapter answers one question: what went wrong (#ref(<fig-15-lengths>, supplement: [Figure])), why (#ref(<fig-15-tga>, supplement: [Figure])), how well the corrected analysis performs (#ref(<fig-15-starts>, supplement: [Figure]) and #ref(<tbl-15-comparison>, supplement: [Table])), and where the residual disagreement lives (#ref(<fig-15-map>, supplement: [Figure])). Before adding a figure, state in one sentence the claim it supports; if you cannot, the figure is decoration. Generate every figure from a script that reads the results files, never by hand from a spreadsheet, so that a corrected input regenerates a corrected figure --- which is precisely what the `figures/` rule in the Makefile guarantees.

#strong[A reproducible methods section names versions, parameters and data.] The version of this analysis's methods section would read: #emph["Protein-coding genes were predicted with Prodigal 2.6.3 \(Hyatt et al. 2010) in single-genome mode (`-p single`) under NCBI translation table 4 (`-g 4`) from the NC\_000908.2 assembly, and compared with the RefSeq annotation of the same assembly (GCF\_000027325.1) by 3' coordinate and strand. Analysis code is in the accompanying repository; the environment is pinned in `environment.yml`."] Every element of that sentence is checkable: a version, a mode, a flag, two accessions and a pointer to code. A methods section that says "genes were predicted using standard parameters" is not reproducible, and reviewers increasingly say so.

#strong[Data and code availability statements are commitments.] A usable statement names the archive, the accession and the licence for data, and the repository, the archived version identifier and the licence for code. Deposit code in a repository and archive it at a digital object identifier so that the version you describe cannot be silently rewritten. Note that FAIR does not require open: controlled-access human data is correctly described by naming the archive and the access procedure \(Wilkinson et al. 2016), as discussed in the ethics chapter.

=== The self-check to apply before anyone else does
<the-self-check-to-apply-before-anyone-else-does>
Run through this list before you submit. It is the list a competent reviewer works through, and every item has been violated by published papers.

+ #strong[Can a stranger regenerate every number?] Clone the repository into a new directory, create the environment from the specification, run `make clean && make`, and compare the output with the results in the manuscript. If it fails, the project is not finished.
+ #strong[Is every parameter in the paper?] Including the defaults you did not change, since a default is a version-dependent choice. The genetic code in this project was a default, and it was wrong.
+ #strong[Does the metric match the claim?] If the claim is about accuracy, the metric must compare against something that can be called truth. This project's metric supports a claim about agreement, and the abstract must say so.
+ #strong[Is the comparison fair?] Both annotations here derive from the same assembly, so no coordinate lift-over or assembly difference confounds the comparison. If your two datasets differ in reference build, read length, depth or batch, that difference is a rival explanation for your result.
+ #strong[Have you looked at the disagreements individually?] Aggregate statistics hide structure. Twenty minutes inspecting the 23 unmatched genes changed the interpretation of this project entirely.
+ #strong[What is the sample size, and of what?] One genome is n = 1 for claims about genomes, however many genes it contains. Pseudoreplication --- treating 524 genes as 524 independent observations about annotation methods --- is the most common statistical error in comparative genomics.
+ #strong[Could the result be an artefact of the pipeline?] Name the most plausible artefact explicitly and say what rules it out. Here, the most plausible was over-prediction in a GC-poor genome; the TGA analysis ruled it out.
+ #strong[Is the raw data still bit-identical?] Verify checksums against the manifest you recorded at the start. A silently corrupted or re-downloaded input invalidates everything downstream.
+ #strong[Does the limitations paragraph exist, and is it specific?] "Further work is needed" is not a limitation. #ref(<sec-15-interpretation>, supplement: [Section]) is the length a real one takes.
+ #strong[Who is credited, and for what?] Agree the contribution statement before submission, and cite the tools by their methods papers so that the people who wrote the software you depended on get the credit the citation system can measure.

== Summary
<sec-15-summary>
- A project becomes tractable when the interest is narrowed to a comparison, a measurable quantity, a dataset that can settle it, and a written statement of what an answer would look like.
- The choice of metric is a scientific decision. Scoring annotation agreement on stop coordinates rather than exact coordinates measures gene detection rather than start-site prediction, and the choice belongs in the abstract.
- A project directory with read-only raw data, disposable derived directories and numbered scripts prevents a class of errors that no amount of care prevents otherwise.
- Commit code, configuration and provenance; never commit derived results, large inputs or anything under controlled access. Commit messages explain why, because the diff already shows what.
- Pin software to patch versions and channels. A version range documents that you did not know what you ran.
- A Makefile records dependencies rather than order, so that only stale results are rebuilt and editing a script invalidates its output. `make clean && make` is a test of whether a project is reproducible.
- Silent analytical failure is the expensive kind: Prodigal exited zero, produced valid GFF3, and was wrong because the default translation table does not apply to #emph[Mycoplasma]. Build an independent check into every pipeline.
- Aggregate agreement conceals structure. In this genome the residual 5% is not spread evenly but concentrated in very short genes and in the MgPa adhesin repeat family, where both methods face genuine ambiguity.
- Stating what a result does not show --- one genome, a reference that is not truth, a metric that defines the headline number --- is part of the result, not a concession.

== Exercises
<sec-15-exercises>
+ Run Prodigal on the bundled genome under translation table 25 and compare the number of predicted coding sequences with the results for tables 4 and 11. Explain the direction of the difference from the stop codons each table defines.
+ The comparison in this chapter scored agreement on the 3' stop coordinate. Recompute sensitivity and precision for genetic code 4 using exact agreement on both coordinates and strand, and state how the headline figure changes.
+ Compute the GC content of the 26 unmatched predictions and compare it with the genome-wide value. Does composition distinguish them from genes both methods agree on?
+ The three reference genes that Prodigal missed entirely are short. Determine the length of the shortest coding sequence Prodigal did predict under code 4, and use it to estimate the effective length threshold of the method on this genome.
+ Write a `make` rule that produces `results/comparison_code25.tsv` without editing any existing rule, and explain which feature of the Makefile makes this possible.
+ The reference annotation contains 20 pseudogenes. Write code to test whether reference coding sequences that overlap a pseudogene are recovered less often than those that do not, and report the two recovery rates.
+ A colleague reports that an #emph[ab initio] predictor "achieves 95% accuracy on bacterial genomes" and proposes to replace curated annotation with it in a 400-genome survey. Write the three questions you would ask before agreeing, drawn from #ref(<sec-15-interpretation>, supplement: [Section]).
+ Take any analysis of your own and apply the ten-point self-check in #ref(<sec-15-writeup>, supplement: [Section]). Report which items you cannot currently satisfy and what each would take to fix.

