== What each chapter established
<sec-16-syntheses>
=== Chapter 1: What bioinformatics is, and how to set up to do it
<chapter-1-what-bioinformatics-is-and-how-to-set-up-to-do-it>
The opening chapter defined the discipline by what it produces: defensible biological statements extracted from large, noisy, machine-generated measurements, as distinct from computational biology, which produces methods and models. It argued that the scale is what forces computation, and did the arithmetic rather than asserting it --- a single human genome at 30-fold coverage with 150 bp reads is about 620 million reads and 206 GB of uncompressed FASTQ, some 2,400 times the row limit of a spreadsheet. It then placed the field's methodological eras against the data problem each one solved, and reduced almost every analysis to a five-stage shape in which each stage contains a decision that must be recorded in a script if the conclusion is to survive questioning months later. The chapter ended by building the environment the rest of the book uses and running a first real analysis: the SARS-CoV-2 reference genome at 29,903 bp and 38% GC, with long open reading frames concentrated on one strand. Everything that follows is a specialisation of that pattern --- a question, a decision, a record.

=== Chapter 2: Molecular biology for people who analyse data
<chapter-2-molecular-biology-for-people-who-analyse-data>
This chapter supplied the biology the algorithms encode, and taught it through the errors it prevents. Strandedness comes first, because a FASTA record stores one strand and says nothing about which, so the reverse complement --- not the complement --- is what every format expects. Translation never fails, which is why a plausible-looking protein is no evidence of a correct reading frame; the diagnostics are an initial methionine, a single terminal stop and no internal stops. Testing a naive open reading frame scan against the SARS-CoV-2 annotation made the point quantitatively: a 100-codon threshold recovers the long genes, misses four real ones and produces a chance hit on the empty strand, while lowering it to 38 codons finds all eleven genes at the cost of growing the output from 9 predictions to 76. The #emph[TP53] worked example --- roughly 19 kb of genomic span, a 2,512 nt mRNA, an 1,182 nt coding sequence and a 393-residue protein identical to UniProt P04637 --- establishes both the central dogma and the cheapest available test of a nucleotide-to-protein pipeline. Coordinates close the chapter, because 1-based inclusive and 0-based half-open systems never announce themselves.

=== Chapter 3: Biological databases and how to get data out of them
<chapter-3-biological-databases-and-how-to-get-data-out-of-them>
Here the book moved from biology to the records that represent it, and to the question of how much a record can be trusted. Resources divide into primary archives, which store what was submitted; curated knowledgebases, which store conclusions with evidence; and derived resources, which compute --- and the kind of resource determines the sentence you are entitled to write about it. Accession discipline follows: INSDC mirroring makes GenBank, ENA and DDBJ one archive under one set of accessions; prefixes encode provenance, with `NM_`/`NP_` curated and `XM_`/`XP_` machine-predicted, and `GCA_` an author's assembly against `GCF_` the RefSeq copy; and the digits after the full stop are a version, without which an accession does not identify data. Dissecting the UniProt entry for p53 showed what curation buys --- 238 papers read, 1,120 cross-references and more than 1,500 features on a protein of 393 residues --- and established the chapter's most portable habit: read the evidence code before the annotation, because `IDA` and `IMP` are experiments while `IEA` and `ISS` are hypotheses. Programmatic access and recorded provenance --- accession, version, date, checksum --- make the rest of the book repeatable.

=== Chapter 4: Sequence alignment and what it tells you
<chapter-4-sequence-alignment-and-what-it-tells-you>
Alignment is the field's central algorithmic idea, and the chapter built it from the recursion up. Similarity is measured and homology is inferred, so no sequence is ever "40% homologous" to another; orthologues arise by speciation and usually keep their function, paralogues by duplication and need not. Dynamic programming gives an exact optimum under a stated scoring scheme, and the worked score matrix and traceback show what that optimum is made of. The chapter then demonstrated, rather than asserted, that the scoring scheme matters: the same distant globin pair aligns over 142 columns at 27.5% identity under BLOSUM62 or PAM250, but collapses to 101 columns at an apparently better 48.5% identity under PAM30, so a matrix chosen for the wrong evolutionary distance silently shortens an alignment instead of failing. BLAST trades optimality for speed, and its statistics carry the same lesson --- the bit score of an alignment is 44.3 whatever database it was found in, while the E-value scales with database size. Multiple alignment of eight p53 orthologues localised conservation to the DNA-binding domain, and a bootstrapped tree showed which clades the data actually resolve and which, at 18% and 34% support, they do not.

=== Chapter 5: The command line as a working environment
<chapter-5-the-command-line-as-a-working-environment>
The toolchain chapters begin with the observation that bioinformatics data are mostly line-oriented text files too large to open. The shell is presented as a programming environment organised around one idea --- programs read a stream, write a stream, and pipes compose them --- so that memory use stays constant however large the file. The text-processing core of `grep`, `cut`, `sort`, `uniq`, `sed`, `awk` and `join` is taught against the formats the book uses, and each idiom arrives with the specific silent error it prevents: sorting before `uniq -c`, anchoring a regular expression, checking row counts across a `join`, which is how the chapter discovers that 30 genes produce 31 joined rows because #emph[CDKN2A] has two reviewed UniProt entries. An `awk` example that computes a perfectly plausible and entirely wrong GC content of 46.42%, through nothing worse than putting two statements in the wrong order, makes the chapter's point about silent failure. Compression is treated as streaming rather than storage, with BGZF and `tabix` turning a compressed VCF into a randomly accessible one, and the chapter closes on exit status, `set -euo pipefail`, defensive scripts and SLURM job arrays.

=== Chapter 6: Python for bioinformatics
<chapter-6-python-for-bioinformatics>
This chapter turns the reader from a user of tools into an author of them. It covers the language core --- types, collections, control flow, functions, files --- with every example drawn from sequence data, and insists on two habits: choose the collection from the question (a list for order, a dictionary for lookup, a set for membership) and convert coordinates in one tested place rather than in your head, because Python is 0-based and half-open while GFF3, VCF and GenBank are 1-based and closed. Biopython's `SeqRecord` and `feature.extract` then remove the hand-arithmetic entirely, which the chapter verifies by translating the SARS-CoV-2 spike feature and recovering a 1,273-residue protein beginning MFVFLVLLPLVS. NumPy earns its place on a 20,000 × 150 quality matrix, where the mean Phred score falls from Q37.2 at the first cycle to Q23.7 at the last and crosses Q30 at cycle 111 --- the kind of summary that decides a trimming parameter. pandas turns a GFF3 or a VCF into a table, and the chapter ends where professional code begins: two assertions are a test suite, and a module with a `__main__` guard is a tool.

=== Chapter 7: R and Bioconductor
<chapter-7-r-and-bioconductor>
R enters where statistics and figures become the task. The chapter's organising claim is that R's design removes loops and index errors by applying operations to whole vectors --- and that the same design produces the book's most dangerous silent error, because recycling repeats a short operand down the columns of a matrix, so dividing a count matrix by a vector of library sizes returns plausible nonsense rather than an error. Factors, coercion and the tibble--data frame distinction are treated in the same spirit: each is a category of wrong answer rather than a language feature. The grammar of graphics is taught by debugging a figure, taking an unreadable linear-scale boxplot of counts spanning five orders of magnitude to a log scale and then to facets that reveal library protocol, not treatment, as the dominant source of variation in the pasilla data. Bioconductor's value is located in its shared classes: `Biostrings`, `GRanges` with `rtracklayer`, and above all `SummarizedExperiment`, which makes the commonest serious bug in expression analysis --- sample annotation detached from its counts --- impossible to commit. A three-line differential test closes it: 1,547 genes pass an uncorrected threshold against roughly 407 expected by chance, and 195 survive Benjamini--Hochberg correction.

=== Chapter 8: From assembled sequence to annotated genome
<chapter-8-from-assembled-sequence-to-annotated-genome>
With sequence, tools and languages in hand, the book turns to interpretation. An assembly is a hypothesis, and the chapter checks contiguity, completeness and contamination before annotating anything, noting that N50 measures contiguity rather than correctness and rewards over-aggressive assembly. Structural annotation of #emph[Mycoplasmoides genitalium] then delivers the chapter's central lesson about parameters: under the bacterial default, genetic code 11, Prodigal predicts 995 genes where the correct code 4 predicts 527, and nothing in the output signals the error. Comparing the prediction with the RefSeq annotation of the same assembly shows what #emph[ab initio] prediction is and is not good at --- 464 genes agree on both coordinates, 37 share a stop but not a start, 23 are found only by RefSeq and 26 only by Prodigal --- and explains the asymmetry: a stop codon is a consequence of the reading frame while a start is a prediction. Eukaryotic prediction needs external evidence because introns make the problem underdetermined. Functional annotation is inference by similarity with an invisible inference chain, which is why the chapter ends by reading evidence codes: all 985 Gene Ontology annotations in this genome are `IEA`, and about a tenth of the proteome remains unannotated after thirty years.

=== Chapter 9: Next-generation sequencing data from reads to results
<chapter-9-next-generation-sequencing-data-from-reads-to-results>
This chapter connects an instrument to a result and insists that each stage is a hypothesis rather than a view of the same thing: a read is an observation, an alignment an inference about origin, a variant call an inference about genotype. It relates each chemistry to the errors it makes, separates library artefacts from biology, and builds a quality report from the bundled reads rather than trusting a tool's traffic lights. Trimming is quantified rather than recommended: at Q25 the data loses 11% of its bases for no gain in an alignment-based analysis. Alignment introduces SAM in detail --- FLAG, CIGAR and MAPQ --- and the sharpest demonstration uses simulated reads whose true origin is known: 25 of 40,000 reads are placed in the wrong location, 38 carry MAPQ 0 in the MgPa adhesin repeats, and MAPQ 0 means "equally good elsewhere" rather than "bad read". The RNA-seq half runs a full DESeq2 analysis of the pasilla experiment, recovering 1,108 genes at an adjusted #emph[p]-value below 0.05 and #emph[pasilla] itself down about 3.7-fold, and ends on what a 5% false discovery rate is a property of: the list, not the gene.

=== Chapter 10: Protein structure and what you can compute from it
<chapter-10-protein-structure-and-what-you-can-compute-from-it>
Structure is where sequence becomes mechanism, and the chapter treats a structure as a measurement with error bars. Judging one means reading resolution, R factors, B factors and missing residues --- 1TUP at 2.20 Å reports no R-free because it was deposited in 1995, and 72 of its residues are modelled nowhere, recorded in `REMARK 465` where code that reads only `ATOM` lines will never see them. Bio.PDB's structure--model--chain--residue--atom hierarchy makes geometry a few lines of code, and the geometry is biology: the p53 zinc is held tetrahedrally by Cys176, His179, Cys238 and Cys242 at 1.99--2.33 Å without touching DNA, while only chain B is bound in the major groove, through twelve residues within 4 Å of it. Superposition supplies the chapter's most transferable number --- three copies of the same domain in one crystal differ by 0.78--1.03 Å, which is the scale against which any other RMSD must be read. The AlphaFold model reproduces that domain to 0.73 Å over 194 Cα atoms and scores p53's disordered regions below 50, which is a correct prediction rather than a failure; high predicted aligned error between two confident domains means their relative arrangement is not predicted at all.

=== Chapter 11: Biological networks and pathway analysis
<chapter-11-biological-networks-and-pathway-analysis>
Having treated genes one at a time, the book turns to their relations, and spends most of its effort on what an edge actually asserts. In the bundled 30-gene STRING slice every edge has text-mining support, 176 of 210 have experimental support and only 85 appear in a curated database, so a functional association is not a physical interaction and filtering by evidence channel is usually the right first move. The chapter then measures the network --- 30 nodes, 210 edges, density 0.483, clustering coefficient 0.646 --- and immediately disowns the measurements as properties of a query-driven selection rather than of the interactome. The same scepticism applies to hubs: #emph[TP53] has degree 29 and five times the betweenness of any other gene, which in a network built by querying for p53-associated genes is no discovery, and degree correlates with annotation count at Spearman 0.746, so hub status partly measures study intensity. Community detection always returns communities, so low modularity and disagreement between algorithms are the signal. Over-representation analysis is then built from one hypergeometric test per term, corrected with Benjamini--Hochberg, and the closing demonstration is that matching the background universe for study intensity cuts the significant-term count from 511 to 121.

=== Chapter 12: Statistics and machine learning on omics data
<chapter-12-statistics-and-machine-learning-on-omics-data>
This chapter supplies the statistical reasoning the rest of the book consumes, and does it by breaking things deliberately. It distinguishes biological from technical replication, establishes that an omics matrix has far more features than samples --- 14,599 genes against seven pasilla samples --- and shows what that guarantees: some feature always separates any two groups. Normalisation is demonstrated rather than described, with the median log ratio between two control samples falling from 1.443, the log ratio of their library sizes, to 0.056 after conversion to counts per million. Batch effects are shown imitating biology: principal components place treatment on PC1 at 39.7% of variance and library protocol on PC2 at 31.8%, and in a deliberately confounded contrast 22.3% of the significant genes are also significant in a pure protocol comparison against 7.8% expected. Multiple testing is calibrated against simulation --- 10,000 null tests give 501 results at #emph[p] \< 0.05 and none after correction --- and the most important experiment permutes the labels: selecting features before cross-validation reports perfect accuracy in 100 of 100 permutations of meaningless labels, while selecting inside each fold reports 0.384, below the majority-class baseline, which is the truthful answer.

=== Chapter 13: Bioinformatics in disease and healthcare
<chapter-13-bioinformatics-in-disease-and-healthcare>
Here the book's methods meet the clinic, and the chapter's discipline is to separate claims by the evidence behind them. Germline and somatic variants differ in calling strategy, expected allele fraction and consent obligations; drivers are separated from passengers by comparison against a background mutation rate that varies by orders of magnitude; mutational signatures describe the chemistry that produced a mutation and not its consequence. The bundled ClinVar slice of 3,888 #emph[TP53] records supplies the clinical reality: about a quarter are variants of uncertain significance, only 191 carry expert-panel review and 192 carry no assertion criteria at all, so review status matters more than the label. Two computed results carry the chapter. Truncating variants are classified pathogenic 81.4% of the time against 16.9% for missense variants, which is the empirical content of the ACMG/AMP criterion PVS1. And 94.1% of pathogenic missense variants fall in the DNA-binding domain at residues 102--292, against 26.1% of benign ones and the 48.6% expected from its length. The chapter closes on the distinction between pathogenic and actionable, on pharmacogenomic guidelines, and on pathogen surveillance, which is limited by sampling and reporting far more often than by sequencing.

=== Chapter 14: Working responsibly with genomic data
<chapter-14-working-responsibly-with-genomic-data>
This chapter argues that the obligations attached to human genomic data follow from four properties of the data itself: it identifies, it cannot be revoked, it implicates relatives who never consented, and it predicts weakly but is read strongly. Two simulations make the first property quantitative. Twenty-five independent common markers make every one of 10,000 simulated individuals unique, and the random match probability falls below one in eight billion at 30 markers, which is why a variant file is identifiable data whatever has been stripped from its header. A reimplementation of the allele-frequency membership statistic then detects 90% of 200 pool contributors from 5,000 markers with no false positives, showing that published summary statistics are not automatically safe and that the attack strengthens as panels densify. From that mechanical basis the chapter works through consent models and the problem of enforcing them in code, controlled access and the five safes, the structure of GDPR, HIPAA, Nigeria's data protection regime and the Nagoya Protocol, the clinical cost of the European-ancestry bias in reference data, and the design decisions --- benefit sharing, authorship, governance --- that separate collaboration from extraction. Its practical residue is a checklist applied before a project starts.

=== Chapter 15: A complete project, start to finish
<chapter-15-a-complete-project-start-to-finish>
The capstone assembles everything into one analysis: how much of a curated bacterial annotation does #emph[ab initio] prediction recover? It builds the infrastructure first --- read-only raw data, disposable derived directories, a pinned environment, a Git history whose messages record decisions, and a Makefile that rebuilds only what is stale --- and then runs an analysis that fails. Prodigal's default translation table treats TGA as a stop codon, which 71.6% of #emph[M. genitalium] genes contain internally, so the first run returns 995 predictions with a median length of 336 bp against 856 bp in the reference, recovering 96.6% of TGA-free genes but only 52.5% of the rest. Diagnosing that against an independent annotation, rather than from an error message, is the chapter's central lesson. Under the correct code the comparison recovers 95.6% of reference coding sequences at 95.1% precision with 92.6% of starts identical, and inspecting the residual disagreements shows them concentrated in very short genes and in the MgPa adhesin repeat family, where neither method is simply right. It closes on write-up: the metric belongs in the abstract, the limitations are part of the result, and a reviewer's checklist is cheaper applied to yourself.

== Question bank: foundations and data
<sec-16-bank-1-4>
Each question is labelled by the kind of thinking it requires. #strong[Recall] questions test whether a definition or fact is available to you. #strong[Application] questions require you to use a method on a specific case. #strong[Interpretation] questions give you a result and ask what it means or whether it is trustworthy; these are the ones that matter most in practice and the ones most often skipped. Attempt each before opening the answers.

#strong[Chapter 1: What bioinformatics is, and how to set up to do it]

+ #emph[\(recall)] State the distinction Chapter 1 draws between bioinformatics and computational biology, in terms of what each produces.
+ #emph[\(recall)] Name the five stages common to almost every sequence analysis, and state what all five have in common.
+ #emph[\(application)] A 30-fold human genome sequenced with 150 bp reads produces about 620 million reads and 206 GB of uncompressed FASTQ. Show the arithmetic, and explain why the comparison with a spreadsheet's row limit is a statement about operations as much as about size.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] A published analysis reports a result but gives neither software versions nor parameters. State what specifically cannot be checked, and why "the method is standard" is not an adequate defence.

#strong[Chapter 2: Molecular biology for people who analyse data]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 5)
+ #emph[\(recall)] Define reading frame, and state how many exist for a double-stranded DNA sequence.
+ #emph[\(recall)] Why is a plausible-looking translated protein not evidence that the reading frame was correct, and what three features should you check instead?
+ #emph[\(application)] A single-base deletion occurs early in the #emph[TP53] coding sequence. Predict the consequence for the protein product and justify it from the triplet nature of translation.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] Scanning the SARS-CoV-2 genome for open reading frames of at least 100 codons recovers the long genes but misses four annotated ones and reports a hit on the strand that carries no genes. Lowering the threshold to 38 codons finds all eleven genes but raises the output from 9 predictions to 76. State what this trade-off is called and how you would choose a threshold.
]

#strong[Chapter 3: Biological databases and how to get data out of them]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 9)
+ #emph[\(recall)] Distinguish a primary archive, a curated knowledgebase and a derived resource, naming one of each.
+ #emph[\(recall)] What do the digits after the full stop in `NM_000546.6` specify, and what is versioned differently in an Ensembl identifier such as `ENST00000269305.9`?
+ #emph[\(application)] You need the canonical human p53 sequence and the boundaries of its DNA-binding domain. State which resource you would query, where in the record the boundaries sit, and what you would record as provenance.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] Two functional annotations on the same protein carry the evidence codes `IDA` and `IEA`. State what each means, which you would build a conclusion on, and what you would check before relying on the weaker one.
]

#strong[Chapter 4: Sequence alignment and what it tells you]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 13)
+ #emph[\(recall)] State the difference between global and local alignment, name an algorithm for each, and give the biological question that selects each one.
+ #emph[\(recall)] Explain what an affine gap penalty models that a linear penalty does not.
+ #emph[\(application)] The same distant globin pair aligns over 142 columns at 27.5% identity under BLOSUM62 and over 101 columns at 48.5% identity under PAM30. Explain why the higher identity is the worse result, and state which matrix you would report.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] A BLAST search of myoglobin against a small protein database returns human haemoglobin alpha at 25.7% identity over 148 residues with an E-value of 9.18 × 10⁻¹⁰, and several p53 entries matching over 8 to 27 residues with E-values between 0.47 and 9.7. State which hits are evidence of homology, and why percentage identity is the weaker guide.
]

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
#strong[\1.] Bioinformatics converts large, noisy, machine-generated measurements into defensible biological statements about a particular sample; computational biology produces the methods and models that make such conversions possible. The distinction is in the product, not in the tools, and it determines how the work is judged --- correctness, reproducibility and relevance to the sample in hand for the first, generality and correctness of the method for the second.

#strong[\2.] Acquiring or retrieving the data; assessing its quality; reducing or transforming it; comparing it with a reference or between groups; and interpreting the comparison. What they have in common is that every stage contains a decision --- a threshold, a reference, a parameter --- which must be recorded in a script, because the question asked months later is whether the conclusion survives changing it.

#strong[\3.] A 3.1 Gb genome at 30-fold coverage is about 93 Gb of sequence; divided by 150 bp that is roughly 620 million reads, and at about 332 bytes per FASTQ record that is some 206 GB. The spreadsheet comparison matters less for the row count than for the operations: aligning each read to a 3.1 Gb reference, modelling base distributions under an error profile, and testing 20,000 genes with false discovery control are not row-by-row arithmetic, so no amount of spreadsheet capacity would help.

#strong[\4.] Without versions you cannot know whether the tool's defaults, algorithms or bug fixes differ from yours, and without parameters you cannot reproduce the numbers at all; the result is unverifiable rather than merely inconvenient. "Standard" is no defence because defaults change between versions and because the parameter that mattered most in this book --- the translation table in Chapters 8 and 15 --- was itself a default.

#strong[\5.] A reading frame is one of the ways a nucleotide sequence can be partitioned into consecutive triplets, fixed by the position of the first base read. There are six for double-stranded DNA: three on each strand.

#strong[\6.] Because translation never fails --- any sequence of bases yields a sequence of amino acids, so the output always looks like a protein. Check for an initial methionine, a single stop at the end, and no internal stops; a frame-shifted translation typically fails the last two.

#strong[\7.] The deletion shifts the frame for every downstream codon, so the sequence after it is wrong and a premature stop appears quickly. In the chapter's worked example ten correct residues are followed by a short stretch belonging to no p53 and then a stop --- 13 amino acids in place of 393, from one base in a 1,182 nt coding sequence. The transcript may also be destroyed by nonsense-mediated decay.

#strong[\8.] It is the sensitivity--precision trade-off: lowering the threshold recovers the short genes (sensitivity) and admits many chance frames and alternative starts (precision), since roughly six of every seven predictions at 38 codons are not new genes. Choose the threshold from what the prediction is for --- a high threshold when you will inspect every hit by hand, a low one when the predictions feed a downstream filter such as homology search --- and state the threshold with the result.

#strong[\9.] A primary archive such as GenBank stores records as submitted, with the submitter responsible for their content; a curated knowledgebase such as UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot or RefSeq records conclusions with evidence attached; a derived resource computes something from the others, such as a genome browser's alignment tracks or a structure prediction database.

#strong[\10.] In an INSDC or RefSeq nucleotide accession the version increments when the sequence changes, so `NM_000546.5` and `NM_000546.6` may be different strings of bases. Ensembl versions genes and transcripts separately from the assembly and increments the suffix when the #emph[model] changes, so the same genomic sequence can carry a new transcript version.

#strong[\11.] Query UniProtKB for P04637. The domain boundaries are in the feature table of the flat file, as the `DNA_BIND 102..292` line, with its own evidence attribution. Record the accession, the entry version or release date, the date of retrieval and a checksum of the downloaded file, because UniProt annotations change.

#strong[\12.] `IDA` is inferred from direct assay --- an experiment on this protein --- while `IEA` is inferred from electronic annotation, transferred automatically from another record without curator review. Build on the `IDA` annotation. Before relying on the `IEA` one, find what it was transferred from, check that the similarity covers the functionally relevant region, and look for direct experimental evidence; an electronic annotation is a hypothesis about function, not an observation of it.

#strong[\13.] Global alignment aligns two sequences along their entire length (Needleman--Wunsch) and suits sequences expected to correspond throughout, such as two orthologues of the same protein. Local alignment finds the best-scoring segment pair (Smith--Waterman) and suits the search for a shared domain in otherwise unrelated sequences.

#strong[\14.] That an insertion or deletion is a single evolutionary event of some length, rather than a series of independent single-residue events. A linear penalty charges the same for each gap position and therefore scatters many short gaps where one long gap belongs; an affine penalty charges a large opening cost and a small extension cost, which recovers the single event.

#strong[\15.] PAM30 is built for sequences that have barely diverged, so it penalises the substitutions that actually occur between distant homologues and the aligner responds by truncating the alignment to a short, well-matching core. The identity rises because the denominator shrank, not because the sequences are more similar. Report the BLOSUM62 or PAM250 result --- 142 columns at 27.5% --- and report identity with the aligned length attached, since identity alone is uninterpretable.

#strong[\16.] Only the haemoglobin alpha hit. The E-value accounts for alignment length and database size, so 9.18 × 10⁻¹⁰ over 148 residues is strong evidence of homology even at 25.7% identity, which sits in the twilight zone where real homologues and convincing accidents coexist. The p53 matches over 8 to 27 residues have E-values close to or above 1, meaning roughly one such hit is expected per search by chance. Percentage identity ignores both the length of the alignment and the size of the search space.

]
