== Question bank: the toolchain
<sec-16-bank-5-7>
#strong[Chapter 5: The command line as a working environment]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 17)
+ #emph[\(recall)] Explain what a pipe does, and why it allows processing of files much larger than available memory.
+ #emph[\(recall)] State the difference between `>` and `>>`, and the specific data loss that confusing them causes.
+ #emph[\(application)] Write a one-line pipeline that counts the sequences in a FASTA file, and explain why counting lines beginning with `>` is correct whereas counting all lines is not.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] An `awk` program reports the GC content of a read file as 46.42%, a plausible figure, when the true value is near 31.7%. The program removes the newline before accumulating base counts but after accumulating the total length. Explain the error, and state the general lesson about this class of bug.
]

#strong[Chapter 6: Python for bioinformatics]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 21)
+ #emph[\(recall)] State what `SeqIO.parse` returns and how it differs from `SeqIO.read`.
+ #emph[\(recall)] Python slices are 0-based and half-open; GFF3, VCF and GenBank coordinates are 1-based and inclusive. Give the Python slice for the GFF3 feature `686..1828`, and state the rule.
+ #emph[\(application)] Write a function returning the GC content of a sequence that handles an empty sequence without raising. State what it should return and justify the choice.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] The mean per-cycle quality of the bundled reads falls from Q37.2 to Q23.7 and crosses Q30 at cycle 111, while the tenth percentile crosses it at cycle 83. State which of these two numbers should inform a trimming decision and why.
]

#strong[Chapter 7: R and Bioconductor]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 25)
+ #emph[\(recall)] State what recycling is in R, and the direction in which it operates on a matrix.
+ #emph[\(recall)] What three behaviours does a tibble change relative to a data frame, and what class of error does each prevent?
+ #emph[\(application)] You have a counts matrix and a separate sample table. Describe the checks to perform before any analysis, and the single assertion that must hold between them.
+ #emph[\(application)] Explain how `pivot_longer` changes the shape of a 14,599-gene by 7-sample count matrix, why `ggplot2` needs that shape, and why Bioconductor assays need the opposite one.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] A boxplot of per-sample gene counts on a linear axis shows flattened boxes and a column of outliers. State the diagnosis and the single change that fixes it, and say why changing the geometry would not.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] Faceting the pasilla count distributions by library protocol shows medians differing by roughly a factor of two between panels, while the four distributions within each panel are closely matched. State what this identifies, and what it implies for any differential expression model fitted to these samples.
]

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
#strong[\17.] A pipe connects one program's standard output to the next program's standard input, so records stream through the chain one at a time. Because no stage holds the whole file, memory use stays roughly constant regardless of file size.

#strong[\18.] `>` truncates the target file before writing; `>>` appends. Confusing them destroys the previous contents irrecoverably, which is why raw data directories are made read-only with `chmod`.

#strong[\19.] `grep -c "^>" sequences.fasta`. Only header lines start with `>`, and each record has exactly one header, whereas sequence lines are wrapped at an arbitrary width, so a line count measures formatting rather than content. The `^` anchor matters: without it the count would include any line containing `>`.

#strong[\20.] The length accumulator sees the line with its trailing newline and the base counts do not, so every line contributes one phantom base to the denominator; with 150 bp lines the inflation is about 0.7% of the total and the GC percentage shifts by a plausible amount. The lesson is that this class of error produces a number in the right range rather than a crash, so a pipeline needs an external check --- here, the genome's own known GC content of 31.7% --- rather than a plausibility judgement.

#strong[\21.] `SeqIO.parse` returns an iterator over all records in a file and suits a file with any number of sequences; `SeqIO.read` returns the single record in a file and raises an error if there is not exactly one. Use `read` when "exactly one record" is a requirement you want enforced.

#strong[\22.] `genome[685:1828]`. Subtract one from the start and leave the end unchanged; the half-open upper bound and the inclusive end coincide numerically, which is why only the start appears to move.

#strong[\23.] It should return a documented sentinel such as `None` or `float("nan")`, not zero and not an exception, because an empty sequence has no GC content and 0% would enter a downstream average as though it were a measurement.

```
def gc_fraction_safe(sequence):
    """GC content as a fraction, or None for an empty sequence."""
    if len(sequence) == 0:
        return None
    gc = sum(1 for base in sequence.upper() if base in "GC")
    return gc / len(sequence)
```

#strong[\24.] The tenth percentile, at cycle 83. The mean describes the typical read, but mismatches are contributed by the worst reads, and a cycle at which a tenth of reads have already fallen below the accuracy threshold is adding more error than information. Trimming decisions are made from the lower edge of the distribution, not from its centre.

#strong[\25.] Recycling repeats the shorter operand until it matches the length of the longer one, silently and without warning when the lengths divide evenly. A matrix in R is a vector with a dimension attribute filled column by column, so a short vector recycles #emph[down the columns] --- which is why dividing a gene-by-sample count matrix by a vector of per-sample library sizes scales genes rather than samples and returns a plausible, wrong answer.

#strong[\26.] No partial matching of column names, which prevents a typo silently selecting the wrong column; no dropping to a vector when a single column is selected, which prevents code that expects a table receiving something else; and no silent conversion of character columns to factors, which prevents the whole class of factor-related errors.

#strong[\27.] Check that the number of samples equals the number of matrix columns, that the two identifier sets are identical, and that they are in the same order; then reorder the sample table by the matrix column names rather than assuming correspondence. The assertion that must hold is that sample-table row names equal matrix column names element by element --- or, better, build a `SummarizedExperiment`, which enforces it permanently.

#strong[\28.] `pivot_longer` turns the 14,599 × 7 wide matrix into roughly 102,000 rows of three columns --- gene, sample, count --- one row per observation. `ggplot2` needs this because every aesthetic maps to a column, so "sample" must be a variable rather than a set of column names. Bioconductor assays need the wide form because the matrix is the computational object: linear algebra, normalisation and model fitting all operate on genes × samples.

#strong[\29.] The counts span five orders of magnitude, so a linear axis gives almost all its height to a few thousand very large values and compresses everything else onto the axis. Change the scale to logarithmic; the geometry is not the problem, and replacing the boxplot with a violin or a histogram would produce an equally unreadable figure on the same axis.

#strong[\30.] It identifies library protocol, not treatment, as the dominant source of variation --- a batch effect of larger magnitude than the biological effect. Since protocol is recorded and is not perfectly confounded with condition in this dataset, it must appear in the design formula as a covariate, so that the condition term is estimated after accounting for it; fitting condition alone would attribute protocol variation to treatment and inflate the dispersion estimates.

