== Question bank: genomes, reads and structures
<sec-16-bank-8-10>
#strong[Chapter 8: From assembled sequence to annotated genome]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 31)
+ #emph[\(recall)] Distinguish structural from functional annotation, and name one tool used for each.
+ #emph[\(recall)] State what the nine columns of a GFF3 record contain, and which attributes make a feature's relationship to another explicit.
+ #emph[\(application)] You have a new bacterial assembly and no annotation. Give the order of steps to a first-pass annotation, and say at which step the translation table must be settled and why.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] Comparing Prodigal with the RefSeq annotation of the same genome gives 464 genes identical at both ends, 37 sharing a stop but not a start, 23 found only by RefSeq and 26 only by Prodigal. Explain why the disagreements concentrate in start codons rather than in gene presence, and what the 23 and the 26 mostly turn out to be.
]

#strong[Chapter 9: Next-generation sequencing data from reads to results]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 35)
+ #emph[\(recall)] State what a Phred score of 30 means, and how the score is encoded in a FASTQ quality line.
+ #emph[\(recall)] Name the eleven mandatory SAM fields' three most frequently misread members --- FLAG, CIGAR and MAPQ --- and state what each encodes.
+ #emph[\(application)] Given paired FASTQ files, give the commands that produce a sorted, indexed BAM, and state why coordinate sorting must precede indexing.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] `samtools flagstat` on the bundled simulated alignment reports 40,000 reads, 100% mapped and 39,968 properly paired. Say what each figure would mean for a real bacterial isolate, and which of the three you would be most suspicious of here.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] Thirty-eight of 40,000 reads carry MAPQ 0, clustered around two positions, and 25 reads are placed somewhere other than where they were simulated from. Explain what MAPQ 0 means, why the two observations are related, and what filtering on MAPQ 20 would do to a variant call in that region.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] The DESeq2 analysis of the pasilla data returns 1,108 genes at an adjusted #emph[p]-value below 0.05, and recovers #emph[pasilla] itself as down about 3.7-fold. State precisely what the 5% false discovery rate promises about this list, and what it promises about any one gene in it.
]

#strong[Chapter 10: Protein structure and what you can compute from it]

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 41)
+ #emph[\(recall)] State what the B-factor column reports in a crystal structure, and what occupies the same column in an AlphaFold model.
+ #emph[\(recall)] Where in a PDB file are residues that were not modelled recorded, and why will code that reads only `ATOM` records miss them?
+ #emph[\(application)] Describe how you would determine which residues of a protein chain contact a bound DNA molecule, including the atom selection and the cut-off, and what you would exclude from both sets.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] Three copies of the p53 DNA-binding domain in one crystal differ by 0.78--1.03 Å over about 195 Cα atoms, and the AlphaFold model matches one of them to 0.73 Å over 194 Cα. State what the first range is for, and what the second number does and does not establish.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] An AlphaFold model has two regions with pLDDT above 90 and a high predicted aligned error between them. State what each measure reports and which statements the model does and does not support.
+ #emph[\(interpretation)] The radius of gyration of the p53 DNA-binding domain alone is 16.6 Å, while that of the full-length AlphaFold model is 33.5 Å. Explain what the difference reflects, and why the full-length figure should not be quoted as a property of the protein.
]

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
#strong[\31.] Structural annotation locates features --- genes, exons, RNA genes, repeats --- in an assembly; Prodigal performs it for bacteria and AUGUSTUS for eukaryotes. Functional annotation assigns biological meaning to those features; domain search against Pfam with HMMER, or InterProScan, performs it.

#strong[\32.] Sequence identifier, source, feature type, start, end, score, strand, phase and attributes. The `ID` and `Parent` attributes in the ninth column express the hierarchy, making a CDS a child of a transcript and a transcript a child of a gene; a spliced feature occupies several lines sharing one `ID`.

#strong[\33.] Assess the assembly --- contiguity, completeness against a conserved single-copy gene set, contamination --- then mask repeats, then predict coding sequences, then predict RNA genes with specialised tools, then transfer functional annotation by domain and orthology search, and finally assess the annotation before release. The translation table must be settled before gene prediction, because it determines which codons terminate an open reading frame: on #emph[M. genitalium] the bacterial default produces 995 predicted genes against 527 under the correct code 4, and nothing in the output signals the error.

#strong[\34.] Because a stop codon is a consequence of the reading frame --- once the frame and the 3' end are right, both methods must agree --- whereas a start codon is a prediction made from a ribosome binding site model among several in-frame candidates within the first 60 bp. Both unmatched groups turn out to be the same handful of loci seen from two sides. Nineteen of the 23 RefSeq-only genes coincide with annotated pseudogenes, which homology evidence supports but which carry a frameshift or internal stop that an #emph[ab initio] model will not call; and 20 of those same 23 do overlap a Prodigal prediction, so they are boundary disagreements rather than genes the predictor failed to see at all. Correspondingly, 19 of the 26 Prodigal-only predictions overlap one of the 20 annotated pseudogene loci. Only three reference genes are absent from the prediction entirely, and the shortest of them, a 147 bp ribosomal protein gene, shows where the length sensitivity of #emph[ab initio] prediction runs out.

#strong[\35.] A Phred score of 30 means an estimated one-in-1,000 probability that the base call is wrong, since $Q = - 10 log_10 P$. It is stored as a single ASCII character whose code point is $Q + 33$, so $Q = 0$ is `!` and $Q = 40$ is `I`\; files from Illumina pipelines before version 1.8 used an offset of 64 instead, which makes the same character mean a score 31 points higher.

#strong[\36.] FLAG is a bit field recording pairing, strand, mate strand, mate mapping, duplicate status and whether the alignment is secondary or supplementary. CIGAR describes how the read aligns --- matches, insertions, deletions, soft clipping --- and therefore determines where the alignment ends, which the read length alone does not. MAPQ is the Phred-scaled confidence that the read was placed in the right location.

#strong[\37.] Align with `bwa mem` piped into `samtools view` to produce BAM, sort with `samtools sort`, then index with `samtools index`. The index maps genomic coordinates to file offsets so a region query can seek directly to the right block; that mapping only exists if the records are stored in coordinate order.

#strong[\38.] For a real bacterial isolate, 95--99% mapped is expected and anything below about 70% calls for taxonomic classification of the unmapped reads; the properly paired figure adds the constraint that mates are in the expected orientation and separation, and a low value points at adapter or insert-size problems. All three figures here are suspicious because they are too good: 100% mapped and 99.92% properly paired are the signature of reads simulated from the same reference, as is the insert size of exactly 349 bp with zero standard deviation.

#strong[\39.] MAPQ 0 means the read aligns equally well in at least two places and the aligner reported one of them arbitrarily --- it is a statement about the reference, not about read quality. The two observations are the same phenomenon: the ambiguous reads sit in copies of the MgPa adhesin repeat family, and a read whose true origin was one copy can be placed in the other. Filtering at MAPQ 20 discards those alignments, which is usually correct because a variant supported only by ambiguously placed reads is an artefact; the cost is that genuine variants in the repeat become invisible rather than wrong, and that difference must be stated in the methods.

#strong[\40.] It promises that, among the 1,108 genes on the list, about 5% are expected to be false discoveries. It promises nothing about any individual gene --- a gene with an adjusted #emph[p]-value just under the threshold is not "95% likely to be real" --- and nothing at all about effect size or biological importance, which is why the fold change and the mean expression must be read alongside it. Recovering the knocked-down gene itself, without having been told to look for it, is a positive control on the whole pipeline rather than part of the statistical claim.

#strong[\41.] In a crystal structure the B-factor reports positional uncertainty or thermal motion for each atom. In an AlphaFold model the same column holds pLDDT, the per-residue confidence on a 0--100 scale, which is not a temperature factor and must not be interpreted as one.

#strong[\42.] In `REMARK 465`, not in the coordinate records. Code that parses only `ATOM` lines sees a continuous chain where there is a gap --- 72 residues of 1TUP are modelled nowhere, all at chain termini --- so distances computed across such a gap are meaningless and a sequence read from the coordinates is incomplete.

#strong[\43.] Take all atoms of standard residues in the protein chain and all atoms of standard residues in the DNA chains, compute the minimum inter-atomic distance from each protein residue to the DNA, and keep residues below a cut-off of about 4 Å, which is the range of hydrogen bonds and close van der Waals contacts. Exclude waters, ions and other heteroatoms from both sets: leaving ordered water molecules in the DNA set inflates the contact list with residues that touch solvent rather than nucleic acid.

#strong[\44.] The 0.78--1.03 Å range is a calibration: it is how much the same domain differs from itself within one crystal, so it is the noise floor against which any other RMSD must be read. The AlphaFold figure of 0.73 Å over 194 Cα establishes that the prediction of that domain is as close to the experiment as the experimental copies are to each other, which is as good as the comparison can resolve. It does not establish that the rest of the model is accurate, nor anything about the domain's orientation relative to the other parts of the protein.

#strong[\45.] pLDDT measures local confidence --- whether the backbone around each residue is placed correctly --- and predicted aligned error measures confidence in the relative position of one part of the model with respect to another. Two confident domains with high error between them support statements about each domain's internal geometry, such as the shape of a binding pocket, and support no statement about their arrangement, so inter-domain distances, overall shape and any docking that spans the two are unsupported.

#strong[\46.] The difference reflects the disordered regions tethering the two confident domains: 16.6 Å is what a compact 150--200 residue domain should give, while 33.5 Å describes a pair of domains drawn in one arbitrary arrangement on flexible linkers. Quoting it as a property of the protein implies a compactness the model does not assert --- the arrangement is exactly what the predicted aligned error says is not predicted --- and in solution the molecule samples an ensemble rather than this one conformation.

]
