== What is in the environment
<sec-env-contents>
#strong[Build toolchain] --- `quarto`, `jupyter`, `nbformat`, `nbclient`. Quarto executes the code in each chapter and assembles the book; the Jupyter packages are what it uses to run Python chunks.

#strong[Python analysis stack] --- `biopython` for sequence, structure and database work; `numpy` and `pandas` for arrays and tabular data; `scipy` and `statsmodels` for statistics; `matplotlib` and `seaborn` for figures; `scikit-learn` for machine learning; `networkx` for graphs; `pysam` for SAM/BAM/VCF access; `requests` for HTTP APIs.

#strong[R and Bioconductor] --- `r-base`, `r-knitr` and `r-rmarkdown` for execution; `r-tidyverse` for data manipulation and ggplot2; `r-igraph` for networks; `r-ape` for phylogenetics; `r-pheatmap`, `r-ggrepel` and `r-patchwork` for figures. From Bioconductor: `Biostrings` (sequences), `GenomicRanges` (intervals), `SummarizedExperiment` (assay containers), `rtracklayer` (genome annotation I/O), `DESeq2`, `edgeR` and `limma` (differential expression), and `VariantAnnotation` (VCF).

#strong[Command-line tools] --- `blast` (BLAST+), `mafft` (multiple alignment), `bwa` and `bowtie2` (read alignment), `samtools` and `bcftools` (alignment and variant files), `fastqc` and `multiqc` (quality control), `seqkit` (sequence manipulation), and `prodigal` (prokaryotic gene prediction).

== Checking the installation
<sec-env-check>
```
python -c "import Bio, pandas, numpy, sklearn, pysam; print('python stack ok')"
Rscript -e 'library(DESeq2); library(tidyverse); cat("r stack ok\n")'
blastn -version && mafft --version && samtools --version | head -1
python scripts/fetch_data.py --check
```

If all four commands succeed you can run every executable example in the book.

== Recording the environment in your own work
<sec-env-record>
An analysis is only reproducible if the software is pinned. Record the environment alongside your results, every time:

```
conda env export --no-builds > environment.yml   # portable across platforms
conda list --explicit > environment-lock.txt     # exact, platform-specific
```

In R, `sessionInfo()` (or `sessioninfo::session_info()`) belongs at the end of every analysis report; in Python, `pip freeze` or `conda list` serves the same purpose. Chapter 15 treats this as part of the deliverable rather than an afterthought.

== Containers
<sec-env-containers>
For work that must run identically on a cluster or in a reviewer's hands, a container is stronger than an environment file, because it pins the operating system as well. A minimal image for this book:

```
# Dockerfile
FROM condaforge/miniforge3:latest
COPY environment.yml /tmp/environment.yml
RUN conda env create -f /tmp/environment.yml && conda clean -afy
SHELL ["conda", "run", "-n", "pablb", "/bin/bash", "-c"]
```

The Bioconda and BioContainers projects publish per-tool images for most of the command-line software used here, which is usually easier than building your own.

